277cc677 [gemini-3.8-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.038 65.51KB 22.26s
277cc677 [gemini-3.8-flash] #The disconnection of COM6 during a trace dump is most likely caused by the hardware watchdog
277cc677 [gemini-3.8-flash] #timer expiring and resetting the RP2040.
277cc677 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc678 [gemini-3.8-flash] #Here is an analysis of why this occurs and other contributing factors:
277cc678 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc678 [gemini-3.8-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
277cc678 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc679 [gemini-3.8-flash] #1. Watchdog Starvation During Trace Transmission (Primary Cause)
277cc679 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• In `runShell()`, the hardware watchdog is enabled with a 1200&nbsp;ms timeout:
277cc679 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc679 [gemini-3.8-flash] #  watchdog_enable(WatchdogTimeout, false); // WatchdogTimeout = 1200 ms
277cc679 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc67a [gemini-3.8-flash] #• In `sampleRom()`, the function captures the trace and then iterates through `pinBuffer` to
277cc67a [gemini-3.8-flash] #transmit every sample:
277cc67a [gemini-3.8-flash] #  * Over 500 samples are transmitted (`id: 200001` through `id: 200513`).
277cc67a [gemini-3.8-flash] #  * Each iteration performs string formatting, invokes `rpcSend()`, calls `cdcFlush()`, and calls
277cc67b [gemini-3.8-flash] #`tud_task()`.
277cc67b [gemini-3.8-flash] #  * Over USB Full-Speed CDC (1&nbsp;ms frame intervals), sending 500+ separate JSON-RPC packets
277cc67b [gemini-3.8-flash] #with individual flushes typically takes between 1.0 and 1.5 seconds.
277cc67b [gemini-3.8-flash] #• `watchdog_update()` is not called anywhere inside `sampleRom()` (neither in the capture
277cc67c [gemini-3.8-flash] #loop nor in the transmission loop). Once the cumulative time exceeds 1200&nbsp;ms, the watchdog
277cc67c [gemini-3.8-flash] #resets the processor. The USB connection drops immediately, which Windows reports as COM6
277cc67c [gemini-3.8-flash] #disconnecting.
277cc67c [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc67d [gemini-3.8-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
277cc67d [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc67d [gemini-3.8-flash] #2. USB Task Starvation During the Capture Loop
277cc67d [gemini-3.8-flash] #• While waiting for bus state changes inside the capture loop (`while ((pins & enable) == 0)`),
277cc67e [gemini-3.8-flash] #`sleep_us(100)` is called without invoking `tud_task()`.
277cc67e [gemini-3.8-flash] #• If a burst capture runs long enough without servicing TinyUSB, the host USB stack may register an
277cc67e [gemini-3.8-flash] #endpoint timeout or transaction failure before transmission even completes.
277cc67f [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc67f [gemini-3.8-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
277cc67f [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc67f [gemini-3.8-flash] #3. Per-Line CDC Flush Overhead
277cc680 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Calling `cdcFlush()` on every single sample forces the USB driver to try to push individual
277cc680 [gemini-3.8-flash] #packets rather than batching CDC data into full 64-byte USB packets.
277cc680 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• This drastically reduces throughput, prolongs the time spent inside the transmit loop, and
277cc680 [gemini-3.8-flash] #increases the likelihood of tripping the watchdog timer.
277cc681 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc681 [gemini-3.8-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
277cc681 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc681 [gemini-3.8-flash] #4. Dynamic Vector Allocation
277cc682 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• `pinBuffer.push_back(...)` is called dynamically without `pinBuffer.reserve()`. Repeated heap
277cc682 [gemini-3.8-flash] #reallocations on an embedded micro-controller can cause execution jitter and memory fragmentation,
277cc682 [gemini-3.8-flash] #although the watchdog reset during transmission is the immediate failure mechanism here.
277cc683 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc683 [gemini-3.8-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
277cc683 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc683 [gemini-3.8-flash] #Suggested Resolutions
277cc684 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc684 [gemini-3.8-flash] #1. Feed the Watchdog During Dump:
277cc684 [gemini-3.8-flash] #   Add a call to `watchdog_update()` inside the sample transmission loop in `sampleRom()` (and
277cc684 [gemini-3.8-flash] #optionally inside the sampling wait loop if sample windows can exceed a few hundred milliseconds).
277cc684 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc685 [gemini-3.8-flash] #2. Batch Transmissions:
277cc685 [gemini-3.8-flash] #   Rather than calling `cdcFlush()` after every single line, flush only when the internal CDC
277cc685 [gemini-3.8-flash] #buffer is full or after the entire buffer of samples has been queued to TinyUSB.
277cc685 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc686 [gemini-3.8-flash] #3. Pre-allocate the Trace Buffer:
277cc686 [gemini-3.8-flash] #   Call `pinBuffer.reserve(2048);` during initialization so heap allocation does not take place
277cc686 [gemini-3.8-flash] #during high-speed sampling.
277cc687 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
277cc687 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• (If you would like the revised implementation of `sampleRom` or helper routines, please let me
277cc687 [gemini-3.8-flash] #know and I will be glad to provide them.)*
277cc687 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
