278a6373 [gemini-3.8-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.026 66.00KB 7.27s
278a6374 [gemini-3.8-flash] #A few interesting observations from the source, trace log, and the PIO comment:
278a6374 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
278a6374 [gemini-3.8-flash] #1. Bus Activity & Target System
278a6374 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• 6502 Execution Trace: The trace clearly shows an active 6502 cycle. We can see a tight
278a6375 [gemini-3.8-flash] #loop executing around `EAAE`–`EAB2` (`E8` for `INX`, `D0 FA` for `BNE`, followed by zero-page
278a6375 [gemini-3.8-flash] #stores and table-driven reads/writes).
278a6375 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Software Polling Artifacts: In `sampleRom()`, the Pico is currently polling
278a6375 [gemini-3.8-flash] #`gpio_get_all()` with a 100 µs sleep (`sleep_us(100)`). On a 1 MHz or 2 MHz 6502 system, individual
278a6376 [gemini-3.8-flash] #machine cycles are 500 ns to 1 µs long. Consequently, the trace is undersampling—catching
278a6376 [gemini-3.8-flash] #occasional bus cycles rather than a contiguous cycle-by-cycle trace.
278a6376 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Typo in Client/Echo: Notice the client or log parser prints `"Addresss Data CS RW T"`
278a6376 [gemini-3.8-flash] #with three 's's, whereas the firmware transmits `"A D CS RW T"`.
278a6377 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
278a6377 [gemini-3.8-flash] #2. Transition to `snoop.pio`
278a6377 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Shifting sampling from the CPU polling loop to the RP2040 PIO state machine is a logical step.
278a6377 [gemini-3.8-flash] #Software polling is too jittery and prone to missing edges on a bus clocked above a few tens of
278a6378 [gemini-3.8-flash] #kilohertz.
278a6378 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• A common challenge with a PIO-based 32-bit GPIO snoop alongside timestamps is FIFO bandwidth and
278a6378 [gemini-3.8-flash] #instruction budget:
278a6378 [gemini-3.8-flash] #  * Pushing both 32-bit GPIO data and a 32-bit timestamp per transaction requires either
278a6379 [gemini-3.8-flash] #interleaving two pushes per sampled edge, chaining a timer state machine via IRQs, or running PIO
278a6379 [gemini-3.8-flash] #into DMA to write directly into an SRAM ring buffer.
278a6379 [gemini-3.8-flash] #  * If Phi2 (the 6502 system clock) is available on a pin, sampling on the falling/rising edge of
278a6379 [gemini-3.8-flash] #Phi2 provides clean, synchronized bus data without needing time-based delta guessing.
278a637a [gemini-3.8-flash] #
278a637a [gemini-3.8-flash] #Whenever you would like to look at the PIO implementation or DMA ring-buffering strategies, just
278a637a [gemini-3.8-flash] #let me know.
278a637a [gemini-3.8-flash] #
