278a65ec [gemini-3.8-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.023 67.97KB 5.44s
278a65ec [gemini-3.8-flash] #At 1 MHz, each 6502 bus cycle is precisely 1 µs (1000 ns), split roughly into:
278a65ec [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Phi1 (Low, ~500 ns): The 6502 calculates the next address; address lines and R/W settle.
278a65ed [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Phi2 (High, ~500 ns): Memory/devices decode the address; data is either placed onto the
278a65ed [gemini-3.8-flash] #bus (read) or driven by the 6502 (write), and is latched on the falling edge of Phi2.
278a65ed [gemini-3.8-flash] #
278a65ed [gemini-3.8-flash] #Looking at your current log, you can see why the trace skips around:
278a65ee [gemini-3.8-flash] #• The firmware sleeps `sleep_us(100)` between samples, meaning roughly 100 entire 6502
278a65ee [gemini-3.8-flash] #instructions/cycles happen between each captured sample.
278a65ee [gemini-3.8-flash] #• When sampling asynchronously without latching to Phi2, you risk capturing the bus while
278a65ee [gemini-3.8-flash] #address/data lines are in mid-transition, which accounts for those partial address/data pairs.
278a65ef [gemini-3.8-flash] #
278a65ef [gemini-3.8-flash] #The PIO Advantage
278a65ef [gemini-3.8-flash] #Moving to the RP2040 PIO changes the game completely:
278a65ef [gemini-3.8-flash] #
278a65f0 [gemini-3.8-flash] #1. Clock-Synchronous Capture:
278a65f0 [gemini-3.8-flash] #   If you feed Phi2 into an unused GPIO pin, the PIO state machine can execute `wait 1 pin
278a65f0 [gemini-3.8-flash] #<phi2_pin>` followed by `wait 0 pin <phi2_pin>` (or sample right near the end of Phi2 high when
278a65f0 [gemini-3.8-flash] #data setup times are met). Every single cycle is captured without missing a beat.
278a65f1 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
278a65f1 [gemini-3.8-flash] #2. Throughput at 1 MHz:
278a65f1 [gemini-3.8-flash] #   * 1 MHz continuous sampling means generating 4 MB/s (GPIO only) or 8 MB/s (GPIO + 32-bit
278a65f1 [gemini-3.8-flash] #timestamp).
278a65f2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #   * RP2040 DMA easily handles up to ~133 MB/s internal SRAM transfers, so streaming 4–8 MB/s into
278a65f2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #a circular RAM buffer in background hardware requires near-zero CPU overhead.
278a65f2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #   * However, CDC USB transfer rates cap out around 1.0 to 1.2 MB/s on Full-Speed USB (Pico 1's
278a65f2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #native USB hardware).
278a65f3 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
278a65f3 [gemini-3.8-flash] #3. Managing the USB Bottleneck:
278a65f3 [gemini-3.8-flash] #   Because 1 MHz produces data faster than USB FS can stream it:
278a65f3 [gemini-3.8-flash] #   * Triggered Bursts: Fill a dedicated circular SRAM buffer (e.g., 64 KB–128 KB, holding
278a65f4 [gemini-3.8-flash] #tens of thousands of cycles) upon a bus trigger (like an address hit, IRQ, or /CE asserted), then
278a65f4 [gemini-3.8-flash] #stop and dump to USB (similar to what `sampleRom` aims to do).
278a65f4 [gemini-3.8-flash] #   * Bus Event Filtering in PIO: Only `push` to FIFO when certain conditions match (e.g.,
278a65f4 [gemini-3.8-flash] #when `/CS` or `/CE` is low, or only on `R/W == 0` writes). This immediately cuts traffic down to a
278a65f5 [gemini-3.8-flash] #fraction of the 1 MHz clock, allowing continuous streaming over USB without overflowing the buffer.
278a65f5 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
278a65f5 [gemini-3.8-flash] #If you have Phi2 routed to a GPIO, or if you are qualifying solely off `/CE` / `/CS`, we can align
278a65f5 [gemini-3.8-flash] #the PIO design around that trigger strategy.
278a65f6 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
