278a80f8 [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.251 65.97KB 388.09s
278a80f8 [kimi-k3] #New since last time: `trace1.txt` (first real capture) and `snoop.pio` (a stub — clearly the intent
278a80f9 [kimi-k3] #to move sampling off the CPU and into PIO). Comments on both, plus what the trace reveals about the
278a80f9 [kimi-k3] #current firmware.
278a80f9 [kimi-k3] #
278a80f9 [kimi-k3] #What the trace shows
278a80fa [kimi-k3] #
278a80fa [kimi-k3] #- Every delta in the T column is a multiple of 0x64 (100 µs). That's the `sleep_us(100)` in
278a80fa [kimi-k3] #`sampleRom`'s change-wait. Likely cause: A13–A15 (GPIO 0–2) float, as your own `mask=0x02000007`
278a80fa [kimi-k3] #comment notes, but `sampleRom`'s inner loop compares full raw `pins` values. The floating lines
278a80fb [kimi-k3] #make `pins != oldPins` on nearly every poll, so you're capturing at a fixed 10 kHz cadence, not on
278a80fb [kimi-k3] #real bus activity. Apply the same float mask in `sampleRom`'s compare and it becomes genuinely
278a80fb [kimi-k3] #change-driven.
278a80fb [kimi-k3] #- CS is '1' on every single line. The EAxx reads and zero-page writes are the 6502 running
278a80fc [kimi-k3] #its monitor from some other device; UB3's /CE never asserts during the entire capture. Either the
278a80fc [kimi-k3] #traced code never touches that ROM, or CE isn't actually reaching GPIO26 (no pulls are set in
278a80fc [kimi-k3] #`initRomulus`, so an unwired pin floats — which would also explain spurious entry into the CE-gated
278a80fc [kimi-k3] #path).
278a80fd [kimi-k3] #- The `rtc.set` failure (`-32501`) matches the stubbed `setTime`. Also note `vidbit.info` (id 1)
278a80fd [kimi-k3] #got no reply — `updateRPC` has no branch for it and no method-not-found fallback.
278a80fd [kimi-k3] #
278a80fe [kimi-k3] #romulon.cpp issues, rough severity order
278a80fe [kimi-k3] #
278a80fe [kimi-k3] #1. `char buffer[20]={32};` in `pinStamp` sets only the first byte to space; the rest are zero.
278a80fe [kimi-k3] #Index 4 is never overwritten, so every emitted JSON string contains a raw NUL — technically invalid
278a80ff [kimi-k3] #JSON. Your host is being lenient. If you want a space-filled canvas, `memset` it.
278a80ff [kimi-k3] #2. `pinBuffer.reserve(204848)` is 512 KB of samples. That exceeds RP2040 SRAM outright, and
278a80ff [kimi-k3] #even on RP2350's 520 KB it leaves almost nothing for stack/heap/USB. Size the buffer to a measured
278a80ff [kimi-k3] #budget.
278a8100 [kimi-k3] #3. `sampleRom(0)` (the `connected && first` path) can never exit via the CE condition — `(pins & 0)
278a8100 [kimi-k3] #== 0` is always true — so it runs until the size break, i.e. memory exhaustion. Meanwhile the
278a8100 [kimi-k3] #watchdog is armed at 1200 ms and never fed during capture, so a long burst resets the chip
278a8100 [kimi-k3] #mid-stream. The trace ending abruptly at id 200513 is consistent with one of these.
278a8101 [kimi-k3] #4. T is truncated to 16 bits (`hexout(buffer+13,t,4)`) — wraps every ~65 ms. There's room in the
278a8101 [kimi-k3] #buffer for 8 digits if you want absolute time.
278a8101 [kimi-k3] #5. Smaller items: `int id=2e5+...` uses a double literal; `count` in `runShell` is unused;
278a8101 [kimi-k3] #`updateRPC` dereferences `payload` without checking `status`; `emitBuffer()`, `startup()`, and the
278a8102 [kimi-k3] #NVM block are dead weight.
278a8102 [kimi-k3] #
278a8102 [kimi-k3] #snoop.pio direction
278a8102 [kimi-k3] #
278a8103 [kimi-k3] #The plan in the header comment is sound. Points worth deciding before you write it:
278a8103 [kimi-k3] #
278a8103 [kimi-k3] #- `in pins, 32` with in-base 0 reproduces `gpio_get_all()`'s exact bit layout, so `pinStamp` works
278a8103 [kimi-k3] #unchanged.
278a8104 [kimi-k3] #- PIO has no microsecond timer, so the "32 bit timestamp" has to be synthesized: the usual trick is
278a8104 [kimi-k3] #a decrementing X register pushed alongside each sample (`in x, 32` after `in pins, 32`, with
278a8104 [kimi-k3] #autopush at 32 bits), giving cycle-tick stamps you convert on the ARM side. That loop is 3
278a8104 [kimi-k3] #cycles/sample — ~42 MS/s at 125 MHz — which no USB link streams live, so you're back to
278a8105 [kimi-k3] #burst-capture into RAM, and the RAM budget question from point 2 above comes with you. A clock
278a8105 [kimi-k3] #divider on the SM, or gating the loop on CE with a `wait`, makes the buffer last much longer.
278a8105 [kimi-k3] #- `.side_set 1` is currently unused; drop it unless you want a scope-trigger pin toggling during
278a8105 [kimi-k3] #capture (genuinely useful for bring-up).
278a8106 [kimi-k3] #
278a8106 [kimi-k3] #Happy to draft the full PIO program plus the DMA/handoff side when you're ready.
278a8106 [kimi-k3] #
278b3cb0 [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.135 70.26KB 117.76s
278b3cb0 [kimi-k3] #Good shape — this is close, but it won't assemble as-is, and there are two runtime traps once it
278b3cb0 [kimi-k3] #does.
278b3cb0 [kimi-k3] #
278b3cb1 [kimi-k3] #As written
278b3cb1 [kimi-k3] #
278b3cb1 [kimi-k3] #1. `jmp x--, loop` has no `loop` label — pioasm will reject it. Add the label at the wrap
278b3cb1 [kimi-k3] #target.
278b3cb2 [kimi-k3] #2. X is never initialised. On SM enable it holds whatever the register last had. Seed it
278b3cb2 [kimi-k3] #once before the wrap target: `mov x, ~null` gives 0xFFFFFFFF and then counts down forever. Note the
278b3cb2 [kimi-k3] #placement matters — anything before `.wrap_target` runs exactly once at start, which is what you
278b3cb3 [kimi-k3] #want.
278b3cb3 [kimi-k3] #
278b3cb3 [kimi-k3] #Minimal corrected version:
278b3cb3 [kimi-k3] #
278b3cb3 [kimi-k3] #
278b3cb4 [kimi-k3] #; snoop.pio
278b3cb4 [kimi-k3] #; transmit 32 bit gpio with 32 bit timestamp
278b3cb4 [kimi-k3] #
278b3cb4 [kimi-k3] #.program snoop
278b3cb5 [kimi-k3] #
278b3cb5 [kimi-k3] #	mov	x, ~null		; once: timestamp starts at max
278b3cb5 [kimi-k3] #.wrap_target
278b3cb5 [kimi-k3] #loop:
278b3cb6 [kimi-k3] #	in	pins, 32
278b3cb6 [kimi-k3] #	in	x, 32
278b3cb6 [kimi-k3] #	jmp	x--, loop
278b3cb6 [kimi-k3] #.wrap
278b3cb7 [kimi-k3] #
278b3cb7 [kimi-k3] #
278b3cb7 [kimi-k3] #The countdown wraps seamlessly: when X hits 0 the jump is not taken, X decrements to 0xFFFFFFFF,
278b3cb7 [kimi-k3] #and `.wrap` sends execution back to the top anyway. Timestamps are the pre-decrement value, pushed
278b3cb8 [kimi-k3] #after the pins word, so the host reads (pins, tick) pairs. I dropped the `side_set` since nothing
278b3cb8 [kimi-k3] #drives it — put it back only if you want a scope marker.
278b3cb8 [kimi-k3] #
278b3cb8 [kimi-k3] #Traps to know about before wiring it up
278b3cb9 [kimi-k3] #
278b3cb9 [kimi-k3] #- X is a loop-iteration counter, not a timer. Right now every iteration is exactly 3
278b3cb9 [kimi-k3] #cycles, so tick delta × 3 = cycle delta, and the host can convert using the SM clock. The moment
278b3cb9 [kimi-k3] #you add CE gating or any branching, keep every path through the loop the same length (pad with `mov
278b3cba [kimi-k3] #y, y`) or the timeline silently distorts.
278b3cba [kimi-k3] #- Rate. 3 cycles/sample at 125 MHz is ~41.7 MS/s, i.e. ~333 MB/s into the RX FIFO. Even
278b3cba [kimi-k3] #with FIFO join (8 words = 4 samples) that's ~192 ns of slack, so DMA must be armed and sustained,
