278b90de [glm-5.3-flash] #Three gotchas, in order of how silently they fail:
278b90de [glm-5.3-flash] #
278b90df [glm-5.3-flash] #1. The declaration must be in scope where you define it. The prototype lives in
278b90df [glm-5.3-flash] #`class/cdc/cdc_device.h`. Your file already calls `tud_task()`, so the device API is evidently
278b90df [glm-5.3-flash] #visible through `picosdk.h` — if `tud_cdc_n_connected(0)` would compile there, the callback
278b90df [glm-5.3-flash] #declaration is too. If it isn't, include the header; without it, C++ name mangling produces a
278b90df [glm-5.3-flash] #different symbol and your definition quietly overrides nothing — no error, callback just
278b90e0 [glm-5.3-flash] #never fires.
278b90e0 [glm-5.3-flash] #2. Duplicate definition. If your cdc wrapper (or pico's stdio_usb, if you build with
278b90e0 [glm-5.3-flash] #`pico_enable_stdio_usb`) already defines the callback, you get a duplicate-symbol link error. In
278b90e0 [glm-5.3-flash] #that case don't redefine — route through the existing one. This is also the signal to prefer Option
278b90e1 [glm-5.3-flash] #A.
278b90e1 [glm-5.3-flash] #3. It only fires on change. DTR is low at boot; it asserts when rpccoms opens COM6
278b90e1 [glm-5.3-flash] #(CreateFile asserts DTR by default). So `hostOpen` starts false and flips on connect. If it never
278b90e1 [glm-5.3-flash] #flips in testing, your host tool isn't asserting DTR — keep the `vidbit.set` handshake and OR the
