278bcf50 [glm-5.3-flash] #cdcReceiveCallback: three problems, one restructure
278bcf50 [glm-5.3-flash] #
278bcf51 [glm-5.3-flash] #1. Context. With pico_stdio_usb linked, the chars-available callback fires from the SDK's
278bcf51 [glm-5.3-flash] #USB service path — i.e. interrupt context. Your spin lock is the right primitive for that, but
278bcf51 [glm-5.3-flash] #`lines.push_back(std::string(...))` mallocs inside an IRQ, which pico's allocator doesn't guarantee
278bcf51 [glm-5.3-flash] #against main-loop malloc interruption.
278bcf52 [glm-5.3-flash] #2. Re-fire gap. The loop caps at 4096 chars, but the callback only fires when chars
278bcf52 [glm-5.3-flash] #become available. If a burst exceeds the cap, the remainder sits unread and no new callback
278bcf52 [glm-5.3-flash] #comes until the next byte arrives — a stall that looks like a lost line.
278bcf52 [glm-5.3-flash] #3. Buffer-full re-entry. `cdcCharCount==4096` breaks, but nothing resets it; the next
278bcf53 [glm-5.3-flash] #callback executes `cdcBuffer[cdcCharCount++]` and writes past the array.
278bcf53 [glm-5.3-flash] #
278bcf53 [glm-5.3-flash] #All three disappear with the same move — callback as flag, drain in the main loop where malloc is
278bcf54 [glm-5.3-flash] #safe:
