278bfdea [glm-5.3-flash] #`bool` — true on success, false on error. The whole AON timer API (`aon_timer_start`,
278bfdeb [glm-5.3-flash] #`aon_timer_set_time`, `aon_timer_get_time`, `aon_timer_stop`) follows the same convention.
278bfdeb [glm-5.3-flash] #
278bfdeb [glm-5.3-flash] #For your code that matters twice over:
278bfdec [glm-5.3-flash] #
278bfdec [glm-5.3-flash] #• 1. The return value is discarded in picosdk.cpp, and the branch returns `void`:*
278bfdec [glm-5.3-flash] #
278bfdec [glm-5.3-flash] #
278bfded [glm-5.3-flash] #void setTime(int64_t seconds){
278bfded [glm-5.3-flash] #	...
278bfded [glm-5.3-flash] #	aon_timer_start(&ts);
278bfded [glm-5.3-flash] #}
278bfded [glm-5.3-flash] #
278bfdee [glm-5.3-flash] #
278bfdee [glm-5.3-flash] #Propagating it fixes both that and the signature mismatch with the RP2040 branch:
278bfdee [glm-5.3-flash] #
278bfdee [glm-5.3-flash] #
278bfdee [glm-5.3-flash] #bool setTime(int64_t seconds){
278bfdef [glm-5.3-flash] #	timespec ts;
278bfdef [glm-5.3-flash] #	ts.tv_sec=(time_t)seconds;
278bfdef [glm-5.3-flash] #	ts.tv_nsec=0;
278bfdef [glm-5.3-flash] #	return aon_timer_start(&ts);
278bfdf0 [glm-5.3-flash] #}
278bfdf0 [glm-5.3-flash] #
278bfdf0 [glm-5.3-flash] #
278bfdf0 [glm-5.3-flash] #Now romulon's `rtc.set` handler gets a meaningful `success` to report, and both ifdef branches
278bfdf1 [glm-5.3-flash] #agree on the type. Note the cast — `tv_sec` is 32-bit on this toolchain, so int64 seconds truncate
278bfdf1 [glm-5.3-flash] #there; fine until 2038, but worth knowing the limit exists.
278bfdf1 [glm-5.3-flash] #
278bfdf1 [glm-5.3-flash] #• 2. It gates `wallTime()` on RP2350.* The AON timer starts from zero at power-on (nothing
278bfdf2 [glm-5.3-flash] #persists without a battery), and `aon_timer_get_time` returns false until the timer has been
278bfdf2 [glm-5.3-flash] #started — so your `wallTime()` returns `""` from boot until the host sends `rtc.set`. With
278bfdf2 [glm-5.3-flash] #`setTime` currently stubbed at the call site (`bool success=false;//setTime(t)`), that's the actual
278bfdf2 [glm-5.3-flash] #chain behind the `-32501` in trace1, and it'll also be why any wall-time stamping stays empty.
278bfdf3 [glm-5.3-flash] #
278bfdf3 [glm-5.3-flash] #Failure semantics in practice: `start` on RP2350 can only really fail if the always-on clock domain
278bfdf3 [glm-5.3-flash] #can't be enabled — not something you'll hit at runtime. The false returns you'll actually encounter
278bfdf3 [glm-5.3-flash] #are `get_time`/`stop` on a timer that was never started. So treat the bool as plumbing, not as an
278bfdf4 [glm-5.3-flash] #error path needing recovery.
278bfdf4 [glm-5.3-flash] #
