278c4d9f [grok-build-0.1] #• Code Review & Observations*
278c4da0 [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4da0 [grok-build-0.1] #Thanks for sharing the files. Here's a direct analysis of the current state of the project:
278c4da0 [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4da1 [grok-build-0.1] #Major Issues
278c4da1 [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4da1 [grok-build-0.1] #• 1. Sampling is far too slow (critical)*
278c4da1 [grok-build-0.1] #The current `runShell()` + `sampleRom()` approach uses `gpio_get_all()` in a software loop with
278c4da2 [grok-build-0.1] #`sleep_us(100)`. This is completely unsuitable for 6502 bus snooping.
278c4da2 [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4da2 [grok-build-0.1] #- At 1 MHz, a 6502 has a ~1µs cycle. You're sampling at ~10 kHz at best.
278c4da2 [grok-build-0.1] #- The PIO program (`snoop.pio`) exists but is not being used. This is the correct tool for the job.
278c4da3 [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4da3 [grok-build-0.1] #• 2. Address reconstruction bugs*
278c4da3 [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4da3 [grok-build-0.1] #int a07 = reverse8((pins >> 5) & 0xff);
278c4da4 [grok-build-0.1] #int a89 = (pins >> 3) & 0x03;
278c4da4 [grok-build-0.1] #int a1315 = pins & 7;
278c4da4 [grok-build-0.1] #int a = a07 | (a89 << 8) | (a10 << 10) | ...;
278c4da4 [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4da5 [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4da5 [grok-build-0.1] #- `reverse8` is being applied to A0-A7, but the bits coming from the GPIO are already in the
278c4da5 [grok-build-0.1] #correct order from the physical wiring. You probably don't want to reverse them here.
278c4da5 [grok-build-0.1] #- `a89` is only 2 bits but you're shifting it to bits 8-9. This is correct in theory, but verify
278c4da6 [grok-build-0.1] #your actual GPIO wiring against the comment block.
278c4da6 [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4da6 [grok-build-0.1] #• 3. `pinStamp()` has formatting issues*
278c4da6 [grok-build-0.1] #- It calls `hexout()` but ignores the return value.
278c4da7 [grok-build-0.1] #- The manual buffer construction is fragile.
278c4da7 [grok-build-0.1] #- The label in the log says `"Addresss Data CS RW T"` (double s).
278c4da7 [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4da7 [grok-build-0.1] #• 4. CS handling is inverted/confusing*
278c4da8 [grok-build-0.1] #You have:
278c4da8 [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4da8 [grok-build-0.1] #const uint32_t UB3_CE = 0x04000000;  // GPIO 26
278c4da8 [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4da9 [grok-build-0.1] #Then checks like `if((pins & UB3_CE) == 0)` (active low, correct), but later you pass `0` to
278c4da9 [grok-build-0.1] #`sampleRom(0)` which would never trigger the exit condition.
278c4da9 [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4da9 [grok-build-0.1] #• 5. PIO is not integrated*
278c4daa [grok-build-0.1] #`initSnoop()` and `pollSnoop()` are empty. The PIO program itself looks reasonable for change
278c4daa [grok-build-0.1] #detection, but it needs proper setup and DMA or direct ISR handling to be useful.
278c4daa [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4daa [grok-build-0.1] #Trace Analysis (trace1.txt)
278c4dab [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4dab [grok-build-0.1] #The trace shows two distinct phases:
278c4dab [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4dab [grok-build-0.1] #1. Early noise / bus floating (first ~28 lines)
278c4dac [grok-build-0.1] #   - Lots of `0000 ff W`, `4c4b 4c R`, etc. This looks like the 6502 is not yet running or lines
278c4dac [grok-build-0.1] #are floating.
278c4dac [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4dac [grok-build-0.1] #2. Real activity starting around `eaae 00 R`
278c4dad [grok-build-0.1] #   - This looks like 6502 reset vector handling or cartridge startup code.
278c4dad [grok-build-0.1] #   - There's a lot of `eab7 f6` / `eab8 00` pairs repeating — this pattern is very consistent.
278c4dad [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4dae [grok-build-0.1] #The repeating pattern of:
278c4dae [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4dae [grok-build-0.1] #eab7 f6 R
278c4dae [grok-build-0.1] #eab8 00 R
278c4daf [grok-build-0.1] #0000 xx R/W
278c4daf [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4daf [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4daf [grok-build-0.1] #...suggests either:
278c4db0 [grok-build-0.1] #- The 6502 is executing from a specific region repeatedly, or
278c4db0 [grok-build-0.1] #- Your address reconstruction is off by a consistent amount in the upper bits.
278c4db0 [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4db0 [grok-build-0.1] #Recommendations (Priority Order)
278c4db1 [grok-build-0.1] #
278c4db1 [grok-build-0.1] #╭────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
