278e70e0 [grok-build-0.1] #• Explanation:*
278e70e0 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e0 [grok-build-0.1] #What `transfer_count` actually means
278e70e1 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e1 [grok-build-0.1] #On the RP2040, when you start a DMA transfer with:
278e70e1 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e1 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e2 [grok-build-0.1] #dma_channel_configure(..., transfer_count, true);
278e70e2 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e2 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e2 [grok-build-0.1] #the hardware loads the value you passed into the `transfer_count` register.
278e70e3 [grok-build-0.1] #• This register counts downwards*, not upwards.
278e70e3 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e3 [grok-build-0.1] #- It starts at the number of transfers you requested.
278e70e3 [grok-build-0.1] #- Every time the DMA successfully moves one item (in your case, one 32-bit word), it decrements
278e70e4 [grok-build-0.1] #`transfer_count` by 1.
278e70e4 [grok-build-0.1] #- When the transfer finishes, `transfer_count` reaches 0.
278e70e4 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e4 [grok-build-0.1] #In your specific case
278e70e5 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e5 [grok-build-0.1] #You are doing this:
278e70e5 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e6 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e6 [grok-build-0.1] #dma_channel_configure(..., max_samples * 2, true);
278e70e6 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e6 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e7 [grok-build-0.1] #So you are requesting `max_samples * 2` transfers (because each captured bus event produces two
278e70e7 [grok-build-0.1] #words: `tick` + `pins`).
278e70e7 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e7 [grok-build-0.1] #This means:
278e70e8 [grok-build-0.1] #- At the start: `transfer_count = 8192` (for MAX_SAMPLES=4096)
278e70e8 [grok-build-0.1] #- While running: it slowly counts down
278e70e8 [grok-build-0.1] #- When finished: `transfer_count = 0`
278e70e8 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e9 [grok-build-0.1] #The function you showed
278e70e9 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e9 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70e9 [grok-build-0.1] #size_t snoopCount() {
278e70ea [grok-build-0.1] #    if (snoop_dma_chan < 0) return 0;
278e70ea [grok-build-0.1] #    return dma_channel_hw_addr(snoop_dma_chan)->transfer_count / 2;
278e70ea [grok-build-0.1] #}
278e70ea [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70eb [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70eb [grok-build-0.1] #The comment `// transfer_count words` is just a reminder that the value is measured in 32-bit
278e70eb [grok-build-0.1] #words (not bytes, not samples).
278e70eb [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70eb [grok-build-0.1] #Dividing by 2 is an attempt to convert back from "words" to "samples" (since 2 words = 1 sample
278e70ec [grok-build-0.1] #pair).
278e70ec [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70ec [grok-build-0.1] #The problem
278e70ec [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70ed [grok-build-0.1] #Because `transfer_count` counts down, this function returns:
278e70ed [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70ed [grok-build-0.1] #- While the DMA is still running → the number of remaining samples (not useful)
278e70ed [grok-build-0.1] #- After the DMA has finished → 0
278e70ee [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70ee [grok-build-0.1] #So once your capture completes (which your code waits for with `while (!isBusCaptureComplete())`),
278e70ee [grok-build-0.1] #calling this function will almost always return 0.
278e70ee [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70ef [grok-build-0.1] #This is why you're likely seeing either no output or very few lines from the sampler.
278e70ef [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70ef [grok-build-0.1] #Summary
278e70ef [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70f0 [grok-build-0.1] #╭───────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────╮
278e70f0 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Moment                   │  `transfer_count` value  │  `transfer_count / 2` means            │
278e70f0 [grok-build-0.1] #├───────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
278e70f0 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Just after starting      │  High (e.g. 8192)        │  How many sample-pairs are still left  │
278e70f1 [grok-build-0.1] #│  During capture           │  Decreasing              │  Remaining work                        │
278e70f1 [grok-build-0.1] #│  After DMA finishes       │  0                       │  0 (nothing left to do)                │
278e70f1 [grok-build-0.1] #╰───────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────╯
278e70f1 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e70f2 [grok-build-0.1] #This is the core reason the current `getCapturedSampleCount()` doesn't tell you how many samples
278e70f2 [grok-build-0.1] #were actually captured.
278e70f2 [grok-build-0.1] #
