278e8b0a [grok-build-0.1] #Why it now works:
278e8b0b [grok-build-0.1] #
278e8b0b [grok-build-0.1] #- `snoopRequested` stores the original number of samples you asked for.
278e8b0b [grok-build-0.1] #- The DMA is configured for `max_samples * 2` words (because each sample is two 32-bit
278e8b0b [grok-build-0.1] #words: tick + pins).
278e8b0c [grok-build-0.1] #- `transfer_count` starts at that word count and counts down as data is moved.
278e8b0c [grok-build-0.1] #- Therefore:
278e8b0c [grok-build-0.1] #
278e8b0d [grok-build-0.1] #  `words_transmitted = (max_samples * 2) - current_transfer_count`
278e8b0d [grok-build-0.1] #  `samples_transmitted = words_transmitted / 2`
278e8b0d [grok-build-0.1] #
278e8b0d [grok-build-0.1] #  Which simplifies to exactly what you wrote:
278e8b0d [grok-build-0.1] #
278e8b0e [grok-build-0.1] #
278e8b0e [grok-build-0.1] #  return snoopRequested - (transfer_count / 2);
278e8b0e [grok-build-0.1] #
278e8b0e [grok-build-0.1] #
278e8b0e [grok-build-0.1] #This gives you the number of complete sample pairs that have actually been written into the buffer
278e8b0f [grok-build-0.1] #so far.
278e8b0f [grok-build-0.1] #
278e8b0f [grok-build-0.1] #When this value is useful:
278e8b0f [grok-build-0.1] #
278e8b10 [grok-build-0.1] #- During capture: it tells you real-time progress.
278e8b10 [grok-build-0.1] #- After `isBusCaptureComplete()` returns true: it will equal the number of samples that
278e8b10 [grok-build-0.1] #were actually delivered (could be less than requested if the PIO stopped pushing early).
278e8b11 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e8b11 [grok-build-0.1] #Minor observations on the current code:
278e8b11 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e8b11 [grok-build-0.1] #- The variable name `snoopRequested` is clearer than the previous `snoopCount`.
278e8b12 [grok-build-0.1] #- The logic is sound as long as you always call `beginSnoop()` before reading the count.
278e8b12 [grok-build-0.1] #- Division by 2 is safe here because you always request an even number of words.
278e8b12 [grok-build-0.1] #
278e8b12 [grok-build-0.1] #This part is now doing what you wanted.
