278fc206 [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc206 [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc207 [grok-build-0.1] #How "push pins" works here:
278fc207 [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc207 [grok-build-0.1] #`push` can only send whatever is currently in the `ISR` register.
278fc207 [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc208 [grok-build-0.1] #So the sequence is:
278fc208 [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc208 [grok-build-0.1] #1. Save the tick (`isr`) into `x` so we don't lose it.
278fc208 [grok-build-0.1] #2. `push` → sends the tick.
278fc209 [grok-build-0.1] #3. Move the pins (`y`) into `ISR`.
278fc209 [grok-build-0.1] #4. `push` → now sends the pins.
278fc209 [grok-build-0.1] #5. Put the saved tick back into `ISR` so the normal decrement logic in the main loop can continue.
278fc209 [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc20a [grok-build-0.1] #This is the standard pattern when you need to push something other than the current `ISR` value
278fc20a [grok-build-0.1] #without destroying your running counter.
278fc20a [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc20a [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
278fc20b [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc20b [grok-build-0.1] #Observations from trace6.txt
278fc20b [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc20b [grok-build-0.1] #- The timestamp is no longer resetting to `ffff` / `ffd8` on every sample like before.
278fc20b [grok-build-0.1] #That's progress.
278fc20c [grok-build-0.1] #- However, it is still not perfectly continuous:
278fc20c [grok-build-0.1] #  - Some samples share the same timestamp (e.g. `e620` appears twice).
278fc20c [grok-build-0.1] #  - There are still upward jumps (e.g. `e5fd` → `fd21`).
278fc20c [grok-build-0.1] #- The address/data patterns still look very repetitive (`4c4b 4c`, `484b 4c`, etc.), but you said
278fc20c [grok-build-0.1] #manual polling looks good, so the reconstruction itself is probably okay.
278fc20d [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc20d [grok-build-0.1] #Quick suggestions
278fc20d [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc20e [grok-build-0.1] #If you want the timestamp to be even cleaner (steadily decreasing across the whole capture with
278fc20e [grok-build-0.1] #fewer duplicates), you have two options:
278fc20e [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc20e [grok-build-0.1] #• A.* Keep the current logic but add a forced decrement right after restoring the tick in
278fc20f [grok-build-0.1] #`changed`:
278fc20f [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc20f [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc20f [grok-build-0.1] #    mov     isr, x
278fc210 [grok-build-0.1] #    jmp     x--, loop          ; force one decrement before going back
278fc210 [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc210 [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc210 [grok-build-0.1] #• B.* (Cleaner for long captures) Stop trying to keep a single running counter in the PIO
278fc211 [grok-build-0.1] #and instead accumulate deltas on the C++ side (using the difference between consecutive tick
278fc211 [grok-build-0.1] #values). This is usually more reliable.
278fc211 [grok-build-0.1] #
278fc211 [grok-build-0.1] #Would you like me to show the version with a forced decrement, or would you rather switch to
278fc212 [grok-build-0.1] #accumulating time on the C++ side?
