27a128ac [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128ac [grok-build-0.1] #How to regenerate a checksum in this project
27a128ac [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128ac [grok-build-0.1] #Godot stores checksums/hashes in two main places:
27a128ad [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128ad [grok-build-0.1] #1. `.import` files (for assets like your `icon.svg`)
27a128ad [grok-build-0.1] #2. `.uid` files (for scripts and scenes)
27a128ad [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128ae [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27a128ae [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128ae [grok-build-0.1] #Best way (Recommended)
27a128ae [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128af [grok-build-0.1] #1. Open the project in the Godot editor.
27a128af [grok-build-0.1] #2. In the FileSystem dock, find the source file (e.g. `icon.svg` or any `.glb`).
27a128af [grok-build-0.1] #3. Right-click it → Reimport.
27a128af [grok-build-0.1] #4. Godot will delete the old cached version and generate a fresh checksum + import file.
27a128b0 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128b0 [grok-build-0.1] #You can also force a full regeneration by:
27a128b0 [grok-build-0.1] #- Deleting the `.import` file (e.g. `icon.svg.import`)
27a128b0 [grok-build-0.1] #- Deleting the corresponding file inside `.godot/imported/`
27a128b1 [grok-build-0.1] #- Then reimporting
27a128b1 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128b1 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27a128b1 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128b2 [grok-build-0.1] #If you want to do it without the editor
27a128b2 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128b2 [grok-build-0.1] #Tell me which file you want the checksum regenerated for, and paste the current content (or
27a128b2 [grok-build-0.1] #the base64 if it's binary).
27a128b3 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128b3 [grok-build-0.1] #Common cases right now:
27a128b3 [grok-build-0.1] #- `icon.svg` → needs new hash in `icon.svg.import`
27a128b3 [grok-build-0.1] #- A new `.glb` you want to spawn
27a128b4 [grok-build-0.1] #- A `.gd` or `.tscn` file (these use UIDs)
27a128b4 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128b4 [grok-build-0.1] #Just say something like:
27a128b4 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128b5 [grok-build-0.1] #	regenerate for icon.svg"
27a128b5 [grok-build-0.1] #	r
27a128b5 [grok-build-0.1] #	new glb checksum"
27a128b5 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128b6 [grok-build-0.1] #And paste the file(s). I'll give you the updated `.import` or `.uid` content.
27a128b6 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128b6 [grok-build-0.1] #Which file's checksum do you need regenerated?
27a128b6 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a128b7 [roha] regenerate for icon.svg"
27a128b7 [roha] r
27a128b7 [roha] new glb checksum"
27a12d08 [nitro@ryzen5] does rpc not have any standard process for ack with checksum on say the tick payload?
27a12da3 [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.022 59.61KB 9.38s
27a12da3 [grok-build-0.1] #• No.*
27a12da3 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12da4 [grok-build-0.1] #Standard JSON-RPC 2.0 does not have any built-in mechanism for ACKs with checksums.
27a12da4 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12da4 [grok-build-0.1] #What JSON-RPC actually gives you
27a12da4 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12da5 [grok-build-0.1] #╭──────────────────────────────┬────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
27a12da5 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Feature                     │  JSON-RPC 2.0  │  Notes                                            │
27a12da5 [grok-build-0.1] #├──────────────────────────────┼────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
27a12da5 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Request / Response          │  Yes           │  Uses `id` to match requests with replies         │
27a12da6 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Error handling              │  Yes           │  `"error"` field in response                      │
27a12da6 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Notifications (no reply)    │  Yes           │  Send without `id`                                │
27a12da6 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Checksum / Integrity        │  **No**        │  Not part of the spec                             │
27a12da6 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Automatic ACK               │  **No**        │  Only the response itself                         │
27a12da7 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Duplicate detection         │  **No**        │  You have to implement it                         │
27a12da7 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Ordering / Reliability      │  **No**        │  WebSocket doesn't guarantee order on reconnects  │
27a12da7 [grok-build-0.1] #╰──────────────────────────────┴────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
27a12da7 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12da8 [grok-build-0.1] #Current situation in your project
27a12da8 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12da8 [grok-build-0.1] #You're currently using JSON-RPC requests for everything (including `tick`), and Godot is
27a12da8 [grok-build-0.1] #replying with the full original payload. This is why you're getting huge nested echoes.
27a12da9 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12da9 [grok-build-0.1] #For something like a `tick` (which looks like a heartbeat or counter), you usually have two
27a12da9 [grok-build-0.1] #choices:
27a12da9 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12daa [grok-build-0.1] #Options
27a12daa [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12daa [grok-build-0.1] #• Option 1: Use JSON-RPC properly (recommended for now)*
27a12daa [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12dab [grok-build-0.1] #- Only send a response when you actually need to acknowledge something.
27a12dab [grok-build-0.1] #- For `tick`, you can treat it as a notification (no reply needed), or send a very
27a12dab [grok-build-0.1] #lightweight reply.
27a12dab [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12dac [grok-build-0.1] #• Option 2: Add your own checksum layer on top*
27a12dac [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12dac [grok-build-0.1] #If you want integrity checking on the payload, you have to add it yourself. Example:
27a12dac [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12dad [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12dad [grok-build-0.1] #{
27a12dad [grok-build-0.1] #  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
27a12dad [grok-build-0.1] #  "id": 42,
27a12dae [grok-build-0.1] #  "method": "tick",
27a12dae [grok-build-0.1] #  "params": {
27a12dae [grok-build-0.1] #    "count": 81,
27a12dae [grok-build-0.1] #    "checksum": "a3f2c9..."   // you compute this
27a12daf [grok-build-0.1] #  }
27a12daf [grok-build-0.1] #}
27a12daf [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12daf [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12db0 [grok-build-0.1] #Then on the Godot side you would:
27a12db0 [grok-build-0.1] #1. Compute the checksum of the received data
27a12db0 [grok-build-0.1] #2. Compare it
27a12db0 [grok-build-0.1] #3. Optionally reply with an ACK containing the same checksum or a status
27a12db0 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12db1 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27a12db1 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12db1 [grok-build-0.1] #Questions to help you decide:
27a12db2 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12db2 [grok-build-0.1] #1. Why do you want a checksum on `tick`?
27a12db2 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Just to know it arrived?
27a12db2 [grok-build-0.1] #   - To detect corruption?
27a12db2 [grok-build-0.1] #   - To prevent duplicate processing?
27a12db3 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Something else?
27a12db3 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12db3 [grok-build-0.1] #2. Do you need Godot to reply to every `tick`, or is it okay if most ticks are
27a12db4 [grok-build-0.1] #fire-and-forget?
27a12db4 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12db4 [grok-build-0.1] #3. Are `tick` messages important, or are they just a test/heartbeat right now?
27a12db4 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a12db4 [grok-build-0.1] #Reply with your goal and I can show you a clean pattern (either proper JSON-RPC usage or a
27a12db5 [grok-build-0.1] #lightweight checksum + ack approach).
27a12db5 [grok-build-0.1] #
