27a1a5f1 [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.041 99.14KB 9.03s
27a1a5f1 [grok-build-0.1] #Yes, a scene can run on a texture in Godot.
27a1a5f1 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5f2 [grok-build-0.1] #This is a very common technique. Here's what you should understand about it:
27a1a5f2 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5f2 [grok-build-0.1] #What "running on a texture" actually means
27a1a5f2 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5f3 [grok-build-0.1] #In Godot, you can render an entire scene (or part of one) into a texture instead of directly to the
27a1a5f3 [grok-build-0.1] #screen. This texture can then be used in multiple ways:
27a1a5f3 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5f3 [grok-build-0.1] #- Displayed inside a 2D UI (e.g. inside your current `Control` scene)
27a1a5f4 [grok-build-0.1] #- Applied as a material on a 3D object
27a1a5f4 [grok-build-0.1] #- Used for effects like portals, mirrors, minimaps, or security cameras
27a1a5f4 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5f4 [grok-build-0.1] #The main tool: SubViewport
27a1a5f5 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5f5 [grok-build-0.1] #The standard way to do this in Godot 4 is with a `SubViewport`.
27a1a5f5 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5f5 [grok-build-0.1] #Key ideas:
27a1a5f6 [grok-build-0.1] #- A `SubViewport` acts like a separate render target.
27a1a5f6 [grok-build-0.1] #- You can put a full scene (including 3D nodes and GLB instances) inside it.
27a1a5f6 [grok-build-0.1] #- You can then grab the result as a texture and display it wherever you want.
27a1a5f6 [grok-build-0.1] #- The scene inside the SubViewport runs normally (its scripts, physics, etc. still work).
27a1a5f7 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5f7 [grok-build-0.1] #How this relates to your current project
27a1a5f7 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5f7 [grok-build-0.1] #You're currently using a `Control` as the root. This makes direct 3D rendering awkward. Rendering a
27a1a5f8 [grok-build-0.1] #scene onto a texture is actually one of the cleanest ways to solve this without changing your root
27a1a5f8 [grok-build-0.1] #node structure.
27a1a5f8 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5f8 [grok-build-0.1] #Common patterns:
27a1a5f9 [grok-build-0.1] #- Keep your 2D UI and log view on the main scene.
27a1a5f9 [grok-build-0.1] #- Put your 3D GLB spawning logic inside a `SubViewport`.
27a1a5f9 [grok-build-0.1] #- Show the 3D content in your UI using a `TextureRect` or `SubViewportContainer`.
27a1a5f9 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5fa [grok-build-0.1] #Important things to consider
27a1a5fa [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5fa [grok-build-0.1] #1. Performance
27a1a5fa [grok-build-0.1] #   Rendering a scene to a texture has a cost. If you're spawning lots of GLBs or running complex
27a1a5fb [grok-build-0.1] #scenes inside the viewport, it can get expensive.
27a1a5fb [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5fb [grok-build-0.1] #2. Input
27a1a5fb [grok-build-0.1] #   If the scene inside the texture needs to receive mouse/keyboard input, you have to handle
27a1a5fc [grok-build-0.1] #forwarding that input yourself. It's not automatic.
27a1a5fc [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5fc [grok-build-0.1] #3. Size & Resolution
27a1a5fc [grok-build-0.1] #   The SubViewport has its own size. If you make it too small, the output will look pixelated. If
27a1a5fd [grok-build-0.1] #too big, it wastes performance.
27a1a5fd [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5fd [grok-build-0.1] #4. Ownership
27a1a5fd [grok-build-0.1] #   The scene running inside the SubViewport is still a normal scene. It can have its own root node,
27a1a5fe [grok-build-0.1] #scripts, and even its own child nodes being spawned.
27a1a5fe [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a5fe [grok-build-0.1] #5. Alternatives
27a1a5fe [grok-build-0.1] #   - Change your main scene root to `Node3D` + overlay 2D on top (more "traditional" 3D project
27a1a5ff [grok-build-0.1] #structure).
27a1a5ff [grok-build-0.1] #   - Use multiple scenes and switch between them.
27a1a5ff [grok-build-0.1] #   - Render to texture only for specific effects.
27a1a5ff [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a600 [grok-build-0.1] #Questions worth thinking about
27a1a600 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a600 [grok-build-0.1] #- Do you want the 3D content to appear inside your current 2D interface (like a window or
27a1a600 [grok-build-0.1] #panel), or do you eventually want the whole project to be 3D with UI on top?
27a1a601 [grok-build-0.1] #- Will the spawned GLB scenes need to be interactive, or are they mostly visual?
27a1a601 [grok-build-0.1] #- Are you thinking of having multiple separate "scenes" running at the same time on different
27a1a601 [grok-build-0.1] #textures?
27a1a601 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1a602 [grok-build-0.1] #Would you like to discuss the trade-offs between using a SubViewport versus restructuring the
27a1a602 [grok-build-0.1] #project root?
27a1a602 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1aaeb [nitro@ryzen5] this is for a ui
27a1abf6 [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.042 102.2KB 16.17s
27a1abf7 [grok-build-0.1] #• Got it.* This changes the focus.
27a1abf7 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1abf7 [grok-build-0.1] #Since this is intended to be part of a UI, here are the most important things to consider:
27a1abf7 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1abf8 [grok-build-0.1] #1. SubViewport is basically required
27a1abf8 [grok-build-0.1] #If you want 3D content (GLBs) to appear inside a 2D UI, you will almost certainly need a
27a1abf8 [grok-build-0.1] #`SubViewport`.
27a1abf8 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1abf9 [grok-build-0.1] #This lets you render a 3D scene into a texture that can then be displayed in your `Control`-based
27a1abf9 [grok-build-0.1] #UI (using a `TextureRect`, `SubViewportContainer`, or similar). Direct 3D nodes don't render
27a1abf9 [grok-build-0.1] #properly under a plain `Control`.
27a1abf9 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1abfa [grok-build-0.1] #2. Separation between UI and 3D content
27a1abfa [grok-build-0.1] #You should think about how separate the 3D part should be:
27a1abfa [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1abfa [grok-build-0.1] #- Will the 3D view be one panel/window inside your UI?
27a1abfb [grok-build-0.1] #- Or do you want the ability to spawn multiple independent 3D views?
27a1abfb [grok-build-0.1] #- How tightly coupled should the 3D content be with your logging and control UI?
27a1abfb [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1abfb [grok-build-0.1] #Keeping the 3D rendering in its own subtree (inside the SubViewport) is generally cleaner.
27a1abfc [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1abfc [grok-build-0.1] #3. Camera and Environment
27a1abfc [grok-build-0.1] #When rendering to a texture for UI use, you are responsible for:
27a1abfc [grok-build-0.1] #- Having a `Camera3D` inside the SubViewport.
27a1abfd [grok-build-0.1] #- Having some lighting (otherwise GLBs will be black).
27a1abfd [grok-build-0.1] #- Deciding the camera behavior (fixed, following the spawned object, user controllable, etc.).
27a1abfd [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1abfd [grok-build-0.1] #This is different from a normal 3D scene where you might rely on the editor camera.
27a1abfe [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1abfe [grok-build-0.1] #4. Size and Resolution Management
27a1abfe [grok-build-0.1] #This is more important than it first appears:
27a1abfe [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1abff [grok-build-0.1] #- The SubViewport has its own fixed resolution.
27a1abff [grok-build-0.1] #- If your UI panel can be resized, you need to decide whether to scale the viewport or re-render at
27a1abff [grok-build-0.1] #a new size.
27a1abff [grok-build-0.1] #- Higher resolution = better quality but higher performance cost.
27a1ac00 [grok-build-0.1] #- You probably don't want the 3D view to run at full window resolution unless necessary.
27a1ac00 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1ac00 [grok-build-0.1] #5. Performance Isolation
27a1ac00 [grok-build-0.1] #Rendering 3D to a texture means it has its own render cost.
27a1ac01 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1ac01 [grok-build-0.1] #Things worth considering:
27a1ac01 [grok-build-0.1] #- Should the 3D viewport keep rendering when the panel is hidden or minimized?
27a1ac01 [grok-build-0.1] #- Do you want to limit the frame rate of the 3D part separately from the main UI?
27a1ac02 [grok-build-0.1] #- How many GLB instances do you expect to have active at once inside the view?
27a1ac02 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1ac02 [grok-build-0.1] #6. Input Handling
27a1ac02 [grok-build-0.1] #If the 3D content ever needs mouse interaction (clicking on objects, orbiting the camera, etc.),
27a1ac03 [grok-build-0.1] #you have to manually forward input from your UI into the SubViewport. It's not automatic like a
27a1ac03 [grok-build-0.1] #normal 3D scene.
27a1ac03 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1ac03 [grok-build-0.1] #7. Scene Ownership
27a1ac04 [grok-build-0.1] #When you receive a "spawn" event, you need to decide:
27a1ac04 [grok-build-0.1] #- Where exactly in the node tree the new GLB instance lives (inside the SubViewport's root).
27a1ac04 [grok-build-0.1] #- Whether each spawn replaces the previous one, or you can have multiple.
27a1ac04 [grok-build-0.1] #- Whether the spawned scene runs its own scripts or is mostly static.
27a1ac05 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1ac05 [grok-build-0.1] #8. Long-term UI Structure
27a1ac05 [grok-build-0.1] #Since this is UI-driven, you might want to think about:
27a1ac05 [grok-build-0.1] #- Using a `SubViewportContainer` so the 3D view can be treated more like a normal UI element.
27a1ac06 [grok-build-0.1] #- Whether the 3D view should be able to be moved, resized, or closed like other UI panels.
27a1ac06 [grok-build-0.1] #- How the log view and the 3D view should coexist on screen.
27a1ac06 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1ac06 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27a1ac07 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1ac07 [grok-build-0.1] #Most important questions right now:
27a1ac07 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1ac07 [grok-build-0.1] #- Do you want the 3D content to appear in a fixed panel, or should it be more flexible
27a1ac08 [grok-build-0.1] #(resizable, multiple views, etc.)?
27a1ac08 [grok-build-0.1] #- Is the 3D mostly for visualization (just showing the models), or will it need any
27a1ac08 [grok-build-0.1] #interaction later?
27a1ac08 [grok-build-0.1] #- Do you expect to show one 3D model at a time, or potentially several at once?
27a1ac09 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1ac09 [grok-build-0.1] #These will strongly influence how you structure the SubViewport setup.
27a1ac09 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1af47 [nitro@ryzen5] is there pixmap and texture type classes?
27a1b08a [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.042 105.9KB 19.27s
27a1b08a [grok-build-0.1] #Yes, but not exactly in the way you might expect.
27a1b08a [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b08b [grok-build-0.1] #Direct Answer
27a1b08b [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b08b [grok-build-0.1] #- Pixmap: No. Godot does not have a class called `Pixmap`.
27a1b08b [grok-build-0.1] #- Texture: Yes. Godot has several texture-related classes.
27a1b08c [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b08c [grok-build-0.1] #What Godot Actually Has
27a1b08c [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b08c [grok-build-0.1] #╭────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────╮
27a1b08d [grok-build-0.1] #│  Concept           │  Godot Class(es)                   │  Purpose                                       │  Closest to "Pixmap"?  │
27a1b08d [grok-build-0.1] #├────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┤
27a1b08d [grok-build-0.1] #│  Raw pixel data    │  `Image`                           │  Stores actual pixel data in memory            │  Yes (closest thing)   │
27a1b08d [grok-build-0.1] #│  Texture resource  │  `Texture2D`, `ImageTexture`       │  Usable texture that can be drawn              │  No                    │
27a1b08e [grok-build-0.1] #│  Viewport output   │  `ViewportTexture`                 │  Texture generated by rendering a SubViewport  │  No                    │
27a1b08e [grok-build-0.1] #│  3D texture        │  `Texture3D`                       │  Used for 3D materials                         │  No                    │
27a1b08e [grok-build-0.1] #│  UI display        │  `TextureRect`, `Sprite2D`         │  Nodes that display textures                   │  No                    │
27a1b08e [grok-build-0.1] #╰────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────╯
27a1b08f [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b08f [grok-build-0.1] #Key Points for Your Use Case
27a1b08f [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b08f [grok-build-0.1] #Since you're working on a UI that needs to display a rendered scene:
27a1b090 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b090 [grok-build-0.1] #- `Image` is Godot’s version of raw pixel/pixmap data. You can create, modify, and read
27a1b090 [grok-build-0.1] #pixels from it directly.
27a1b090 [grok-build-0.1] #- To turn pixel data into something you can display, you usually convert it into an `ImageTexture`.
27a1b091 [grok-build-0.1] #- When you render a scene to a texture (using `SubViewport`), you don’t usually work with `Image`
27a1b091 [grok-build-0.1] #directly. Instead you get a `ViewportTexture`.
27a1b091 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b091 [grok-build-0.1] #Important Distinction
27a1b092 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b092 [grok-build-0.1] #╭─────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────╮
27a1b092 [grok-build-0.1] #│  You Want To...                         │  What You Should Use                │
27a1b092 [grok-build-0.1] #├─────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
27a1b093 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Render a 3D scene into the UI          │  `SubViewport` + `ViewportTexture`  │
27a1b093 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Create or manipulate raw pixels        │  `Image` + `ImageTexture`           │
27a1b093 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Just display an existing texture       │  `TextureRect`                      │
27a1b093 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Apply a texture to a 3D model          │  `StandardMaterial3D`               │
27a1b094 [grok-build-0.1] #╰─────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────╯
27a1b094 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b094 [grok-build-0.1] #Recommendation for Your Project
27a1b094 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b095 [grok-build-0.1] #Because you said this is for a UI, the most relevant path is:
27a1b095 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b095 [grok-build-0.1] #- Use a `SubViewport` to render your 3D content.
27a1b095 [grok-build-0.1] #- Access it via `SubViewport.get_texture()` (returns a `ViewportTexture`).
27a1b096 [grok-build-0.1] #- Display that texture in your 2D UI using a `TextureRect` or `SubViewportContainer`.
27a1b096 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b096 [grok-build-0.1] #You generally won’t need to touch `Image` unless you want to do pixel-level operations (like
27a1b096 [grok-build-0.1] #reading back the rendered result, applying filters, or saving screenshots).
27a1b097 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b097 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27a1b097 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b097 [grok-build-0.1] #Would you like me to explain the difference in practice between using a `ViewportTexture` vs
27a1b098 [grok-build-0.1] #creating an `ImageTexture` manually?
27a1b098 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b4e9 [nitro@ryzen5] for antia aliased clean lines courtesy shaders
27a1b608 [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.044 109.4KB 17.53s
27a1b608 [grok-build-0.1] #• Yes.* For anti-aliased clean lines when rendering to a texture for UI, shaders become one
27a1b609 [grok-build-0.1] #of the stronger options.
27a1b609 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b609 [grok-build-0.1] #Here's what you should consider important:
27a1b609 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b60a [grok-build-0.1] #1. Viewport MSAA vs Shader-based Anti-Aliasing
27a1b60a [grok-build-0.1] #- SubViewport MSAA (`msaa_2d` / `msaa_3d`) gives you free anti-aliasing, but it's limited.
27a1b60a [grok-build-0.1] #It struggles with very thin lines (1–2 pixels) and can look blurry or inconsistent once the texture
27a1b60a [grok-build-0.1] #is scaled or displayed in the UI.
27a1b60b [grok-build-0.1] #- Shader-based anti-aliasing (especially analytical or SDF methods) usually gives much
27a1b60b [grok-build-0.1] #cleaner, sharper results for lines, particularly at small sizes or when the output texture is not
27a1b60b [grok-build-0.1] #rendered at native resolution.
27a1b60b [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b60c [grok-build-0.1] #For UI work, many people find MSAA alone is not enough for "clean" lines.
27a1b60c [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b60c [grok-build-0.1] #2. Where the Lines Are Being Drawn Matters
27a1b60c [grok-build-0.1] #This affects the best approach:
27a1b60d [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b60d [grok-build-0.1] #- 2D lines inside the SubViewport: Shaders or `Line2D` with proper settings can work well.
27a1b60d [grok-build-0.1] #- 3D lines (wireframes, edges, paths on GLB models): This is harder. Godot’s default
27a1b60d [grok-build-0.1] #wireframe rendering is usually aliased. You’ll almost always need a custom shader here.
27a1b60e [grok-build-0.1] #- Lines drawn directly in the main Control UI (outside the texture): Different story — you
27a1b60e [grok-build-0.1] #can often get away with simpler methods.
27a1b60e [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b60e [grok-build-0.1] #Since you said this is for a UI, decide whether the clean lines should live inside the
27a1b60f [grok-build-0.1] #rendered texture or in the 2D UI layer on top of it.
27a1b60f [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b60f [grok-build-0.1] #3. Resolution and Scaling Issues
27a1b60f [grok-build-0.1] #When rendering to a texture:
27a1b610 [grok-build-0.1] #- Thin lines are very sensitive to the SubViewport’s resolution.
27a1b610 [grok-build-0.1] #- If the UI panel can be resized, or if the texture is displayed at a different scale than it was
27a1b610 [grok-build-0.1] #rendered, aliasing gets worse.
27a1b610 [grok-build-0.1] #- Shader-based lines can be made resolution-independent (or at least more tolerant) compared to
27a1b611 [grok-build-0.1] #rasterized geometry.
27a1b611 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b611 [grok-build-0.1] #4. Performance Cost
27a1b611 [grok-build-0.1] #Important considerations here:
27a1b612 [grok-build-0.1] #- Heavy anti-aliasing shaders (especially per-pixel distance calculations) have a cost.
27a1b612 [grok-build-0.1] #- If you're running the SubViewport at high resolution + expensive line shaders + multiple spawned
27a1b612 [grok-build-0.1] #GLBs, it adds up.
27a1b612 [grok-build-0.1] #- You may want to render the 3D/content at a lower internal resolution and let the shader handle
27a1b613 [grok-build-0.1] #the clean lines.
27a1b613 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b613 [grok-build-0.1] #5. Common Approaches in Godot 4 (ranked for your case)
27a1b613 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b614 [grok-build-0.1] #╭───────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────┬──────────────┬────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────╮
27a1b614 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Approach                     │  Cleanliness for Lines  │  Difficulty  │  Good for UI Texture?  │  Notes                             │
27a1b614 [grok-build-0.1] #├───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤
27a1b614 [grok-build-0.1] #│  `Line2D` node + antialiased  │  Good                   │  Low         │  Yes                   │  Simplest for 2D                   │
27a1b615 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Viewport MSAA only           │  Average                │  Very Low    │  Limited               │  Often not enough for thin lines   │
27a1b615 [grok-build-0.1] #│  CanvasItem shader (2D)       │  Very Good              │  Medium      │  Yes                   │  Analytical AA possible            │
27a1b615 [grok-build-0.1] #│  3D line shader               │  Excellent              │  High        │  Yes                   │  Needed for proper 3D edges/wires  │
27a1b615 [grok-build-0.1] #│  SDF-based line shader        │  Excellent              │  High        │  Yes                   │  Very crisp, resolution tolerant   │
