27a1b616 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b616 [grok-build-0.1] #6. Other Practical Factors
27a1b616 [grok-build-0.1] #- Alpha blending: Clean lines often require proper alpha handling, which can interact badly
27a1b617 [grok-build-0.1] #with the texture format or UI blending.
27a1b617 [grok-build-0.1] #- Pixel snapping: Godot’s 2D rendering can snap things to pixels, which fights
27a1b617 [grok-build-0.1] #anti-aliasing. You may need to disable it in places.
27a1b617 [grok-build-0.1] #- Final display: Even if the lines are perfect in the SubViewport texture, how you sample
27a1b618 [grok-build-0.1] #that texture in the UI (`TextureRect` filtering, etc.) can re-introduce aliasing.
27a1b618 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b618 [grok-build-0.1] #Key Questions for Your Specific Case
27a1b618 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b619 [grok-build-0.1] #- Are the clean lines mainly 2D (diagrams, connections, UI overlays) or do they need to be
27a1b619 [grok-build-0.1] #drawn on/around 3D models?
27a1b619 [grok-build-0.1] #- Do you need the lines to stay crisp when the UI panel is resized?
27a1b619 [grok-build-0.1] #- Are these lines high-frequency (lots of them) or relatively few?
27a1b61a [grok-build-0.1] #- Do you need the lines to be interactive (selectable, hoverable) or purely visual?
27a1b61a [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1b61a [grok-build-0.1] #This will determine whether a relatively simple 2D shader approach is enough, or whether you need
27a1b61a [grok-build-0.1] #something more involved.
27a1b61b [grok-build-0.1] #
