27a1c383 [grok-build-0.1] #• Understood.* This is a much clearer picture now.
27a1c384 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c384 [grok-build-0.1] #Summary of Requirements
27a1c384 [grok-build-0.1] #- Frame buffer is generated on the 6502 side from video opcodes.
27a1c384 [grok-build-0.1] #- Lines are sent as they’re generated (or per frame).
27a1c385 [grok-build-0.1] #- Full redraw every frame.
27a1c385 [grok-build-0.1] #- Lines are discardable — no persistence, no fading, no state to maintain between frames.
27a1c385 [grok-build-0.1] #- Goal is simple + clean anti-aliased lines.
27a1c385 [grok-build-0.1] #- This will be displayed as a UI element (via texture/SubViewport).
27a1c386 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c386 [grok-build-0.1] #What Matters Most in This Scenario
27a1c386 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c386 [grok-build-0.1] #Here are the key things you should weigh, in rough order of importance for keeping things simple
27a1c387 [grok-build-0.1] #and clean:
27a1c387 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c387 [grok-build-0.1] #1. Redraw Strategy (This is the Simplest Part)
27a1c387 [grok-build-0.1] #Since everything is discardable and you want a full redraw every frame, the model should be:
27a1c388 [grok-build-0.1] #- Receive new set of lines
27a1c388 [grok-build-0.1] #- Clear previous content
27a1c388 [grok-build-0.1] #- Draw the new lines
27a1c388 [grok-build-0.1] #- Done
27a1c389 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c389 [grok-build-0.1] #This is actually one of the easiest vector display scenarios. You don’t need to worry about
27a1c389 [grok-build-0.1] #incremental drawing, dirty regions, or managing lifetime of lines. The simplicity here is a real
27a1c389 [grok-build-0.1] #advantage.
27a1c38a [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c38a [grok-build-0.1] #2. Data Shape You’ll Receive
27a1c38a [grok-build-0.1] #This affects how clean the implementation can stay. Think about what the simulator will actually
27a1c38a [grok-build-0.1] #send over the wire:
27a1c38b [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c38b [grok-build-0.1] #- A list of line segments per frame? (`[{x1,y1,x2,y2}, ...]`)
27a1c38b [grok-build-0.1] #- Individual draw commands as they happen?
27a1c38b [grok-build-0.1] #- A full frame buffer that Godot has to interpret?
27a1c38c [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c38c [grok-build-0.1] #The cleaner and more regular the data structure, the simpler the Godot side becomes. Sending a
27a1c38c [grok-build-0.1] #batch of lines once per frame (or when ready) is generally easier to handle cleanly than a stream
27a1c38c [grok-build-0.1] #of individual opcodes.
27a1c38d [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c38d [grok-build-0.1] #3. Rendering Method (The Real Quality vs Simplicity Trade-off)
27a1c38d [grok-build-0.1] #This is where you make the biggest decision for "clean lines":
27a1c38d [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c38e [grok-build-0.1] #- Option A (Simplest): Use `Line2D` nodes. Create them fresh each frame, then free them.
27a1c38e [grok-build-0.1] #Easy to understand, but can get messy with many lines and sometimes struggles with very thin clean
27a1c38e [grok-build-0.1] #anti-aliasing.
27a1c38e [grok-build-0.1] #- Option B (Cleaner long-term): Use `draw_line()` in `_draw()` or a simple custom shader.
27a1c38f [grok-build-0.1] #Usually gives more consistent anti-aliasing control with less node overhead.
27a1c38f [grok-build-0.1] #- Option C: Pre-bake into an `Image` / `ImageTexture`. Simple in concept, but often worse
27a1c38f [grok-build-0.1] #for anti-aliased lines.
27a1c38f [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c390 [grok-build-0.1] #For a baseline of "simple + clean", most people underestimate how quickly `Line2D` nodes become
27a1c390 [grok-build-0.1] #annoying when redrawing many lines every frame.
27a1c390 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c390 [grok-build-0.1] #4. SubViewport + UI Texture Specifics
27a1c391 [grok-build-0.1] #Since this has to live in a UI texture:
27a1c391 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c391 [grok-build-0.1] #- You’ll want the SubViewport to be cleared cleanly every frame.
27a1c391 [grok-build-0.1] #- Texture filtering on the final display side is critical. Linear filtering will destroy line
27a1c392 [grok-build-0.1] #crispness.
27a1c392 [grok-build-0.1] #- Resolution of the SubViewport matters a lot for thin lines. Too low and they look bad; too high
27a1c392 [grok-build-0.1] #and you waste performance for no gain.
27a1c392 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c393 [grok-build-0.1] #5. Coordinate System & Precision
27a1c393 [grok-build-0.1] #Because this comes from a 6502 simulator, you’ll need a clean, predictable mapping from the
27a1c393 [grok-build-0.1] #original vector space into the SubViewport.
27a1c393 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c394 [grok-build-0.1] #Things to watch:
27a1c394 [grok-build-0.1] #- Integer vs floating point conversion
27a1c394 [grok-build-0.1] #- Whether you snap coordinates or keep them sub-pixel
27a1c394 [grok-build-0.1] #- Aspect ratio handling
27a1c395 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c395 [grok-build-0.1] #Even small inconsistencies here can make lines look uneven or stepped.
27a1c395 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c395 [grok-build-0.1] #6. When to Update
27a1c396 [grok-build-0.1] #You mentioned the frame buffer is "sent as generated". This raises a practical question:
27a1c396 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c396 [grok-build-0.1] #- Do you want to redraw the moment new line data arrives?
27a1c396 [grok-build-0.1] #- Or buffer it and redraw once per Godot frame (more stable)?
27a1c397 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c397 [grok-build-0.1] #For maximum simplicity, redrawing once per frame (even if you have partial data) is often cleaner
27a1c397 [grok-build-0.1] #than trying to react to every incoming packet.
27a1c397 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c398 [grok-build-0.1] #7. Performance Reality Check
27a1c398 [grok-build-0.1] #Since lines are discardable, you have some freedom:
27a1c398 [grok-build-0.1] #- You can afford to throw away and recreate drawing elements every frame.
27a1c398 [grok-build-0.1] #- You don’t need to optimize for reuse or object pooling yet.
27a1c399 [grok-build-0.1] #- However, if the number of lines gets high, even a simple approach can start to feel heavy if
27a1c399 [grok-build-0.1] #you’re creating hundreds of `Line2D` nodes every frame.
27a1c399 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c399 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27a1c39a [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c39a [grok-build-0.1] #Bottom Line Recommendation (for keeping it simple)
27a1c39a [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c39a [grok-build-0.1] #For a clean baseline, the strongest path is usually:
27a1c39b [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c39b [grok-build-0.1] #- Render inside a `SubViewport` using 2D canvas drawing.
27a1c39b [grok-build-0.1] #- Accept a list of lines per frame from the simulator.
27a1c39b [grok-build-0.1] #- Clear and redraw everything fresh each frame.
27a1c39c [grok-build-0.1] #- Use either a very lightweight line shader or careful `draw_line()` calls rather than lots of
27a1c39c [grok-build-0.1] #`Line2D` nodes.
27a1c39c [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c39c [grok-build-0.1] #This keeps the logic straightforward while giving you direct control over line quality.
27a1c39d [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c39d [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27a1c39d [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1c39d [grok-build-0.1] #Would you like to discuss the trade-offs between using `draw_line()` in a script vs a simple canvas
27a1c39e [grok-build-0.1] #shader for this kind of per-frame vector data? Or should we focus more on how the data should be
27a1c39e [grok-build-0.1] #structured when coming over the network?
27a1c39e [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cc70 [nitro@ryzen5] on other end of the connection
27a1cc77 [nitro@ryzen5] 1. CPU writes to `$3000` (DMA Go)  starts DVG.2. DVG reads from current bank (`$4000` or `$4400`, selected via `$4001` bit 1).3. DVG executes opcode stream starting at bank base:   - LABS  set absolute position + scale   - VCTR / SVEC  draw vector (line) with brightness   - JSRL  call subroutine (shape in ROM/RAM)   - RTSL  return from subroutine   - HALT  stop drawing, raise "done" signal4. While running: `$2002` bit 7 = 1 (busy)5. On HALT: clear bit 7  CPU can prepare next frame6. CPU flips bank for next frame (double buffering)
27a1cda9 [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.048 128.5KB 18.79s
27a1cda9 [grok-build-0.1] #• Good, this is very useful context.*
27a1cdaa [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdaa [grok-build-0.1] #This is the classic Atari DVG (Digital Vector Generator) behavior. Here's how I see this affecting
27a1cdaa [grok-build-0.1] #the Godot side, focusing on what matters for keeping things simple and clean.
27a1cdaa [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdab [grok-build-0.1] #1. Godot Should Not Interpret DVG Opcodes
27a1cdab [grok-build-0.1] #The most important architectural decision:
27a1cdab [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdab [grok-build-0.1] #- The emulator/simulator side (your TypeScript code) should do the hard work of executing
27a1cdac [grok-build-0.1] #the opcode stream (LABS, VCTR, SVEC, JSRL, RTSL, etc.).
27a1cdac [grok-build-0.1] #- Godot should receive a flat list of finished lines for each frame.
27a1cdac [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdac [grok-build-0.1] #Trying to run DVG opcodes on the Godot side would add unnecessary complexity (subroutine stacks,
27a1cdad [grok-build-0.1] #scale handling, bank switching, etc.). Keep the protocol dumb on the receiving end.
27a1cdad [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdad [grok-build-0.1] #2. What a Frame Should Look Like to Godot
27a1cdad [grok-build-0.1] #For a clean baseline, the ideal payload per frame is something like:
27a1cdae [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdae [grok-build-0.1] #- A list of vectors
27a1cdae [grok-build-0.1] #- Each vector contains:
27a1cdae [grok-build-0.1] #  - Start position (x, y)
27a1cdaf [grok-build-0.1] #  - End position (x, y)
27a1cdaf [grok-build-0.1] #  - Brightness / intensity (0–15 or normalized 0.0–1.0)
27a1cdaf [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdaf [grok-build-0.1] #This matches how the DVG actually works after all the opcode processing is done. The JSRL/RTSL
27a1cdb0 [grok-build-0.1] #subroutines should be flattened on the emulator side before sending.
27a1cdb0 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdb0 [grok-build-0.1] #3. Double Buffering Behavior
27a1cdb0 [grok-build-0.1] #The fact that the Atari side uses bank flipping (4000 / 4400) is actually helpful for us:
27a1cdb1 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdb1 [grok-build-0.1] #- One bank can be drawn by the DVG while the CPU prepares the next.
27a1cdb1 [grok-build-0.1] #- On the Godot side, you can treat incoming frames as complete snapshots.
27a1cdb1 [grok-build-0.1] #- You don’t need to worry about partial frames or mid-draw updates (assuming the simulator only
27a1cdb2 [grok-build-0.1] #sends a frame when the DVG reaches HALT).
27a1cdb2 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdb2 [grok-build-0.1] #This fits very well with your “full redraw every frame, discardable” requirement.
27a1cdb2 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdb3 [grok-build-0.1] #4. Timing and When to Send
27a1cdb3 [grok-build-0.1] #Key points for the protocol:
27a1cdb3 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdb3 [grok-build-0.1] #- Only send a frame to Godot when the DVG has finished (i.e. after HALT).
27a1cdb4 [grok-build-0.1] #- You probably don’t want to send every individual opcode or every small vector as it’s drawn. That
27a1cdb4 [grok-build-0.1] #would create too much traffic and jitter.
27a1cdb4 [grok-build-0.1] #- Sending one complete frame when the DVG finishes is much cleaner.
27a1cdb4 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdb5 [grok-build-0.1] #This also means Godot can simply replace the entire vector list each time it receives a new frame.
27a1cdb5 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdb5 [grok-build-0.1] #5. Coordinate and Scale Considerations
27a1cdb5 [grok-build-0.1] #From the DVG side you’ll need to decide:
27a1cdb6 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdb6 [grok-build-0.1] #- What coordinate range are you sending? (Classic DVG used roughly -1024 to +1023 or similar)
27a1cdb6 [grok-build-0.1] #- Are you sending the raw DVG coordinates, or are you normalizing them (e.g. -1 to +1 or 0 to
27a1cdb6 [grok-build-0.1] #viewport size)?
27a1cdb7 [grok-build-0.1] #- Scale information from LABS — does the simulator resolve this before sending, or do you want
27a1cdb7 [grok-build-0.1] #Godot to handle global scaling?
27a1cdb7 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdb7 [grok-build-0.1] #For a simple baseline, it’s usually cleaner if the emulator resolves position + scale and just
27a1cdb8 [grok-build-0.1] #sends final screen-space (or normalized) line coordinates.
27a1cdb8 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdb8 [grok-build-0.1] #6. Brightness
27a1cdb8 [grok-build-0.1] #Brightness is important for the vector look. Even for a clean baseline, you should probably carry
27a1cdb9 [grok-build-0.1] #brightness through so you can at least modulate line width or alpha later. Ignoring it completely
27a1cdb9 [grok-build-0.1] #early on can make everything look flat.
27a1cdb9 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdba [grok-build-0.1] #Summary – Recommended Mental Model
27a1cdba [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdba [grok-build-0.1] #╭───────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────╮
27a1cdba [grok-build-0.1] #│  Atari Side                   │  Godot Side                           │  Recommendation                           │
27a1cdba [grok-build-0.1] #├───────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┤
27a1cdbb [grok-build-0.1] #│  Runs DVG opcodes             │  Receives processed lines             │  Emulator does the opcode work            │
27a1cdbb [grok-build-0.1] #│  Bank switching               │  Just receives complete frames        │  Send only on HALT                        │
27a1cdbb [grok-build-0.1] #│  JSRL / RTSL                  │  Should not exist on Godot side       │  Flatten on emulator side                 │
27a1cdbb [grok-build-0.1] #│  Per-frame redraw             │  Replace entire display each frame    │  Simple and matches your goal             │
27a1cdbc [grok-build-0.1] #│  Brightness                   │  Should be sent                       │  Useful even for baseline                 │
27a1cdbc [grok-build-0.1] #╰───────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────╯
27a1cdbc [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdbc [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27a1cdbd [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdbd [grok-build-0.1] #Questions that would help refine this:
27a1cdbd [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdbd [grok-build-0.1] #1. Do you want to send raw DVG-style coordinates and let Godot do the final scaling, or normalize
27a1cdbe [grok-build-0.1] #on the emulator side before sending?
27a1cdbe [grok-build-0.1] #2. How often do you expect frames to be sent? (e.g. every time the DVG halts, or throttled to 60
27a1cdbe [grok-build-0.1] #fps?)
27a1cdbe [grok-build-0.1] #3. Will brightness be sent as the original 0–15 value, or normalized?
27a1cdbf [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1cdbf [grok-build-0.1] #This setup is actually quite friendly for a clean implementation on the Godot side, as long as the
27a1cdbf [grok-build-0.1] #emulator side does the opcode interpretation and sends finished line lists.
27a1cdbf [grok-build-0.1] #
