27a1d2d4 [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.049 133.4KB 18.90s
27a1d2d5 [grok-build-0.1] #• Good question.* Let's look at this realistically for your use case.
27a1d2d5 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2d5 [grok-build-0.1] #How clean can glTF represent a 2D vector line list?
27a1d2d5 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2d6 [grok-build-0.1] #• Short answer:*
27a1d2d6 [grok-build-0.1] #It can be reasonably clean if you treat it purely as a data container (using its buffer
27a1d2d6 [grok-build-0.1] #system), but it is not a natural fit for per-frame, discardable 2D vector data.
27a1d2d6 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2d6 [grok-build-0.1] #Best Way to Represent It in glTF
27a1d2d7 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2d7 [grok-build-0.1] #The cleanest representation in glTF would be:
27a1d2d7 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2d7 [grok-build-0.1] #- Use a Mesh with `mode: 1` (LINES)
27a1d2d8 [grok-build-0.1] #- Use `POSITION` accessor with `componentType: 5126` (FLOAT) and `type: "VEC2"`
27a1d2d8 [grok-build-0.1] #- Store the data in a buffer as a flat list of `x, y` pairs
27a1d2d8 [grok-build-0.1] #- Each pair of vertices = one line segment
27a1d2d8 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2d9 [grok-build-0.1] #Example logical structure:
27a1d2d9 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2d9 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2d9 [grok-build-0.1] #Buffer (raw bytes)
27a1d2da [grok-build-0.1] #[
27a1d2da [grok-build-0.1] #  x1, y1,     // start of line 1
27a1d2da [grok-build-0.1] #  x2, y2,     // end of line 1
27a1d2da [grok-build-0.1] #  x3, y3,     // start of line 2
27a1d2db [grok-build-0.1] #  x4, y4,     // end of line 2
27a1d2db [grok-build-0.1] #  ...
27a1d2db [grok-build-0.1] #]
27a1d2dc [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2dc [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2dc [grok-build-0.1] #This maps directly to what you asked for ("use buffer for x,y").
27a1d2dc [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2dc [grok-build-0.1] #You can also add:
27a1d2dd [grok-build-0.1] #- A second accessor (`COLOR_0`) for brightness per vertex (or per line if you duplicate vertices).
27a1d2dd [grok-build-0.1] #- This keeps brightness data alongside the coordinates in the buffer system.
27a1d2dd [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2de [grok-build-0.1] #Cleanliness Assessment
27a1d2de [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2de [grok-build-0.1] #╭─────────────────────────────┬────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
27a1d2de [grok-build-0.1] #│  Aspect                     │  How Clean?        │  Notes                                                        │
27a1d2df [grok-build-0.1] #├─────────────────────────────┼────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
27a1d2df [grok-build-0.1] #│  Buffer layout (x,y)        │  Quite clean       │  Simple flat VEC2 works well                                  │
27a1d2df [grok-build-0.1] #│  Line representation        │  Moderately clean  │  `mode: 1` (LINES) is the proper way                          │
27a1d2df [grok-build-0.1] #│  Brightness / intensity     │  Acceptable        │  Can use COLOR_0, but feels bolted on                         │
27a1d2df [grok-build-0.1] #│  Per-frame updates          │  **Poor**          │  glTF is not designed for this                                │
27a1d2e0 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Overhead                   │  High              │  Lots of JSON metadata for what is basically a list of lines  │
27a1d2e0 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Loading in Godot           │  Awkward           │  Standard importer is heavy for this                          │
27a1d2e0 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Simplicity                 │  Low               │  More complex than needed                                     │
27a1d2e1 [grok-build-0.1] #╰─────────────────────────────┴────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
27a1d2e1 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2e1 [grok-build-0.1] #Important Limitations for Your Case
27a1d2e1 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2e2 [grok-build-0.1] #- glTF is overkill here. You're sending simple, discardable line lists every frame.
27a1d2e2 [grok-build-0.1] #Wrapping them in a full glTF asset (with accessors, bufferViews, meshes, etc.) adds significant
27a1d2e2 [grok-build-0.1] #parsing and structural overhead for very little benefit.
27a1d2e2 [grok-build-0.1] #- Not great for streaming. Godot’s glTF loader is built for loading models once, not for
27a1d2e3 [grok-build-0.1] #receiving and discarding hundreds of tiny “models” per second.
27a1d2e3 [grok-build-0.1] #- Coordinate system friction. glTF expects 3D data by default. Forcing it into 2D requires
27a1d2e3 [grok-build-0.1] #extra care (especially with Y-axis direction).
27a1d2e3 [grok-build-0.1] #- You’d likely end up parsing the buffers manually anyway rather than using Godot’s normal
27a1d2e4 [grok-build-0.1] #glTF import pipeline.
27a1d2e4 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2e4 [grok-build-0.1] #When This Approach Makes Sense
27a1d2e4 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2e5 [grok-build-0.1] #Using glTF buffers would only really be worth it if you had one or more of these goals:
27a1d2e5 [grok-build-0.1] #- You want to reuse existing glTF tooling / exporters on the emulator side.
27a1d2e5 [grok-build-0.1] #- You plan to eventually attach more data (UVs, normals, etc.) later.
27a1d2e5 [grok-build-0.1] #- You want a standardized binary + JSON container format.
27a1d2e5 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2e6 [grok-build-0.1] #For a pure vector display peripheral with simple redraws, this is usually more complexity than
27a1d2e6 [grok-build-0.1] #necessary.
27a1d2e6 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2e6 [grok-build-0.1] #Better Alternatives (for context)
27a1d2e7 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2e7 [grok-build-0.1] #Since you want to keep it simple:
27a1d2e7 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2e7 [grok-build-0.1] #- Raw JSON array of lines (`[[x1,y1,x2,y2], ...]`)
27a1d2e8 [grok-build-0.1] #- A very small custom binary format (just a header + float buffer)
27a1d2e8 [grok-build-0.1] #- MessagePack or similar (compact but still structured)
27a1d2e8 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2e8 [grok-build-0.1] #These are all significantly cleaner than glTF for this specific workload.
27a1d2e9 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1d2e9 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
