27a1dcdc [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.052 142.7KB 14.95s
27a1dcdc [grok-build-0.1] #• Got it.* This is an important clarification.
27a1dcdc [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dcdc [grok-build-0.1] #You plan to receive data over the wire (WebSocket) and manually interpret it in GDScript,
27a1dcdd [grok-build-0.1] #rather than using Godot’s built-in glTF loader.
27a1dcdd [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dcdd [grok-build-0.1] #Given that glTF is meant to be a first-class citizen in the design, here’s what actually matters in
27a1dcdd [grok-build-0.1] #this scenario:
27a1dcde [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dcde [grok-build-0.1] #1. Parsing Cost in GDScript
27a1dcde [grok-build-0.1] #This is the biggest practical constraint.
27a1dcde [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dcdf [grok-build-0.1] #Manually parsing glTF in GDScript every frame has real overhead:
27a1dcdf [grok-build-0.1] #- JSON parsing (`JSON.parse_string`) is relatively expensive.
27a1dcdf [grok-build-0.1] #- Walking the glTF structure (accessors, bufferViews, buffers) adds more work.
27a1dcdf [grok-build-0.1] #- Extracting binary data (especially if base64 encoded) is even heavier.
27a1dce0 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dce0 [grok-build-0.1] #For a high-frequency vector display (even if lines are discardable), this can become noticeably
27a1dce0 [grok-build-0.1] #slow if the frame rate of the DVG is high or the number of lines is large.
27a1dce0 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dce1 [grok-build-0.1] #2. How Much of glTF Do You Actually Need?
27a1dce1 [grok-build-0.1] #Since you're interpreting it yourself, you don’t have to support the full spec. This is where you
27a1dce1 [grok-build-0.1] #can keep things reasonable.
27a1dce1 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dce2 [grok-build-0.1] #The minimal useful subset for your case would likely be:
27a1dce2 [grok-build-0.1] #- One mesh
27a1dce2 [grok-build-0.1] #- One primitive with `mode: 1` (LINES)
27a1dce2 [grok-build-0.1] #- `POSITION` accessor (VEC2)
27a1dce3 [grok-build-0.1] #- Optionally `COLOR_0` for brightness
27a1dce3 [grok-build-0.1] #- The buffer data itself
27a1dce3 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dce3 [grok-build-0.1] #Everything else (scenes, nodes, materials, animations, etc.) can be ignored.
27a1dce4 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dce4 [grok-build-0.1] #The question becomes: how much of the glTF structure are you willing to carry just because glTF is
27a1dce4 [grok-build-0.1] #“first class”?
27a1dce4 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dce5 [grok-build-0.1] #3. Binary vs Text Delivery
27a1dce5 [grok-build-0.1] #This affects cleanliness significantly:
27a1dce5 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dce5 [grok-build-0.1] #- JSON glTF + base64 buffer → Easy to send in JSON-RPC, but very wasteful and slow to
27a1dce6 [grok-build-0.1] #decode in GDScript.
27a1dce6 [grok-build-0.1] #- .glb (binary) → Much more efficient, but now you have to parse a binary format manually
27a1dce6 [grok-build-0.1] #in GDScript (header + chunks). This is doable but more error-prone.
27a1dce6 [grok-build-0.1] #- Hybrid → Send a very small glTF JSON that references a separate binary blob you send
27a1dce7 [grok-build-0.1] #alongside it.
27a1dce7 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dce7 [grok-build-0.1] #For per-frame vector data, pure JSON + base64 is usually the worst choice for performance.
27a1dce7 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dce8 [grok-build-0.1] #4. Trade-off Between Standardization and Simplicity
27a1dce8 [grok-build-0.1] #This is the core tension right now:
27a1dce8 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dce8 [grok-build-0.1] #- Pro-glTF: You get a standardized, well-defined buffer layout for coordinates and
27a1dce9 [grok-build-0.1] #attributes. Other tools can potentially generate or consume the same data. It stays consistent with
27a1dce9 [grok-build-0.1] #how you handle real 3D models elsewhere.
27a1dce9 [grok-build-0.1] #- Con-glTF: You pay a structural and parsing tax for something that is fundamentally just a
27a1dce9 [grok-build-0.1] #list of 2D lines. In GDScript, a raw array or a tiny custom binary format would be dramatically
27a1dcea [grok-build-0.1] #simpler and faster to interpret.
27a1dcea [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dcea [grok-build-0.1] #Because you’re doing the interpretation yourself, the “first class” benefit mostly comes from the
27a1dcea [grok-build-0.1] #data model, not from using Godot’s loader.
27a1dceb [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dceb [grok-build-0.1] #5. Other Practical Considerations
27a1dceb [grok-build-0.1] #- Brightness: Easy to carry in `COLOR_0`, but you’ll have to decide whether to send it
27a1dceb [grok-build-0.1] #per-vertex or per-line (duplicating vertices).
27a1dcec [grok-build-0.1] #- Coordinate system: You’ll need to define and enforce a consistent mapping (especially Y
27a1dcec [grok-build-0.1] #direction) since glTF is 3D-oriented by default.
27a1dcec [grok-build-0.1] #- Validation: If you only support a tiny subset, you should be strict about what you
27a1dcec [grok-build-0.1] #accept. Loose parsing can hide problems.
27a1dced [grok-build-0.1] #- Future evolution: If glTF is first-class, you may later want to send more complex vector
27a1dced [grok-build-0.1] #data (curves, multiple layers, etc.). Designing the minimal structure now with that in mind helps.
27a1dced [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dcee [grok-build-0.1] #Realistic Assessment for Your Baseline
27a1dcee [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dcee [grok-build-0.1] #If you go this route, the cleanest outcome is probably:
27a1dcee [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dcee [grok-build-0.1] #- Accept a very restricted form of glTF (or .glb).
27a1dcef [grok-build-0.1] #- Write a small dedicated parser that only looks for the line list buffers.
27a1dcef [grok-build-0.1] #- Treat anything outside that subset as invalid.
27a1dcef [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dcef [grok-build-0.1] #Even then, it will be more code and slower than sending a simple list of lines.
27a1dcf0 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dcf0 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27a1dcf0 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dcf0 [grok-build-0.1] #Would you like to discuss the actual trade-offs between:
27a1dcf1 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dcf1 [grok-build-0.1] #- Using a minimal glTF structure (even if parsed manually), or
27a1dcf1 [grok-build-0.1] #- Using a custom lightweight format that still borrows glTF’s ideas (e.g. typed buffers + metadata)
27a1dcf2 [grok-build-0.1] #without the full glTF container?
27a1dcf2 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1dcf2 [grok-build-0.1] #Or do you want to focus on how the buffer layout itself should look if you commit to glTF?
