27a1e006 [grok-build-0.1] #Since you’re comfortable with JSON parsing and glTF is first-class, let’s focus on what actually
27a1e007 [grok-build-0.1] #matters for representing 2D vector line lists cleanly.
27a1e007 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e007 [grok-build-0.1] #Core Question Reframed
27a1e007 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e008 [grok-build-0.1] #How clean can we make a glTF payload when the receiving side will manually interpret it in GDScript
27a1e008 [grok-build-0.1] #for a simple per-frame vector display?
27a1e008 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e008 [grok-build-0.1] #Recommended Minimal Structure
27a1e009 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e009 [grok-build-0.1] #For your use case, the cleanest approach is to treat glTF almost like a typed buffer schema rather
27a1e009 [grok-build-0.1] #than a full 3D asset.
27a1e009 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e00a [grok-build-0.1] #The most straightforward representation would be:
27a1e00a [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e00a [grok-build-0.1] #- One mesh
27a1e00a [grok-build-0.1] #- One primitive using `mode: 1` (LINES)
27a1e00b [grok-build-0.1] #- `POSITION` as `VEC2` (this matches what you asked about — using a buffer for x,y)
27a1e00b [grok-build-0.1] #- Optionally `COLOR_0` to carry brightness/intensity
27a1e00b [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e00b [grok-build-0.1] #This keeps the structure very flat. You’re basically just using glTF’s accessor + buffer system to
27a1e00c [grok-build-0.1] #describe a list of line segments.
27a1e00c [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e00c [grok-build-0.1] #Key Design Points
27a1e00c [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e00d [grok-build-0.1] #╭─────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
27a1e00d [grok-build-0.1] #│  Aspect                     │  Recommendation                                       │  Why it matters for you                            │
27a1e00d [grok-build-0.1] #├─────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
27a1e00d [grok-build-0.1] #│  **POSITION**               │  Use `VEC2` (not VEC3)                                │  Keeps data minimal and honest for 2D              │
27a1e00e [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Buffer layout**          │  Flat interleaved or separate accessors               │  Simple to walk in GDScript                        │
27a1e00e [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Brightness**             │  `COLOR_0` (as vec4 or just alpha)                    │  Natural place to put intensity                    │
27a1e00e [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Metadata**               │  Keep it extremely small                              │  You don’t want to parse scenes/nodes every frame  │
27a1e00e [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Per-frame nature**       │  Accept that each message is essentially one mesh     │  No need for full scene graphs                     │
27a1e00f [grok-build-0.1] #╰─────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
27a1e00f [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e00f [grok-build-0.1] #Things Worth Deciding Early
27a1e00f [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e010 [grok-build-0.1] #1. How much glTF structure do you actually want to carry?
27a1e010 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Do you want a full valid glTF (with `asset`, `scenes`, `nodes`, etc.) even if you ignore most
27a1e010 [grok-build-0.1] #of it?
27a1e010 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Or are you okay with a very stripped-down glTF that only contains the mesh + buffers?
27a1e011 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e011 [grok-build-0.1] #2. Brightness handling
27a1e011 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Do you want brightness per line (you’d need to duplicate vertices), or per vertex?
27a1e011 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Are you happy using `COLOR_0`, or would you rather put intensity in a custom attribute?
27a1e012 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e012 [grok-build-0.1] #3. Coordinate system
27a1e012 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Will you normalize on the sending side (e.g. -1 to 1), or send raw DVG-style integer
27a1e012 [grok-build-0.1] #coordinates and scale in Godot?
27a1e013 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Y-axis direction needs to be consistent.
27a1e013 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e013 [grok-build-0.1] #4. Format on the wire
27a1e013 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Since JSON parsing is acceptable, are you leaning toward `.gltf` (JSON + base64 buffers) or
27a1e014 [grok-build-0.1] #`.glb` (binary)?
27a1e014 [grok-build-0.1] #   - `.glb` is cleaner as data, but harder to construct manually on the simulator side.
27a1e014 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e014 [grok-build-0.1] #Realistic Cleanliness
27a1e015 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e015 [grok-build-0.1] #If glTF is genuinely first-class in the design, then using a minimal mesh + VEC2 buffer is probably
27a1e015 [grok-build-0.1] #the least ugly way to do it. It’s not the absolute simplest possible format, but it gives you a
27a1e015 [grok-build-0.1] #consistent, documented way to describe line lists that could also be generated or inspected by
27a1e016 [grok-build-0.1] #other tools.
27a1e016 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e016 [grok-build-0.1] #However, because you’re manually interpreting it, you will still be doing a lot of the work
27a1e016 [grok-build-0.1] #yourself (walking accessors, resolving bufferViews, etc.). The "first class" benefit mostly comes
27a1e017 [grok-build-0.1] #from the data model and future compatibility rather than convenience in GDScript.
27a1e017 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e017 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27a1e017 [grok-build-0.1] #
