27a1e492 [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.054 151.2KB 13.99s
27a1e493 [grok-build-0.1] #• Yes, let's talk about it directly.*
27a1e493 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e493 [grok-build-0.1] #You're planning to send glTF-structured data (JSON + binary buffers) over your JSON-RPC
27a1e493 [grok-build-0.1] #style WebSocket connection for per-frame vector lines. Here are the real considerations:
27a1e494 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e494 [grok-build-0.1] #1. How You Actually Transport "JSON + Buffers"
27a1e494 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e494 [grok-build-0.1] #You have a few practical options over WebSocket:
27a1e495 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e495 [grok-build-0.1] #╭─────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────╮
27a1e495 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Approach                       │  Format                                  │  Pros                                       │  Cons                                                       │  Recommendation                               │
27a1e495 [grok-build-0.1] #├─────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────┤
27a1e496 [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Full glTF as JSON**          │  JSON with base64 buffers inside         │  Easy to debug, fits naturally in JSON-RPC  │  ~33% size bloat from base64, verbose                       │  Only okay for small frames                   │
27a1e496 [grok-build-0.1] #│  **.glb as base64**             │  JSON-RPC with one big base64 string     │  Still text-based, one field                │  Same base64 overhead, ugly for large data                  │  Acceptable if frames stay small              │
27a1e496 [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Hybrid (recommended path)**  │  JSON metadata + separate binary buffer  │  Efficient, clean separation                │  More complex on both sides (especially if using JSON-RPC)  │  Usually best                                 │
27a1e496 [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Raw .glb binary**            │  Send as binary WebSocket frame          │  Most efficient                             │  Breaks "pure JSON-RPC" model                               │  Good if you're willing to mix text + binary  │
27a1e497 [grok-build-0.1] #╰─────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────╯
27a1e497 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e497 [grok-build-0.1] #Since you're currently using a JSON-RPC style, the cleanest compromise is usually sending the glTF
27a1e497 [grok-build-0.1] #JSON with base64-encoded buffers inside it, at least to start.
27a1e498 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e498 [grok-build-0.1] #2. Important Practical Issues
27a1e498 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e499 [grok-build-0.1] #- Overhead
27a1e499 [grok-build-0.1] #  Even a minimal glTF has quite a bit of structural JSON (`accessors`, `bufferViews`, `buffers`,
27a1e499 [grok-build-0.1] #`meshes`, etc.). For a vector frame with only a few hundred lines, this can easily be larger than
27a1e499 [grok-build-0.1] #the actual line data.
27a1e49a [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e49a [grok-build-0.1] #- Base64 penalty
27a1e49a [grok-build-0.1] #  If you embed the coordinate buffer as base64, you pay ~33% extra size every frame. For a vector
27a1e49a [grok-build-0.1] #display this can add up quickly.
27a1e49b [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e49b [grok-build-0.1] #- Parsing in GDScript
27a1e49b [grok-build-0.1] #  You will need to:
27a1e49b [grok-build-0.1] #  1. Parse the outer JSON-RPC message
27a1e49b [grok-build-0.1] #  2. Parse the inner glTF JSON
27a1e49c [grok-build-0.1] #  3. Decode base64 (if used)
27a1e49c [grok-build-0.1] #  4. Walk the accessors to extract the `POSITION` buffer
27a1e49c [grok-build-0.1] #  This is doable, but it's more steps than just receiving a flat array of lines.
27a1e49c [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e49d [grok-build-0.1] #- Message framing
27a1e49d [grok-build-0.1] #  How will you distinguish between:
27a1e49d [grok-build-0.1] #  - A normal RPC call (`tick`, `init`, etc.)
27a1e49d [grok-build-0.1] #  - A vector frame (which is really just data, not a command)?
27a1e49e [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e49e [grok-build-0.1] #  You'll probably want a dedicated method like `"vectorFrame"` or `"displayFrame"` rather than
27a1e49e [grok-build-0.1] #trying to reuse `tick`.
27a1e49e [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e49f [grok-build-0.1] #3. Design Thoughts Specific to Your Setup
27a1e49f [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e49f [grok-build-0.1] #- Keep the glTF extremely minimal.
27a1e49f [grok-build-0.1] #  Don't send full scenes, nodes, or materials every frame. Ideally you only send the mesh + one
27a1e4a0 [grok-build-0.1] #primitive + the buffer data. Anything else is noise.
27a1e4a0 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e4a0 [grok-build-0.1] #- Consider separating concerns
27a1e4a1 [grok-build-0.1] #  You could send a small JSON-RPC envelope that contains either:
27a1e4a1 [grok-build-0.1] #  - A full minimal glTF (as string or object), or
27a1e4a1 [grok-build-0.1] #  - Just the raw buffer data + a tiny schema description.
27a1e4a1 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e4a2 [grok-build-0.1] #  Many people in this situation eventually move toward a custom lightweight format that
27a1e4a2 [grok-build-0.1] #looks like glTF's buffer style but without the full wrapper.
27a1e4a2 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e4a2 [grok-build-0.1] #- Versioning
27a1e4a3 [grok-build-0.1] #  If glTF is first-class, you should think about how you'll evolve the format later (adding more
27a1e4a3 [grok-build-0.1] #attributes, different primitive types, etc.). Putting a small version field at the top level is
27a1e4a3 [grok-build-0.1] #worth doing early.
27a1e4a3 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e4a4 [grok-build-0.1] #- Binary vs Text WebSocket
27a1e4a4 [grok-build-0.1] #  WebSockets can send binary frames. If you ever want to drop base64, you could send the actual
27a1e4a4 [grok-build-0.1] #`.glb` bytes as a binary message instead of wrapping everything in JSON. This is worth keeping in
27a1e4a4 [grok-build-0.1] #mind even if you start with JSON.
27a1e4a5 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e4a5 [grok-build-0.1] #4. Realistic Assessment
27a1e4a5 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e4a5 [grok-build-0.1] #For a simple baseline, sending glTF (even minimal) per frame over JSON-RPC is workable, but
27a1e4a6 [grok-build-0.1] #it is not the lightest path. The main value you get is consistency with your "glTF is first-class"
27a1e4a6 [grok-build-0.1] #goal and a well-defined way to describe the line data.
27a1e4a6 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e4a6 [grok-build-0.1] #The friction points will be:
27a1e4a7 [grok-build-0.1] #- Structural bloat
27a1e4a7 [grok-build-0.1] #- Base64 cost (if you use it)
27a1e4a7 [grok-build-0.1] #- Having to manually walk the glTF structure every frame in GDScript
27a1e4a7 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e4a8 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27a1e4a8 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e4a8 [grok-build-0.1] #Would you like to discuss a concrete message shape? For example:
27a1e4a8 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e4a9 [grok-build-0.1] #- What a minimal per-frame glTF message might look like inside your JSON-RPC
27a1e4a9 [grok-build-0.1] #- Whether to embed the buffer as base64 or move toward binary frames
27a1e4a9 [grok-build-0.1] #- How to structure the RPC method itself (`vectorFrame`, etc.)
27a1e4a9 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a1e4aa [grok-build-0.1] #Or do you want to talk about the buffer layout inside the glTF first?
27a1e4aa [grok-build-0.1] #
