27a21365 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a21365 [grok-build-0.1] #Correct Pattern You Should Use
27a21365 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a21365 [grok-build-0.1] #For this kind of per-frame vector display, the cleanest simple approach is:
27a21366 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a21366 [grok-build-0.1] #- `node.gd` (networking): receives the data, converts it, then tells the `Node2D` what to draw.
27a21366 [grok-build-0.1] #- `node2d.gd` (drawing): stores the lines and draws them in `_draw()`.
27a21366 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a21367 [grok-build-0.1] #Here's the minimal correct shape for `node2d.gd`:
27a21367 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a21367 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a21367 [grok-build-0.1] #extends Node2D
27a21368 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a21368 [grok-build-0.1] #var lines: PackedVector2Array = PackedVector2Array()
27a21368 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a21369 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a21369 [grok-build-0.1] #func set_lines(new_lines: PackedVector2Array) -> void:
27a21369 [grok-build-0.1] #	lines = new_lines
27a21369 [grok-build-0.1] #	queue_redraw()           # This is required
27a2136a [grok-build-0.1] #
27a2136a [grok-build-0.1] #
27a2136a [grok-build-0.1] #func _draw() -> void:
27a2136a [grok-build-0.1] #	for i in range(0, lines.size(), 2):
27a2136b [grok-build-0.1] #		var start := lines[i]
27a2136b [grok-build-0.1] #		var end   := lines[i + 1]
27a2136b [grok-build-0.1] #		draw_line(start, end, Color.WHITE, 1.0)
27a2136b [grok-build-0.1] #
27a2136c [grok-build-0.1] #
27a2136c [grok-build-0.1] #Then in `node.gd`, when you receive a `"draw"` message, you would do something like:
27a2136c [grok-build-0.1] #
27a2136c [grok-build-0.1] #
27a2136d [grok-build-0.1] #"draw":
27a2136d [grok-build-0.1] #    var vec_lines = base64_to_vec2_array(params.buffer)   # or however you structure it
27a2136d [grok-build-0.1] #    var node2d = $Node2D
27a2136d [grok-build-0.1] #    node2d.set_lines(vec_lines)
27a2136e [grok-build-0.1] #
27a2136e [grok-build-0.1] #
27a2136e [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27a2136e [grok-build-0.1] #
27a2136e [grok-build-0.1] #Questions before we go further:
27a2136f [grok-build-0.1] #
27a2136f [grok-build-0.1] #1. Do you want `node.gd` to find the `Node2D` using `$Node2D`, or would you prefer to use an
27a2136f [grok-build-0.1] #`@export`?
27a2136f [grok-build-0.1] #
27a21370 [grok-build-0.1] #2. Are you still planning to send raw base64 for now, or do you want to start shaping the message
27a21370 [grok-build-0.1] #toward the glTF structure?
27a21370 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a21370 [grok-build-0.1] #3. Should the `Node2D` clear the previous frame every time new data arrives (which is what
27a21371 [grok-build-0.1] #`set_lines` above does), or do you want any persistence?
27a21371 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a21371 [grok-build-0.1] #Let me know your preference and I’ll give you the matching updates for both scripts.
27a21371 [grok-build-0.1] #
