27a3b965 [nitro@ryzen5] what is a meta float argument?
27a3bb82 [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.001 1.440KB 32.98s
27a3bb82 [grok-build-0.1] #In the context of Godot, a "meta float argument" usually refers to something
27a3bb82 [grok-build-0.1] #specific in Godot’s internal API description (like in `extension_api.json` or when using
27a3bb83 [grok-build-0.1] #GDExtension).
27a3bb83 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb83 [grok-build-0.1] #What it actually means:
27a3bb83 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb84 [grok-build-0.1] #When Godot describes a method or function (for example, for C++ bindings, GDExtension, or editor
27a3bb84 [grok-build-0.1] #introspection), each argument has this structure:
27a3bb84 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb84 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb85 [grok-build-0.1] #{
27a3bb85 [grok-build-0.1] #  "name": "duration",
27a3bb85 [grok-build-0.1] #  "type": "float",
27a3bb86 [grok-build-0.1] #  "meta": "float"
27a3bb86 [grok-build-0.1] #}
27a3bb86 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb86 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb87 [grok-build-0.1] #- `"type": "float"` → tells you it’s a floating-point number.
27a3bb87 [grok-build-0.1] #- `"meta": "float"` → gives more precise information about the float type.
27a3bb87 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb87 [grok-build-0.1] #Why does the "meta" field exist?
27a3bb88 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb88 [grok-build-0.1] #Godot supports two floating-point precisions:
27a3bb88 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb89 [grok-build-0.1] #╭────────────────────────┬──────────────────┬───────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────╮
27a3bb89 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Build Type            │  `"meta"` value  │  Actual C++ type      │  Notes                                   │
27a3bb89 [grok-build-0.1] #├────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼───────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤
27a3bb89 [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Single** precision  │  `"float"`       │  `float`              │  Default in most builds                  │
27a3bb8a [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Double** precision  │  `"double"`      │  `double`             │  Used in scientific/precise simulations  │
27a3bb8a [grok-build-0.1] #╰────────────────────────┴──────────────────┴───────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────╯
27a3bb8a [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb8a [grok-build-0.1] #So when you see `"meta": "float"`, it means the argument expects a normal single-precision float.
27a3bb8b [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb8b [grok-build-0.1] #Common places you’ll see this:
27a3bb8b [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb8b [grok-build-0.1] #- `extension_api.json` (the file Godot generates for GDExtension)
27a3bb8c [grok-build-0.1] #- When inspecting methods via `ClassDB`
27a3bb8c [grok-build-0.1] #- When creating GDExtension bindings in C++
27a3bb8c [grok-build-0.1] #- In some generated binding code or JSON project data
27a3bb8c [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb8d [grok-build-0.1] #Example
27a3bb8d [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb8d [grok-build-0.1] #A method signature might be described like this:
27a3bb8e [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb8e [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb8e [grok-build-0.1] #{
27a3bb8e [grok-build-0.1] #  "name": "set_speed",
27a3bb8f [grok-build-0.1] #  "arguments": [
27a3bb8f [grok-build-0.1] #    {
27a3bb8f [grok-build-0.1] #      "name": "value",
27a3bb8f [grok-build-0.1] #      "type": "float",
27a3bb90 [grok-build-0.1] #      "meta": "float"
27a3bb90 [grok-build-0.1] #    }
27a3bb90 [grok-build-0.1] #  ]
27a3bb91 [grok-build-0.1] #}
27a3bb91 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb91 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb91 [grok-build-0.1] #This means the function takes a float argument.
27a3bb92 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb92 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27a3bb92 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bb92 [grok-build-0.1] #• Would you like me to explain how this differs between single and double precision builds, or
27a3bb93 [grok-build-0.1] #how to handle float arguments when writing GDExtension code?*
27a3bb93 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3bf4b [nitro@ryzen5] is godot upto supporting publishing serious games to steam?
27a3c045 [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.002 3.904KB 15.33s
27a3c046 [grok-build-0.1] #Yes, Godot can be used to publish serious games on Steam, and people are already doing it
27a3c046 [grok-build-0.1] #successfully. However, whether it's a good choice depends on the scale and type of game
27a3c046 [grok-build-0.1] #you're making.
27a3c046 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c047 [grok-build-0.1] #Here's a realistic breakdown:
27a3c047 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c047 [grok-build-0.1] #Current State (Godot 4.x)
27a3c047 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c048 [grok-build-0.1] #╭────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────╮
27a3c048 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Aspect                            │  Status                                       │  Notes                                        │
27a3c048 [grok-build-0.1] #├────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────┤
27a3c049 [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Publishing to Steam**           │  Good                                         │  Many games have shipped successfully         │
27a3c049 [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Steamworks Integration**        │  Decent                                       │  Official GDExtension exists                  │
27a3c049 [grok-build-0.1] #│  **2D Games**                      │  Very strong                                  │  Excellent for serious 2D games               │
27a3c049 [grok-build-0.1] #│  **3D Games**                      │  Acceptable but more work                     │  Improving, but still behind Unreal           │
27a3c04a [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Large scope / Big teams**       │  Possible but harder                          │  Tooling and performance require more effort  │
27a3c04a [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Performance optimization**      │  Improving                                    │  Better in 4.3+, but not automatic            │
27a3c04a [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Stability for commercial use**  │  Good (4.3+)                                  │  Much better than Godot 3                     │
27a3c04a [grok-build-0.1] #╰────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────╯
27a3c04b [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c04b [grok-build-0.1] #Real-World Examples
27a3c04b [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c04b [grok-build-0.1] #Godot has been used for several commercially successful Steam releases, including:
27a3c04c [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c04c [grok-build-0.1] #- Cassette Beasts (highly rated RPG)
27a3c04c [grok-build-0.1] #- Dome Keeper
27a3c04d [grok-build-0.1] #- Brotato
27a3c04d [grok-build-0.1] #- The Cosmic Wheel Sisterhood
27a3c04d [grok-build-0.1] #- The Last Spell
27a3c04d [grok-build-0.1] #- Tails Noir
27a3c04e [grok-build-0.1] #- Godot Wild Jam winners and various mid-sized indie titles
27a3c04e [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c04e [grok-build-0.1] #These are mostly mid-sized indie games rather than big-budget AA or AAA titles.
27a3c04e [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c04f [grok-build-0.1] #Important Limitations
27a3c04f [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c04f [grok-build-0.1] #Here are the main things to consider if you're aiming for a "serious" game:
27a3c04f [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c050 [grok-build-0.1] #- 3D is still the weakest area: If you're making a large, complex 3D game (especially with
27a3c050 [grok-build-0.1] #heavy graphics or open worlds), Unreal Engine is generally more mature and less painful.
27a3c050 [grok-build-0.1] #- Team size & tooling: Godot lacks some of the advanced collaboration and large-project
27a3c051 [grok-build-0.1] #tools that Unity and Unreal have.
27a3c051 [grok-build-0.1] #- Performance: You often need to do more manual optimization, especially in 3D or with
27a3c051 [grok-build-0.1] #complex gameplay systems.
27a3c051 [grok-build-0.1] #- Steamworks: The integration works, but it's not as seamless as Unity's or Unreal's
27a3c052 [grok-build-0.1] #built-in plugins. You'll be using a GDExtension.
27a3c052 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c052 [grok-build-0.1] #Recommendation
27a3c052 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c052 [grok-build-0.1] #╭───────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────╮
27a3c052 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Type of Game                         │  Is Godot Suitable?      │  Recommendation          │
27a3c053 [grok-build-0.1] #├───────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┤
27a3c053 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Serious 2D game                      │  Yes                     │  Good choice             │
27a3c053 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Mid-sized 3D game                    │  Yes, with caveats       │  Viable                  │
27a3c053 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Large / high-budget 3D game          │  Risky                   │  Consider Unreal         │
27a3c054 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Multiplayer game (complex)           │  Possible                │  Requires extra work     │
27a3c054 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Solo or small team serious game      │  Yes                     │  Very reasonable choice  │
27a3c054 [grok-build-0.1] #╰───────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────╯
27a3c055 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c055 [grok-build-0.1] #Bottom Line
27a3c055 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c055 [grok-build-0.1] #Godot is mature enough for many serious commercial games, especially 2D or medium-scope 3D
27a3c056 [grok-build-0.1] #titles. It is no longer just a "hobby engine." However, it still requires more technical effort in
27a3c056 [grok-build-0.1] #some areas compared to Unity or Unreal if you're pushing for high production values or very large
27a3c056 [grok-build-0.1] #scope.
27a3c056 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c057 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27a3c057 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c057 [grok-build-0.1] #• Quick question to give you a better answer:*
27a3c057 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c058 [grok-build-0.1] #What kind of game are you planning to make? (2D or 3D? Singleplayer or multiplayer? Rough
27a3c058 [grok-build-0.1] #scope/team size?)
27a3c058 [grok-build-0.1] #
27a3c059 [grok-build-0.1] #This will help me tell you whether Godot is realistically a good fit for your case.
