27bd6d0a [deepseek-v4-flash] #There’s no explicit question here, so I compared the trace with `romulon.cpp` and noted the things
27bd6d0a [deepseek-v4-flash] #that stand out.
27bd6d0a [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d0a [deepseek-v4-flash] #The IDs line up as expected:
27bd6d0b [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d0b [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `297225` through `301320` are exactly 4096 `"sample"` lines from `sampleRomPio()`.
27bd6d0b [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `301321` is `"capture":"done 4096 samples"`.
27bd6d0b [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The two `"pin"` lines after that come from the normal `runShell()` loop, not from the capture
27bd6d0c [deepseek-v4-flash] #buffer.
27bd6d0c [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d0c [deepseek-v4-flash] #Observations / possible issues:
27bd6d0c [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d0d [deepseek-v4-flash] #1. `vidbit.info` is requested but never answered.
27bd6d0d [deepseek-v4-flash] #   `rpccoms` sends `{"method":"vidbit.info","id":1}`, but `updateRPC()` only handles `rtc.set`,
27bd6d0d [deepseek-v4-flash] #`vidbit.set`, and `vidbit.keys`. There is no response with `"id":1` in the trace.
27bd6d0d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d0e [deepseek-v4-flash] #2. The initial `"status&3 panic":"2"` is probably not a real panic.
27bd6d0e [deepseek-v4-flash] #   `runShell()` sends that whenever `cdcStatus()` has bits `0` or `1` set. At startup `cdcStatus()`
27bd6d0e [deepseek-v4-flash] #returned `2`, which is often just CDC line-state/DTR activity. Treating that as a panic explains
27bd6d0e [deepseek-v4-flash] #the unrelated out-of-band id `297224` before any normal RPC response.
27bd6d0f [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d0f [deepseek-v4-flash] #3. `vidbit.keys` emits invalid JSON for newline/control input.
27bd6d0f [deepseek-v4-flash] #   The code does:
27bd6d0f [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d0f [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d0f [deepseek-v4-flash] #   out << "\"result\":\"" << s << "\""
27bd6d10 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d10 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d10 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   without JSON escaping. When `text` is `"\n"`, the actual newline appears inside the JSON string,
27bd6d10 [deepseek-v4-flash] #which is why the trace shows:
27bd6d11 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d11 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d11 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   [RX] line:{"jsonrpc":"2.0","result":"
27bd6d11 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   [RX] line:","id":4}
27bd6d12 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d12 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d12 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   That is split into two lines and is not valid JSON.
27bd6d12 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d13 [deepseek-v4-flash] #4. The one-shot PIO capture is started from the shell loop, not from an RPC method.
27bd6d13 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   `sampleRomPio(0,false)` only runs once on the first bus-change after `connected` becomes true.
27bd6d13 [deepseek-v4-flash] #After that, `first` is `false`, so there is no way to request another capture without a
27bd6d13 [deepseek-v4-flash] #reset/reconnect unless a new RPC command is added.
27bd6d14 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd6d14 [deepseek-v4-flash] #If you tell me what symptom you’re chasing — startup panic, missing `info` response, capture
27bd6d14 [deepseek-v4-flash] #timing, or JSON parsing — I can narrow it down further.
27bd6d14 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
