27bd92a9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.075 406.5KB 8.08s
27bd92a9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #It doesn’t fry valid UTF‑8 — it just copies every byte through. If the input `std::string` contains
27bd92a9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #UTF‑8 text, the bytes are already multi‑byte sequences; the loop sees each byte in turn and pushes
27bd92a9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #it into `out`. The sequence is preserved exactly.
27bd92aa [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd92aa [deepseek-v4-flash] #For example, the input `"héllo"` is the bytes `68 C3 A9 6C 6C 6F`. The function will output `"h`
27bd92aa [deepseek-v4-flash] #then `C3` then `A9` then `llo` — the same bytes inside quotes. That’s still the same UTF‑8 string.
27bd92aa [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd92ab [deepseek-v4-flash] #JSON itself allows raw Unicode characters in strings when the document is encoded as UTF‑8. So this
27bd92ab [deepseek-v4-flash] #output is valid.
27bd92ab [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd92ab [deepseek-v4-flash] #The only case it would produce invalid JSON is if the input contains invalid UTF‑8 byte sequences.
27bd92ac [deepseek-v4-flash] #If your source strings are always valid UTF‑8, you’re fine.
27bd92ac [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27bd92ac [deepseek-v4-flash] #If you meant you wanted non‑ASCII escaped as `\uXXXX` so the output is pure ASCII, that’s a
27bd92ac [deepseek-v4-flash] #different requirement — and that’s what the earlier more complex code could do. But you said you
27bd92ad [deepseek-v4-flash] #were happy leaving Unicode unescaped, so the simple version does exactly that.
27bd92ad [deepseek-v4-flash] #
