27c89423 [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.071 53.90KB 402.88s
27c89423 [deepseek-v4-flash] #I read this as a bus-capture/trace dump, so the first thing I’d do is filter before trying to
27c89423 [deepseek-v4-flash] #decode instructions.
27c89423 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c89423 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Timestamp deltas
27c89423 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c89424 [deepseek-v4-flash] #The PIO timestamp is a countdown in `isr`, and it is only decremented on the no-change
27c89424 [deepseek-v4-flash] #path. On the `changed` path the current tick is pushed, but not advanced before the next compare.
27c89424 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c89424 [deepseek-v4-flash] #So consecutive rows with the same `t` are not actually “zero time apart.” They mean the pin field
27c89425 [deepseek-v4-flash] #changed on consecutive PIO sample opportunities without a stable sample in between. That is exactly
27c89425 [deepseek-v4-flash] #the kind of settling/jitter burst you are seeing.
27c89425 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c89425 [deepseek-v4-flash] #For a first filter in C++:
27c89425 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c89425 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Group samples by identical, wrap-aware timestamp.
27c89426 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Keep only the last sample in each group.
27c89426 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The last sample is the one that appears to have settled, because the next sample with a different
27c89426 [deepseek-v4-flash] #timestamp means there was finally a quiet/no-change interval.
27c89426 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c89426 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Also, `pinStamp` is only printing 4 hex digits of a 32-bit tick. That will make the countdown look
27c89427 [deepseek-v4-flash] #non-monotonic when the low 16 bits wrap. The first few timestamps in the trace already look suspect
27c89427 [deepseek-v4-flash] #(`9d36`, `adb2`, `219a`, `09ba`, then `c740`). For delta work, keep the full 32-bit tick and use
27c89427 [deepseek-v4-flash] #modulo subtraction.
27c89427 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c89428 [deepseek-v4-flash] #PIO-side jitter detection/filtering
27c89428 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c89428 [deepseek-v4-flash] #PIO can detect repeated transitions, but the current PIO program records them all. A PIO debounce
27c89428 [deepseek-v4-flash] #is possible in principle:
27c89429 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c89429 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- On the first change, do not immediately push.
27c89429 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Wait one more sample period and compare again.
27c89429 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Only push if the same value is still present.
27c89429 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8942a [deepseek-v4-flash] #That will reject very short single-sample bounces, but it costs extra PIO state and makes the
27c8942a [deepseek-v4-flash] #timestamp semantics trickier. The easier path is probably to leave PIO as a fast edge-catcher and
27c8942a [deepseek-v4-flash] #filter bursts in C++/host code using the timestamp grouping above.
27c8942a [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8942a [deepseek-v4-flash] #One important PIO detail: the state machine appears to be running continuously before DMA starts,
27c8942a [deepseek-v4-flash] #so the RX FIFO may already contain old events. Clear the FIFO, or restart/reset the PWM, before
27c8942b [deepseek-v4-flash] #starting each capture. Otherwise the first samples in the trace can be stale.
27c8942b [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8942b [deepseek-v4-flash] #Watch out for undecoded GPIO bits
27c8942b [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8942b [deepseek-v4-flash] #PIO `pins` samples all 32 GPIOs, including GPIO25 if the green LED is being blinked. The decoded
27c8942c [deepseek-v4-flash] #output in `pinStamp` does not show GPIO25, so an LED transition can produce a sample that looks
27c8942c [deepseek-v4-flash] #like an exact duplicate of the previous bus state.
27c8942c [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8942c [deepseek-v4-flash] #If the LED/blink function is active during capture, mask those unused bits out of the
27c8942d [deepseek-v4-flash] #change-detection logic or stop blinking while capturing. Otherwise you will generate spurious
27c8942d [deepseek-v4-flash] #samples and odd timestamp groups.
27c8942d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8942d [deepseek-v4-flash] #6502 opcode/operand identification
27c8942d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8942e [deepseek-v4-flash] #There is no reliable “single opcode bit” that tells you whether a fetched byte is an opcode or
27c8942e [deepseek-v4-flash] #operand, or how many following bytes belong to the instruction. The 6502 opcode map does not encode
27c8942e [deepseek-v4-flash] #instruction length in one simple bit.
27c8942e [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8942e [deepseek-v4-flash] #The reliable way to get instruction boundaries is to use the 6502 `SYNC` output. SYNC is high on
27c8942f [deepseek-v4-flash] #the opcode-fetch cycle. If you can bring SYNC into a spare GPIO and include it in the captured pin
27c8942f [deepseek-v4-flash] #word, then:
27c8942f [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8942f [deepseek-v4-flash] #- A `sync=1` sample marks a new instruction fetch.
27c8942f [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The data byte on that sample is the opcode.
27c89430 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Follow it with the operand bytes based on a proper 6502 opcode-length table.
27c89430 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c89430 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Without SYNC, you can sometimes start from a known code boundary and decode forward, but any
27c89430 [deepseek-v4-flash] #interrupt or unknown boundary will desynchronise the stream. The bus trace alone cannot reliably
27c89431 [deepseek-v4-flash] #distinguish an opcode fetch from an operand/data byte fetch.
27c89431 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c89431 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Build/code issues
27c89431 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c89431 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Two obvious issues in the shared source:
27c89432 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c89432 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `snoop.cpp` creates a config variable, but then the `sm_config_set_*` calls and `pio_sm_init()`
27c89432 [deepseek-v4-flash] #use `&c`. That will not compile unless `c` is declared or renamed to the config variable.
27c89432 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `stringify()` in `romulon.cpp` uses `text` but does not take a `text` argument, while `main()`
27c89432 [deepseek-v4-flash] #calls `stringify(picoTitle)`. The signature needs to accept the string parameter.
27c89432 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c89432 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Once the trace is filtered to settled bus states, and SYNC/opcode-length table is available, this
27c89433 [deepseek-v4-flash] #should become much more readable as a real 6502 instruction trace.
27c89433 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
