27c8e20c [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.034 72.50KB 142.99s
27c8e20c [deepseek-v4-flash] #The short answer is:
27c8e20c [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e20c [deepseek-v4-flash] #• Don’t try to deduce SYNC from the captured address/data trace. Wire SYNC in instead.*
27c8e20c [deepseek-v4-flash] #There is no reliable bus-only signature that says “this byte is an opcode, not an operand or data
27c8e20d [deepseek-v4-flash] #byte.”
27c8e20d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e20d [deepseek-v4-flash] #The good news: you don’t need another spare GPIO. You already have one that is logically redundant
27c8e20d [deepseek-v4-flash] #— the ROM `CS` line.
27c8e20d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e20e [deepseek-v4-flash] #Why `CS` is redundant on the 1541
27c8e20e [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e20e [deepseek-v4-flash] #The `CS` pin on the 2364 ROM socket is not an independent CPU signal. It is just the 1541’s address
27c8e20e [deepseek-v4-flash] #decoder selecting one ROM socket.
27c8e20e [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e20f [deepseek-v4-flash] #Your README confirms the two fixed windows:
27c8e20f [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e20f [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Low DOS ROM: `C000–DFFF`
27c8e20f [deepseek-v4-flash] #- High DOS/Kernal ROM: `E000–FFFF`
27c8e20f [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e210 [deepseek-v4-flash] #If you are already sampling enough address bits to reconstruct the full 16-bit 6502 address, then
27c8e210 [deepseek-v4-flash] #you can compute whether that ROM socket is selected:
27c8e210 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e210 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e210 [deepseek-v4-flash] #ROM selected  <=>  address is inside the window of that ROM
27c8e210 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e211 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e211 [deepseek-v4-flash] #So `CS` is derived information, not a critical extra bit to sample. You can free that GPIO and
27c8e211 [deepseek-v4-flash] #connect it to the 6502’s SYNC output instead.
27c8e211 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e212 [deepseek-v4-flash] #What to change
27c8e212 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e212 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Move the wire currently on GPIO26 from the ROM socket `CS` pin to the 6502 `SYNC` pin.
27c8e212 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Keep all address/data/RW wiring as it is.
27c8e213 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- In `pinStamp()`, treat GPIO26 as `sync`, not `cs`.
27c8e213 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- If you still want to print or filter by ROM selection, derive it from the 16-bit address after
27c8e213 [deepseek-v4-flash] #decoding.
27c8e213 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e214 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Why SYNC is the only sane answer
27c8e214 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e214 [deepseek-v4-flash] #6502 SYNC is asserted during the opcode fetch cycle. That is the clean marker for:
27c8e214 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e214 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e214 [deepseek-v4-flash] #this data byte is an opcode
27c8e215 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e215 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e215 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Without it, operand bytes and data bytes look exactly like opcode fetches on the bus. Every
27c8e215 [deepseek-v4-flash] #possible byte value is also a valid 6502 opcode if you include undocumented opcodes, so a heuristic
27c8e215 [deepseek-v4-flash] #based on “does this byte look like a valid opcode?” cannot work in general.
27c8e216 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e216 [deepseek-v4-flash] #You can get away without SYNC only if you are doing offline disassembly from a known reset vector.
27c8e216 [deepseek-v4-flash] #That is a different operation: you reconstruct a listing from the ROM image, not from the live bus
27c8e216 [deepseek-v4-flash] #trace. And even then, one branch or interrupt that you mispredict will desynchronise the rest of
27c8e217 [deepseek-v4-flash] #the stream.
27c8e217 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e217 [deepseek-v4-flash] #With SYNC wired to GPIO26, each trace row can carry:
27c8e217 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e217 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e218 [deepseek-v4-flash] #address data R/W S timestamp
27c8e218 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e218 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e218 [deepseek-v4-flash] #where `S=1` means “instruction fetch starts here.”
27c8e218 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e219 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Practical suggestion while rewiring
27c8e219 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e219 [deepseek-v4-flash] #The trace you posted has a lot of duplicate/transitional samples. Before doing instruction-boundary
27c8e219 [deepseek-v4-flash] #analysis, filter the samples so that each stable bus state is represented once. SYNC itself will
27c8e21a [deepseek-v4-flash] #then mark much cleaner instruction starts.
27c8e21a [deepseek-v4-flash] #
27c8e21a [deepseek-v4-flash] #Also, the current code drop still has the old `stringify()` signature/argument mismatch:
27c8e21a [deepseek-v4-flash] #`updateRPC()` calls `stringify(s)` while `stringify()` is declared with no parameter and references
27c8e21b [deepseek-v4-flash] #an undefined `text`. That source file won’t compile as pasted.
27c8e21b [deepseek-v4-flash] #
