27c921cb [gemini-3.8-flash] #Hello!
27c921cc [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c921cc [gemini-3.8-flash] #From a hardware and transport perspective, RP2040 FIFOs (both PIO FIFOs and DMA queues) are
27c921cc [gemini-3.8-flash] #strictly first-in, first-out. Hardware will not swap or transpose entries in transit.
27c921cc [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c921cd [gemini-3.8-flash] #However, captured data can frequently appear out of order or corrupted due to several
27c921cd [gemini-3.8-flash] #subtle software and state issues:
27c921cd [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c921cd [gemini-3.8-flash] #1. Phase Shift / Word Misalignment (Most Common)
27c921ce [gemini-3.8-flash] #In your capture routine, each sample consists of two 32-bit words:
27c921ce [gemini-3.8-flash] #• `captureBuffer[i * 2]` (tick)
27c921ce [gemini-3.8-flash] #• `captureBuffer[i * 2 + 1]` (pins)
27c921ce [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c921cf [gemini-3.8-flash] #If the PIO or DMA gets out of phase by a single 32-bit word, the interpretation alternates: the
27c921cf [gemini-3.8-flash] #code will read pins as ticks and ticks as pins. This leads to erratic timestamp calculations that
27c921cf [gemini-3.8-flash] #mimic severe out-of-order events. This often happens if:
27c921cf [gemini-3.8-flash] #• A previous capture was aborted halfway through a tick/pin push pair.
27c921d0 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• The PIO RX FIFO was not completely drained or cleared before arming DMA.
27c921d0 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• The PIO state machine began execution and pushed one word before the DMA channel was triggered.
27c921d0 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c921d0 [gemini-3.8-flash] #2. FIFO Overrun (Dropped Samples)
27c921d1 [gemini-3.8-flash] #If the host CPU or DMA cannot drain the PIO FIFO quickly enough and the PIO pushes non-blockingly,
27c921d1 [gemini-3.8-flash] #entries are discarded. If an odd number of words is dropped, this immediately creates the
27c921d1 [gemini-3.8-flash] #1-word phase shift described above for all subsequent samples.
27c921d1 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c921d2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #3. Down-Counting Timers
27c921d2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #If the PIO generates `tick` values using an internal loop counter (for example, `mov x, ...`
27c921d2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #followed by `jmp x--`), the counter decrements rather than increments. If interpreted directly as
27c921d2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #elapsed time without subtracting from a base value, subsequent samples will show decreasing
27c921d3 [gemini-3.8-flash] #timestamps.
27c921d3 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c921d3 [gemini-3.8-flash] #4. Leftover Data Across Sessions
27c921d4 [gemini-3.8-flash] #If `beginSnoop()` does not explicitly restart the PIO state machine clock divider and flush the RX
27c921d4 [gemini-3.8-flash] #FIFO (`pio_sm_clear_fifos`), leftover data from an earlier run will be DMA'd first, creating an
27c921d4 [gemini-3.8-flash] #apparent time jump or interleaving old and new captures.
27c921d4 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c921d5 [gemini-3.8-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27c921d5 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c921d5 [gemini-3.8-flash] #To diagnose this, checking whether timestamps are jumping erratically between pin masks and actual
27c921d5 [gemini-3.8-flash] #tick numbers is usually the quickest way to confirm a 1-word alignment slip.
27c921d6 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
