27c92c06 [gemini-3.8-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.018 19.64KB 12.63s
27c92c06 [gemini-3.8-flash] #Several interesting aspects and subtle details stand out across the shared files:
27c92c06 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c92c07 [gemini-3.8-flash] #1. Full 16-bit Address Decoding via CPU Tap
27c92c07 [gemini-3.8-flash] #Because the 2364 ROM socket (UB3) only routes A_0 through A_12, the additional lines for A_13 \dots
27c92c07 [gemini-3.8-flash] #A_15 and R/\overlineW routed directly from the 6502 pins allow full 64\,\textKB bus monitoring
27c92c07 [gemini-3.8-flash] #rather than just observing local ROM chip accesses. Reversing A_0 \dots A_7 in `pinStamp()` with
27c92c08 [gemini-3.8-flash] #`reverse8()` aligns neatly with the physical pin order along that side of the 2364 socket.
27c92c08 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c92c08 [gemini-3.8-flash] #2. PIO State Machine (`snoop.pio`)
27c92c08 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• State Preservation Across Pushes: Using register `x` as a temporary holder for the tick
27c92c09 [gemini-3.8-flash] #counter before pushing to the RX FIFO works around the hardware behavior where manual `push`
27c92c09 [gemini-3.8-flash] #instructions clear the ISR.
27c92c09 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Sampling Rate: The loop executes a `nop [8]` before reading the pins, giving a consistent
27c92c09 [gemini-3.8-flash] #sampling cadence while monitoring bus transactions. If higher bus frequencies or narrow transition
27c92c0a [gemini-3.8-flash] #windows ever need to be captured, that delay can be adjusted down to match the 1541's \approx
27c92c0a [gemini-3.8-flash] #1\,\textMHz bus clock \phi_2.
27c92c0a [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c92c0b [gemini-3.8-flash] #3. Implementation Details in `snoop.cpp`
27c92c0b [gemini-3.8-flash] #A few items in the C++ layer may warrant a quick check depending on your build configuration:
27c92c0b [gemini-3.8-flash] #• `fifo` Scope: In `beginSnoop()`, `fifo` is assigned directly without a local type
27c92c0b [gemini-3.8-flash] #declaration (`fifo = &snoop_pio->rxf[snoop_sm];`). Unless defined in a shared header or globally,
27c92c0c [gemini-3.8-flash] #this may require an explicit pointer type.
27c92c0c [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Unused Configuration Structure: `initSnoop()` initializes `pio_sm_config c` and then
27c92c0c [gemini-3.8-flash] #immediately creates a second `pio_sm_config config` that is never applied to the state machine.
27c92c0c [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Pin Direction Setup: While `pio_gpio_init()` assigns the pad control over to the PIO
27c92c0d [gemini-3.8-flash] #instance, explicitly calling `pio_sm_set_consecutive_pindirs()` ensures the state machine input
27c92c0d [gemini-3.8-flash] #mapping is explicitly configured for input.
27c92c0d [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c92c0d [gemini-3.8-flash] #4. Hardware Expansion (SWD Tail)
27c92c0e [gemini-3.8-flash] #The note in the documentation regarding using the SWD pads for additional GPIO on the RP2040 (GPIO
27c92c0e [gemini-3.8-flash] #30 and 31) is a practical choice once the 28 standard header pins are fully allocated to the bus
27c92c0e [gemini-3.8-flash] #lines.
27c92c0e [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c92c0f [gemini-3.8-flash] #If you would like closer analysis on the DMA pacing, RPC packet serialization, or the PIO
27c92c0f [gemini-3.8-flash] #transition timing relative to the 6502 clock phases, please let me know.
27c92c0f [gemini-3.8-flash] #
