27c95777 [gemini-3.8-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.015 32.00KB 10.67s
27c95777 [gemini-3.8-flash] #1. The Decrementing Tick Order and Identical Timestamps
27c95777 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c95778 [gemini-3.8-flash] #The tick values are actually arriving in chronological order:
27c95778 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c95778 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Countdown Counter: In `snoop.pio`, the tick is seeded with `mov isr, ~null`
27c95778 [gemini-3.8-flash] #(`0xFFFFFFFF`) and decremented using `jmp x--, save`. Higher numerical values represent earlier
27c95779 [gemini-3.8-flash] #points in time. Therefore, `0xed32f9` occurred before `0xed32f3` by exactly 6 loop iterations.
27c95779 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Identical Timestamps on Rapid Changes: In `snoop.pio`, when the state machine branches to
27c95779 [gemini-3.8-flash] #`changed`, it pushes the current `isr` and jumps directly back to `loop` without executing the `jmp
27c95779 [gemini-3.8-flash] #x--, save` decrement step. If pins are settling and register another change immediately on the
27c9577a [gemini-3.8-flash] #subsequent pass, `isr` retains the exact same value.
27c9577a [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c9577a [gemini-3.8-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27c9577a [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c9577b [gemini-3.8-flash] #2. Detecting Settling and Jitter
27c9577b [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c9577b [gemini-3.8-flash] #On a 6502 bus, address lines propagate through internal decoders and external buffers with varying
27c9577b [gemini-3.8-flash] #propagation delays (often skewing across a 30–100 ns window).
27c9577c [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c9577c [gemini-3.8-flash] #The trace reflects this: `4c47` versus `4847` is bit 10 toggling while the bus stabilizes.
27c9577c [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c9577c [gemini-3.8-flash] #Handling in PIO (Hardware Filtering)
27c9577d [gemini-3.8-flash] #If you do not have a free pin to sample \phi_2 directly:
27c9577d [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Instead of immediately pushing upon the first detection of `x != y`, the PIO routine can execute
27c9577d [gemini-3.8-flash] #a short settling delay (for example, 150–250 ns).
27c9577d [gemini-3.8-flash] #• After the delay, it samples `pins` again. If the bus has stabilized, it pushes that single
27c9577e [gemini-3.8-flash] #consolidated value and ignores the intermediate transitions.
27c9577e [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c9577e [gemini-3.8-flash] #Handling in C++ (Software Filtering)
27c9577e [gemini-3.8-flash] #Because bus settling occurs across single-digit tick deltas:
27c9577f [gemini-3.8-flash] #• A reusable filter function can process the raw capture buffer into an intermediate settled event
27c9577f [gemini-3.8-flash] #buffer.
27c9577f [gemini-3.8-flash] #• When adjacent samples fall within a settling threshold (\Delta t \le \textsettle\_limit), earlier
27c9577f [gemini-3.8-flash] #transient samples are discarded, keeping only the final state before the bus went quiet.
27c95780 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c95780 [gemini-3.8-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27c95780 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c95780 [gemini-3.8-flash] #3. Inferring the 6502 `SYNC` Line in C++
27c95781 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c95781 [gemini-3.8-flash] #Without a physical pin for 6502 pin 7 (`SYNC`), you can infer opcode fetch cycles by running a
27c95781 [gemini-3.8-flash] #lightweight bus cycle tracker:
27c95782 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27c95782 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Initial Synchronization: The 6502 boot sequence always reads the reset vector from `FFFC`
27c95782 [gemini-3.8-flash] #and `FFFD` (with R/\overlineW = 1). Once the initial program counter (PC) is loaded, the subsequent
27c95782 [gemini-3.8-flash] #read cycle at that target address is guaranteed to be an opcode fetch (`SYNC = 1`).
27c95783 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Cycle State Machine: Every 6502 opcode has a deterministic cycle length and memory access
27c95783 [gemini-3.8-flash] #sequence (e.g., fetch opcode \rightarrow fetch operand low \rightarrow fetch operand high
27c95783 [gemini-3.8-flash] #\rightarrow internal operation / memory access).
27c95783 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• PC Tracking: By tracking the decoded instruction, the state machine knows how many bus
27c95784 [gemini-3.8-flash] #cycles to wait before the next opcode fetch occurs. Branches, jumps, interrupts, and subroutines
27c95784 [gemini-3.8-flash] #update the expected PC, keeping the synthetic `SYNC` flag aligned.
27c95784 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
