27ca7643 [gemini-3.8-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.011 21.23KB 7.17s
27ca7643 [gemini-3.8-flash] #1. Bus Settling and Jitter in PIO
27ca7644 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27ca7644 [gemini-3.8-flash] #The multiple records observed with identical or adjacent timestamps are the result of signal skew
27ca7644 [gemini-3.8-flash] #across physical traces and logic gate propagation delays. Because the PIO loop executes at system
27ca7644 [gemini-3.8-flash] #clock speed (typically 125 MHz to 133 MHz, or ~7.5–8 ns per cycle), it captures the intermediate
27ca7645 [gemini-3.8-flash] #settling states as the 6502 address and control lines transition.
27ca7645 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27ca7645 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• PIO-side Debounce / Delay:
27ca7645 [gemini-3.8-flash] #  Rather than pushing immediately upon `x != y`, the PIO program can insert a brief settling delay.
27ca7646 [gemini-3.8-flash] #After detecting an initial change, the state machine can execute a fixed wait loop or delay counter
27ca7646 [gemini-3.8-flash] #(for example, 40–80 ns) and then sample the bus once more before pushing to the FIFO.
27ca7646 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Clock Qualification (Φ2):
27ca7646 [gemini-3.8-flash] #  On the 6502 bus (running at ~1 MHz in the 1541), address lines become stable approximately
27ca7647 [gemini-3.8-flash] #100–300 ns after the rising edge of phase 1, and read data is valid near the falling edge of phase
27ca7647 [gemini-3.8-flash] #2 (\Phi_2). If one of the unused GPIOs or an external latch is tied to \Phi_2, sampling can be
27ca7647 [gemini-3.8-flash] #triggered strictly on the falling edge of \Phi_2 using `wait 1 gpio ...` / `wait 0 gpio ...`,
27ca7647 [gemini-3.8-flash] #entirely bypassing settling transients.
27ca7648 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27ca7648 [gemini-3.8-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27ca7648 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27ca7648 [gemini-3.8-flash] #2. Settling Filter in C++
27ca7649 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27ca7649 [gemini-3.8-flash] #If bus capture remains continuous without PIO qualification, filtering is straightforward during
27ca7649 [gemini-3.8-flash] #post-processing or stream consumption:
27ca7649 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27ca764a [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Cluster Windowing:
27ca764a [gemini-3.8-flash] #  Because the 1541 bus runs at 1 MHz (1\,μ\texts per bus cycle), any series of raw samples arriving
27ca764a [gemini-3.8-flash] #within a narrow threshold (e.g., within 10–20 PIO clock ticks of each other) represent the same
27ca764a [gemini-3.8-flash] #transition event.
27ca764b [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Last-Wins Strategy:
27ca764b [gemini-3.8-flash] #  A dedicated filter function can group samples by timestamp delta. For any cluster where
27ca764b [gemini-3.8-flash] #consecutive timestamps differ by less than the settling threshold, all transient intermediate
27ca764b [gemini-3.8-flash] #states can be discarded, keeping only the final sample in the cluster as the resolved cycle value.
27ca764c [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27ca764c [gemini-3.8-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
27ca764c [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27ca764c [gemini-3.8-flash] #3. Inferring the SYNC Signal in Software
27ca764c [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27ca764d [gemini-3.8-flash] #Without the physical SYNC pin (6502 pin 7) connected to a GPIO:
27ca764d [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27ca764d [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Vector Seeding:
27ca764e [gemini-3.8-flash] #  Following a reset sequence, the 6502 executes seven internal cycles, reading the reset vector
27ca764e [gemini-3.8-flash] #from `FFFC` and `FFFD`. The word retrieved is the program counter (PC) for the first instruction.
27ca764e [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Instruction Decoder State Machine:
27ca764e [gemini-3.8-flash] #  By implementing a small table lookup based on standard 6502 instruction lengths and addressing
27ca764f [gemini-3.8-flash] #modes, the C++ processor can track instruction boundaries:
27ca764f [gemini-3.8-flash] #  1. The byte read at the current PC is flagged as an opcode fetch (`SYNC = 1`).
27ca764f [gemini-3.8-flash] #  2. The table yields the expected operand byte count and instruction cycle behavior.
27ca764f [gemini-3.8-flash] #  3. Subsequent bus reads within that instruction window are flagged as operand/data fetches (`SYNC
27ca7650 [gemini-3.8-flash] #= 0`).
27ca7650 [gemini-3.8-flash] #  4. The internal PC advances according to the instruction rules, including tracking relative
27ca7650 [gemini-3.8-flash] #branches and absolute jumps, keeping the software in lockstep with the hardware execution stream.
27ca7650 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27ca7651 [gemini-3.8-flash] #Let me know if you would like dedicated PIO settling routines or the C++ bus-filtering and
27ca7651 [gemini-3.8-flash] #instruction-tracking functions written out.
27ca7651 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
