27f38db1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 kimi-k2.7-code 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.040 21.18KB 144.36s
27f38db1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #Hi Simon / nitrologic,
27f38db1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38db1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #Thanks for sharing the latest romulon files. A few observations on what’s standing out now versus
27f38db2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #earlier versions, plus some things worth tightening:
27f38db2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38db2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #• What’s new / different*
27f38db2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #- The PIO-based capture path is now the primary sampler (`snoopRom`), replacing or superseding the
27f38db2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #older `sampleRom` bit-bash loop. That’s a big step — much higher bandwidth and lower CPU
27f38db3 [kimi-k2.7-code] #involvement.
27f38db3 [kimi-k2.7-code] #- Timestamps are now derived from the PIO countdown counter and rendered as `epoch - tick`, giving
27f38db3 [kimi-k2.7-code] #positive monotonic deltas. The trace excerpts in `thoughts.txt` look coherent: roughly 80–90 ns per
27f38db3 [kimi-k2.7-code] #count at 125 MHz.
27f38db4 [kimi-k2.7-code] #- The JSON-RPC surface has been simplified to a small set of methods (`rtc.set`, `vidbit.set`,
27f38db4 [kimi-k2.7-code] #`vidbit.keys`) and a result-pushing channel for samples.
27f38db4 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38db4 [kimi-k2.7-code] #• Things I’d flag*
27f38db5 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38db5 [kimi-k2.7-code] #1. `int id = 2e5 + (sendCount++);` — `2e5` is a `double` literal. It happens to truncate to
27f38db5 [kimi-k2.7-code] #`200000`, but it’s a needless trap. Use `200000`.
27f38db5 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38db6 [kimi-k2.7-code] #2. `memcmp(_nvm, nvm_header, sizeof(nonVolatile))` compares 24 bytes starting from a pointer to the
27f38db6 [kimi-k2.7-code] #string literal `"PICOTOOL"`. The literal is only 9 bytes including the NUL, so this reads past it,
27f38db6 [kimi-k2.7-code] #and it also compares padding/flags. Better: `memcmp(_nvm->header, nvm_header, 8) == 0`.
27f38db6 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38db7 [kimi-k2.7-code] #3. The PIO program uses `x` for two jobs: countdown timer and temporary pin sample. The
27f38db7 [kimi-k2.7-code] #restore-via-ISR trick works, but it means:
27f38db7 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - Time spent in the “changed” push path is not counted, so long bursts of transitions drift
27f38db7 [kimi-k2.7-code] #slightly.
27f38db8 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - Once `x` decrements to zero, `jmp x--, count` stops jumping and `x` stays at zero forever,
27f38db8 [kimi-k2.7-code] #freezing the timer. At 125 MHz that’s ~343 seconds of idle bus, so it may not bite you in practice,
27f38db8 [kimi-k2.7-code] #but it is a real landmine for long captures.
27f38db8 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38db9 [kimi-k2.7-code] #4. There’s no debounce/settle filtering yet, which matches what you already noted in
27f38db9 [kimi-k2.7-code] #`thoughts.txt`. The trace excerpts with intermediate values like `4c4b → 4c47 → 4847` are classic
27f38db9 [kimi-k2.7-code] #bus skew. You can attack this either in the PIO (require N stable samples before reporting a
27f38db9 [kimi-k2.7-code] #change) or in C++ (drop/merge samples whose duration is below a glitch threshold).
27f38dba [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38dba [kimi-k2.7-code] #5. `beginSnoop` does not reset the PIO state machine or drain stale FIFO data before starting a new
27f38dba [kimi-k2.7-code] #capture. If you ever restart capture after a partial run, the first samples could be leftovers.
27f38dba [kimi-k2.7-code] #Consider clearing the FIFO and restarting the SM program counter before arming DMA.
27f38dbb [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38dbb [kimi-k2.7-code] #6. `std::endl` in `rpcSend` flushes the stream every single sample. For 4096 samples that’s a lot
27f38dbb [kimi-k2.7-code] #of USB overhead. Use `'\n'` and rely on the periodic `cdcFlush()` calls you already have in the
27f38dbb [kimi-k2.7-code] #sample loop.
27f38dbc [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38dbc [kimi-k2.7-code] #7. `updateRPC` calls `parser.parseJSON` but never checks the return value before using `payload`. A
27f38dbc [kimi-k2.7-code] #malformed line will likely crash. Check the parse status and guard `payload`.
27f38dbc [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38dbd [kimi-k2.7-code] #8. `rpcSend` takes `std::string` by value. For hot paths, `const std::string&` removes copies.
27f38dbd [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38dbd [kimi-k2.7-code] #9. `sampleRom` looks like dead code now — left over from before PIO. If it’s not used, consider
27f38dbd [kimi-k2.7-code] #removing it to reduce confusion.
27f38dbe [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38dbe [kimi-k2.7-code] #10. `runShell` only arms `snoopRom` once after `vidbit.set` because `snoopReady` is never set true
27f38dbe [kimi-k2.7-code] #again. If you want recurring captures, you’ll need to reset `snoopReady` somewhere or change the
27f38dbe [kimi-k2.7-code] #trigger model.
27f38dbf [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38dbf [kimi-k2.7-code] #11. The pin-map comment says `A7..A0 | 5..12`, and `pinStamp` does `reverse8((pins>>5)&0xff)`. Make
27f38dbf [kimi-k2.7-code] #sure the reversal matches your physical wiring — it’s easy for A0/A7 to end up swapped.
27f38dbf [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38dc0 [kimi-k2.7-code] #12. The address mask in `runShell` (`0x02000007`) ignores A13–A15 plus the LED pin. That’s fine for
27f38dc0 [kimi-k2.7-code] #suppressing noise, but be aware it means high-address transitions alone won’t wake the shell loop.
27f38dc0 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38dc0 [kimi-k2.7-code] #• Overall*
27f38dc1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #The architecture feels right: PIO does the heavy lifting, the shell loop stays responsive, and
27f38dc1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #samples stream out as JSON-RPC. The two biggest wins from here are probably adding glitch filtering
27f38dc1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #for the skew you’re already seeing, and making `snoopRom` robust for repeated captures by resetting
27f38dc1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #state each time.
27f38dc2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27f38dc2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #Let me know if you want me to sketch out a PIO debouncer or a C++ sample-merging filter.
27f38dc2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
