27fa630d [kimi-k2.7-code] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 kimi-k2.7-code 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.019 19.69KB 138.15s
27fa630d [kimi-k2.7-code] #Hi — here are some observations on the current `romulon` code, no patches unless you want them.
27fa630d [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27fa630e [kimi-k2.7-code] #Big-picture
27fa630e [kimi-k2.7-code] #This is a Pico-based 6502-bus sampler: PIO watches GPIOs, DMA pushes `(tick, pins)` pairs into a
27fa630e [kimi-k2.7-code] #buffer, and the main loop streams formatted samples out over USB CDC as JSON-RPC. The decoding in
27fa630e [kimi-k2.7-code] #`pinStamp()` maps the raw Pico GPIOs back to 6502 address/data/RW/CS lines.
27fa630f [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27fa630f [kimi-k2.7-code] #Things that look like they need fixing
27fa630f [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27fa6310 [kimi-k2.7-code] #1. `pinBuffer` is never sized, so `snoopLog()` probably sees zero samples
27fa6310 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - In `snoopRom()` you `reserve(MAX_SAMPLES)` then take `pinBuffer.data()`, but `reserve()` does
27fa6310 [kimi-k2.7-code] #not change `size()`.
27fa6310 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - `snoopLog()` reads `pinBuffer.size()`, which is still `0`.
27fa6311 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - The DMA will overwrite the allocated storage, but the vector does not know about it.
27fa6311 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - Fix: `pinBuffer.resize(MAX_SAMPLES)` before starting DMA, or track the sample count
27fa6311 [kimi-k2.7-code] #separately.
27fa6311 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27fa6312 [kimi-k2.7-code] #2. `snoopLog()` can underflow when `n == 0`
27fa6312 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - `n = pinBuffer.size(); if (n > 128) n = 128;` then `for (i = 0; i < n - 1; i++)`.
27fa6312 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - If `n == 0`, `n - 1` wraps to `SIZE_MAX` and the loop runs out of bounds.
27fa6312 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - Guard with `if (n < 2) return;`.
27fa6313 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27fa6313 [kimi-k2.7-code] #3. `beginSnoop()` DMA transfer count is `max_samples * 2`
27fa6313 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - That matches the `(tick, pins)` pairs, which is fine as long as `max_samples` really means
27fa6313 [kimi-k2.7-code] #“number of `GPIOSample` entries.”
27fa6314 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - Just make sure the caller passes the sample count, not the raw `uint32_t` word count.
27fa6314 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27fa6314 [kimi-k2.7-code] #4. `rpcSend` id uses a double literal
27fa6314 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - `int id = 2e5 + (sendCount++);` — `2e5` has type `double`. It will truncate, but it’s worth
27fa6315 [kimi-k2.7-code] #changing to `200000`.
27fa6315 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27fa6315 [kimi-k2.7-code] #5. JSON parse result is not checked
27fa6315 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - `parser.parseJSON(line, &payload)` returns a status, but you dereference `payload`
27fa6316 [kimi-k2.7-code] #unconditionally.
27fa6316 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - If parsing fails, this will crash.
27fa6316 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27fa6316 [kimi-k2.7-code] #6. `std::endl` after every JSON line
27fa6317 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - Each `out << ... << std::endl;` flushes the stream. Over CDC that can be expensive.
27fa6317 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - Prefer `'\n'` and flush explicitly when you need to (you already call `cdcFlush()`).
27fa6317 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27fa6317 [kimi-k2.7-code] #7. `runShell()` watchdog update is conditional on `!bootSel`
27fa6318 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - If `readBootSelect()` ever returns `true` for a long time, the watchdog could fire.
27fa6318 [kimi-k2.7-code] #   - Usually you want to `watchdog_update()` on every healthy loop iteration.
27fa6318 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27fa6318 [kimi-k2.7-code] #Smaller / stylistic notes
27fa6319 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27fa6319 [kimi-k2.7-code] #- `pinStamp()` uses a fixed static `stampBuffer[20]` and returns a `std::string` constructed from
27fa6319 [kimi-k2.7-code] #it. That’s not thread-safe, but on a single-core Pico it’s probably fine. Still, a local buffer or
27fa6319 [kimi-k2.7-code] #direct construction would be cleaner.
27fa631a [kimi-k2.7-code] #- Lots of globals are declared but unused (`currentPins`, `currentCount`, `blinkCount`, `rtcGood`,
27fa631a [kimi-k2.7-code] #`mode`, `dpins`, `apins`, `hitCount`, `commandQueue`, `shellActive`, `emitBuffer`). Not a bug, but
27fa631a [kimi-k2.7-code] #it makes it harder to see what’s actually wired up.
27fa631a [kimi-k2.7-code] #- `stringify()` always `reserve(1024)` regardless of input length; `reserve(text.size() * 2 + 2)`
27fa631b [kimi-k2.7-code] #would be closer to reality.
27fa631b [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27fa631b [kimi-k2.7-code] #PIO sampler
27fa631b [kimi-k2.7-code] #The PIO loop is simple and should work for detecting pin changes. A couple of things to keep in
27fa631c [kimi-k2.7-code] #mind:
27fa631c [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27fa631c [kimi-k2.7-code] #- The counter `x` decrements continuously, so the “timestamp” is really a relative cycle count, not
27fa631c [kimi-k2.7-code] #wall time. Wrapping is fine for deltas as long as you compare consecutive samples.
27fa631d [kimi-k2.7-code] #- With the current delay slot and NOP, you’re sampling GPIO roughly every ~9–10 PIO cycles (~70–80
27fa631d [kimi-k2.7-code] #ns at 125 MHz). That’s fast enough for a 1 MHz 6502, but multiple rapid transitions within that
27fa631d [kimi-k2.7-code] #window will be merged into the first observed change.
27fa631d [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27fa631e [kimi-k2.7-code] #Overall
27fa631e [kimi-k2.7-code] #The architecture is clear, but the `pinBuffer` sizing bug means the logging path is probably not
27fa631e [kimi-k2.7-code] #doing what you think it’s doing right now. Fixing `reserve` → `resize` and guarding the `n - 1`
27fa631f [kimi-k2.7-code] #loop are the two highest-impact changes.
27fa631f [kimi-k2.7-code] #
27fa631f [kimi-k2.7-code] #Want me to turn these into a patch or refactor `snoopRom`/`snoopLog` into a cleaner sample-pump?
27fa631f [kimi-k2.7-code] #
