27fad85d [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.008 22.15KB 15.66s
27fad85d [grok-build-0.1] #Here's my honest take on the romulon codebase after reading through it.
27fad85d [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad85e [grok-build-0.1] #Overall Impression
27fad85e [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad85e [grok-build-0.1] #This is a classic "make the Pico do something it wasn't really designed for" project — using PIO +
27fad85e [grok-build-0.1] #DMA to snoop a 6502 bus at decent speed. The core idea is sound, and you're getting real data out,
27fad85f [grok-build-0.1] #which is the important part. However, the implementation has several fragile areas that are likely
27fad85f [grok-build-0.1] #to bite you as you try to improve capture quality or add features.
27fad85f [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad85f [grok-build-0.1] #Critical Issues
27fad860 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad860 [grok-build-0.1] #• 1. Dangerous buffer handling (biggest red flag)*
27fad860 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad860 [grok-build-0.1] #uint32_t* buffer = (uint32_t*)(pinBuffer.data());
27fad861 [grok-build-0.1] #beginSnoop(buffer, MAX_SAMPLES);
27fad861 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad861 [grok-build-0.1] #You're casting a `std::vector<GPIOSample>` directly to a `uint32_t` and letting DMA write into
27fad861 [grok-build-0.1] #it. This works right now* because `GPIOSample` is two `uint32_t`s, but it's undefined behavior
27fad862 [grok-build-0.1] #territory. Any reallocation, or even some vector operations, can invalidate the pointer. You should
27fad862 [grok-build-0.1] #either:
27fad862 [grok-build-0.1] #- Use a raw array + manual tracking, or
27fad862 [grok-build-0.1] #- Use a `std::vector<uint32_t>` and reinterpret the data later.
27fad863 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad863 [grok-build-0.1] #• 2. PIO timestamp mechanism is brittle*
27fad863 [grok-build-0.1] #You're using the PIO's `x` register as a free-running down-counter. This creates several problems:
27fad863 [grok-build-0.1] #- Timestamps go backwards (hence the `epoch - tick` dance).
27fad864 [grok-build-0.1] #- Resolution and wrap behavior are awkward.
27fad864 [grok-build-0.1] #- The way you're pushing two words (timestamp + pins) on every change has some structural issues in
27fad864 [grok-build-0.1] #the PIO program.
27fad864 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad865 [grok-build-0.1] #The current `snoop.pio` is trying to do too much in one program. The `jmp x--, count` + `nop [4]`
27fad865 [grok-build-0.1] #pattern is a bit of a hack for timing.
27fad865 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad865 [grok-build-0.1] #• 3. Missing definitions*
27fad866 [grok-build-0.1] #Several functions are used but not present in the files you shared:
27fad866 [grok-build-0.1] #- `hexout()`
27fad866 [grok-build-0.1] #- `cdcReadLine()`, `cdcFlush()`, `cdcStatus()`, `cdcInit()`
27fad866 [grok-build-0.1] #- `out` stream
27fad867 [grok-build-0.1] #- `setTime()`, `readBootSelect()`, etc.
27fad867 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad867 [grok-build-0.1] #This makes it hard to fully review the USB/CDC side.
27fad867 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad868 [grok-build-0.1] #Design Observations
27fad868 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad868 [grok-build-0.1] #╭──────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────╮
27fad868 [grok-build-0.1] #│  Area                        │  Comment                                                                                        │  Risk    │
27fad869 [grok-build-0.1] #├──────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────┤
27fad869 [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Address reconstruction**  │  `reverse8()` + manual bit shuffling is error-prone and hard to maintain                        │  Medium  │
27fad869 [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Filtering**               │  Doing basic delta filtering in `snoopLog()` is fine, but you're still shipping a lot of noise  │  Low     │
27fad869 [grok-build-0.1] #│  **RPC protocol**            │  Very ad-hoc. Mixing different result shapes makes client parsing painful                       │  Medium  │
27fad86a [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Main loop**               │  Polling `gpio_get_all()` every iteration while also doing snooping is messy                    │  Medium  │
27fad86a [grok-build-0.1] #│  **Globals**                 │  Heavy use of globals for state (`snoopConnected`, `epoch`, etc.)                               │  Medium  │
27fad86a [grok-build-0.1] #╰──────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────╯
27fad86a [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad86b [grok-build-0.1] #The `pinStamp()` function is doing too much work (bit mangling + formatting) on every sample. This
27fad86b [grok-build-0.1] #should probably be separated into "decode" and "format" stages.
27fad86b [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad86b [grok-build-0.1] #PIO Program Thoughts
27fad86c [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad86c [grok-build-0.1] #The current approach of pushing on every pin change is aggressive. You're going to get a
27fad86c [grok-build-0.1] #lot of settling/glitch samples (which you're already seeing in `thoughts.txt`).
27fad86c [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad86d [grok-build-0.1] #A few directions people usually go:
27fad86d [grok-build-0.1] #- Capture on every change but do smarter filtering later (what you're doing)
27fad86d [grok-build-0.1] #- Add some hysteresis/debounce logic in the PIO itself
27fad86e [grok-build-0.1] #- Switch to sampling at a fixed rate instead of change detection (simpler but higher bandwidth)
27fad86e [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad86e [grok-build-0.1] #Your current PIO also has a subtle issue: when it detects a change, it pushes the old
27fad86e [grok-build-0.1] #timestamp and the new pins (or vice versa). The ordering isn't super clear.
27fad86f [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad86f [grok-build-0.1] #Questions
27fad86f [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad86f [grok-build-0.1] #To give you more useful feedback, I need to know your priorities:
27fad870 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad870 [grok-build-0.1] #1. What is the actual goal right now?
27fad870 [grok-build-0.1] #   Just getting clean traces, or do you want to do protocol decoding (detect opcodes, operands,
27fad870 [grok-build-0.1] #memory accesses, etc.)?
27fad871 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad871 [grok-build-0.1] #2. How important is timing accuracy vs throughput?
27fad871 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad871 [grok-build-0.1] #3. Are you planning to keep using the current JSON-RPC-over-CDC approach, or is that just for
27fad872 [grok-build-0.1] #development?
27fad872 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad872 [grok-build-0.1] #4. Do you want me to focus on any specific part? (e.g. fixing the PIO program, cleaning up the
27fad872 [grok-build-0.1] #capture pipeline, making the address decoding less painful, etc.)
27fad873 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad873 [grok-build-0.1] #Also, one quick tactical suggestion: stop using the vector cast trick immediately and switch to a
27fad873 [grok-build-0.1] #plain `uint32_t` buffer for DMA. That's the change most likely to save you pain later.
27fad873 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fad874 [grok-build-0.1] #Want me to go deeper on any of these areas?
27fad874 [grok-build-0.1] #
