27fb47f6 [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.007 19.97KB 34.80s
27fb47f7 [grok-build-0.1] #Thank you for sharing the Romulon source. The project is a clean attempt to use RP2040 PIO + DMA
27fb47f7 [grok-build-0.1] #for non-intrusive 6502 bus capture on a 1541, with the host side apparently being a JSON-RPC client
27fb47f7 [grok-build-0.1] #(vidbit).
27fb47f7 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb47f8 [grok-build-0.1] #High-level observations
27fb47f8 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb47f8 [grok-build-0.1] #The core capture path (snoop.pio → DMA → pinBuffer) is the right shape for this kind of work. Using
27fb47f8 [grok-build-0.1] #a free-running down-counter for relative timestamps avoids needing another timer channel. The
27fb47f9 [grok-build-0.1] #filtering in snoopLog (delta < 20 ticks) is a reasonable first attempt at removing settling/glitch
27fb47f9 [grok-build-0.1] #events.
27fb47f9 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb47f9 [grok-build-0.1] #Notable issues
27fb47fa [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb47fa [grok-build-0.1] #- Memory budget. `MAX_SAMPLES = 1024*1024` with `GPIOSample` (8 bytes each) is 8 MiB. The
27fb47fa [grok-build-0.1] #RP2040 has ~264 KiB of SRAM. `pinBuffer.resize(MAX_SAMPLES)` will fail at runtime. Even 16k–32k
27fb47fa [grok-build-0.1] #samples is already pushing it once you account for stack, USB buffers, etc.
27fb47fb [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb47fb [grok-build-0.1] #- Captured count handling. After `snoopComplete()`, the code still walks `pinBuffer.size()`
27fb47fb [grok-build-0.1] #(the full allocated size) and hard-caps at 1024. It should use `getCapturedSampleCount()` and only
27fb47fb [grok-build-0.1] #process/resize to the actual number of valid pairs written by DMA.
27fb47fc [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb47fc [grok-build-0.1] #- Epoch and ordering. `epoch` is set lazily inside `pinStamp`. When `snoopLog` later calls
27fb47fc [grok-build-0.1] #it on a buffer that was captured with `epoch==0`, the first call mutates the global. This works for
27fb47fc [grok-build-0.1] #one-shot logs but is fragile if you ever want to log multiple captures or interleave other output.
27fb47fd [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb47fd [grok-build-0.1] #- PIO counter behaviour. The down-counter + `nop [4]` gives a coarse but usable tick. The
27fb47fd [grok-build-0.1] #current filtering assumes the first of a close pair is the "real" value. You may want to keep the
27fb47fd [grok-build-0.1] #last of a settling burst instead (or both with a flag).
27fb47fe [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb47fe [grok-build-0.1] #- Trigger path. The polling loop with `sleep_us(1)` for the CS trigger is acceptable for a
27fb47fe [grok-build-0.1] #one-time arm, but it burns cycles and can miss the exact edge if the mask is broad.
27fb47fe [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb47ff [grok-build-0.1] #- Missing pieces. Several symbols (`hexout`, the cdc* layer, `out`, `JSONParser`,
27fb47ff [grok-build-0.1] #`setTime`, `readBootSelect`, watchdog wrappers) are referenced but not present in the shared files.
27fb47ff [grok-build-0.1] #I assume they live in picosdk/ or other .cpp units.
27fb4800 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb4800 [grok-build-0.1] #Smaller notes
27fb4800 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb4800 [grok-build-0.1] #- `reverse8` is only used for A0–A7. The rest of the address reconstruction is done with shifts and
27fb4801 [grok-build-0.1] #masks. This is fine, but the mapping is now spread across `pinStamp` and the pin table comment.
27fb4801 [grok-build-0.1] #- `snoopLog` only ever sends the first 1024 qualifying samples even after a large capture. If the
27fb4801 [grok-build-0.1] #intent is "send everything that survived filtering," the 1024 cap should probably be removed or
27fb4801 [grok-build-0.1] #made a parameter.
27fb4802 [grok-build-0.1] #- The JSON-RPC framing writes directly to `out` with `std::endl`. On a USB CDC link this can
27fb4802 [grok-build-0.1] #produce a lot of small packets.
27fb4802 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb4802 [grok-build-0.1] #If you have a specific goal for the next step (smaller reliable capture buffer, better settling
27fb4803 [grok-build-0.1] #filter, timestamp format change, trigger improvements, host-side trace decoder, etc.), let me know
27fb4803 [grok-build-0.1] #and I can give targeted suggestions or sketch the shape of a helper function.
27fb4803 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb4cb8 [grok-build-0.1] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.011 33.85KB 27.70s
27fb4cb8 [grok-build-0.1] #Thank you for the update.
27fb4cb8 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb4cb8 [grok-build-0.1] #The reduction of `MAX_SAMPLES` to 4096 is a clear improvement and now fits comfortably in RP2040
27fb4cb9 [grok-build-0.1] #SRAM.
27fb4cb9 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb4cb9 [grok-build-0.1] #Remaining issues worth addressing
27fb4cb9 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb4cba [grok-build-0.1] #• Actual captured length is ignored*
27fb4cba [grok-build-0.1] #After `snoopComplete()` returns, the code still treats the entire `pinBuffer` (size 4096) as valid
27fb4cba [grok-build-0.1] #data. The DMA only fills as many pairs as actually occurred. `getCapturedSampleCount()` exists for
27fb4cba [grok-build-0.1] #this reason but is not used after the capture completes. As a result, `snoopLog()` walks past the
27fb4cbb [grok-build-0.1] #real data and the 1024 hard cap inside it becomes the only thing limiting output.
27fb4cbb [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb4cbb [grok-build-0.1] #• Epoch handling across multiple captures*
27fb4cbb [grok-build-0.1] #`epoch` is set once on the first call to `pinStamp` and then never updated. Because the PIO counter
27fb4cbc [grok-build-0.1] #keeps running between captures, a second `snoopRom` + `snoopLog` will produce incorrect deltas in
27fb4cbc [grok-build-0.1] #`pinStamp`. Each capture run should establish its own reference point from the data it just
27fb4cbc [grok-build-0.1] #received.
27fb4cbc [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb4cbd [grok-build-0.1] #• Filtering and logging limits*
27fb4cbd [grok-build-0.1] #`snoopLog()` still forces `n = min(n, 1024)` and then applies the `< 20` delta filter. With the new
27fb4cbd [grok-build-0.1] #smaller buffer size, the 1024 cap is less necessary, but the logic still doesn't use the real
27fb4cbd [grok-build-0.1] #number of samples returned by the DMA.
27fb4cbe [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb4cbe [grok-build-0.1] #• Buffer ownership after DMA*
27fb4cbe [grok-build-0.1] #The vector is resized to `MAX_SAMPLES` before capture, then the DMA writes directly into it as raw
27fb4cbe [grok-build-0.1] #`uint32_t` pairs. After capture it would be cleaner to trim the vector down to the actual number of
27fb4cbf [grok-build-0.1] #complete samples before any processing or logging.
27fb4cbf [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb4cbf [grok-build-0.1] #• Trigger path*
27fb4cbf [grok-build-0.1] #The busy-wait with `sleep_us(1)` for the CS trigger is still present. It works but occupies the
27fb4cc0 [grok-build-0.1] #core during arming.
27fb4cc0 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb4cc0 [grok-build-0.1] #Other observations
27fb4cc0 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb4cc1 [grok-build-0.1] #- The live pin polling loop in `runShell` and the one-shot snoop path are still tightly coupled
27fb4cc1 [grok-build-0.1] #through the `snoopConnected` / `snoopReady` flags. That design is understandable for the current
27fb4cc1 [grok-build-0.1] #host interaction model.
27fb4cc1 [grok-build-0.1] #- `pinStamp` still does all the address reconstruction inline. The bit mapping comments at the top
27fb4cc2 [grok-build-0.1] #are helpful but the reconstruction logic is spread out.
27fb4cc2 [grok-build-0.1] #- Yielding with `watchdog_update`, `tud_task`, and `cdcFlush` every 8 logged lines during
27fb4cc2 [grok-build-0.1] #`snoopLog` is still a reasonable way to keep USB responsive.
27fb4cc2 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb4cc3 [grok-build-0.1] #If you would like to focus on any of the above (especially proper use of captured count,
27fb4cc3 [grok-build-0.1] #per-capture epoch, or trimming the buffer after DMA), let me know which area to discuss in more
27fb4cc3 [grok-build-0.1] #detail.
27fb4cc4 [grok-build-0.1] #
