27fb58ff [grok-build-0.1] #Thank you for the trace.
27fb5900 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb5900 [grok-build-0.1] #Observations from the log
27fb5900 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb5900 [grok-build-0.1] #- The capture begins with a long run of `$0000,00,W,0` entries. These have very large timestamp
27fb5901 [grok-build-0.1] #deltas at first and then continue with smaller ones. This pattern is almost certainly not real ROM
27fb5901 [grok-build-0.1] #traffic — it looks like either floating bus conditions, periods when the monitored ROM is not
27fb5901 [grok-build-0.1] #selected, or address reconstruction showing 0 when the upper address bits are not being driven to
27fb5901 [grok-build-0.1] #the expected values.
27fb5902 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb5902 [grok-build-0.1] #- There is a short burst around `BF89` and `0080`/`$0081` with mixed CS states.
27fb5902 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb5902 [grok-build-0.1] #- The trace then settles into a very regular alternating pattern:
27fb5903 [grok-build-0.1] #  - `EAAD` reading `95`
27fb5903 [grok-build-0.1] #  - `EAB0` reading `D0`
27fb5903 [grok-build-0.1] #  - All marked as reads (`R`) with CS=`1`
27fb5903 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb5904 [grok-build-0.1] #  The timestamps keep incrementing steadily. This is classic 6502 tight-loop polling behaviour —
27fb5904 [grok-build-0.1] #the CPU is repeatedly executing LDA instructions to these two absolute addresses, most likely
27fb5904 [grok-build-0.1] #waiting on a status flag, disk controller, or serial bus state.
27fb5904 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb5905 [grok-build-0.1] #- Importantly, the long polling loop shows CS=`1` (inactive) for every sample. If the goal is to
27fb5905 [grok-build-0.1] #capture data from the ROM chip whose /CE you wired to GPIO 26, these accesses are not coming from
27fb5905 [grok-build-0.1] #that ROM. They are either from the other ROM or from RAM.
27fb5905 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb5906 [grok-build-0.1] #Immediate problems visible in the current capture path
27fb5906 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb5906 [grok-build-0.1] #1. `snoopLog` still ignores the real number of samples returned by DMA and hard-caps at 1024.
27fb5906 [grok-build-0.1] #2. No per-capture epoch is being set from the actual buffer, so relative timestamps can be wrong on
27fb5907 [grok-build-0.1] #subsequent captures.
27fb5907 [grok-build-0.1] #3. No filtering on CS state. When the monitored ROM is not selected you get either garbage
27fb5907 [grok-build-0.1] #addresses or bus values from other devices.
27fb5907 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb5908 [grok-build-0.1] #Suggested next steps
27fb5908 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb5908 [grok-build-0.1] #- After `snoopComplete()`, trim `pinBuffer` to the actual count returned by
27fb5908 [grok-build-0.1] #`getCapturedSampleCount()`.
27fb5909 [grok-build-0.1] #- Establish `epoch` from the first valid sample of the current capture only.
27fb5909 [grok-build-0.1] #- In the logging loop, only emit entries where CS == 0 (ROM selected) and the access is a
27fb5909 [grok-build-0.1] #read. This will remove the `$0000,00,W` noise and focus on actual ROM traffic.
27fb5909 [grok-build-0.1] #
27fb590a [grok-build-0.1] #Would you like me to show the updated versions of `snoopRom` and `snoopLog` that do the above (plus
27fb590a [grok-build-0.1] #a small helper to decode the CS/RW fields cleanly)? I can also add an optional address-range filter
27fb590a [grok-build-0.1] #if you tell me which ROM region you are currently trying to trace (C000–DFFF or E000–FFFF).
27fb590a [grok-build-0.1] #
