281da5d8 [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.007 68.38KB 21.22s
281da5d9 [deepseek-flash] #The shared content is consistent and mostly what I’d expect from a schema-export workflow:
281da5d9 [deepseek-flash] #
281da5d9 [deepseek-flash] #- `scrapeschema.gd` is a headless `SceneTree` exporter. It builds the schema dictionary,
281da5d9 [deepseek-flash] #writes JSON, and quits. It exports the category lists (`node`, `resource`, `object`, `editor`), the
281da5d9 [deepseek-flash] #`variant` list, engine version metadata, and repository/license fields.
281da5d9 [deepseek-flash] #- `godotSchema.json.txt` matches that exporter’s structure. The JSON contains no method or
281da5da [deepseek-flash] #property signatures, only class-name categories and variants.
281da5da [deepseek-flash] #- `scrapegodot.txt` is a related but different script. It extends `Node`, runs from
281da5da [deepseek-flash] #`_ready`, and includes signature-formatting helpers (`formatParam`, `formatParamList`,
281da5da [deepseek-flash] #`getSignature`, `getSignatures`). Those helpers are not used by `scrapeschema.gd` and are not
281da5db [deepseek-flash] #reflected in the JSON.
281da5db [deepseek-flash] #- The first file appears to be a base64-encoded copy of `scrapeschema.gd`.
281da5db [deepseek-flash] #
281da5db [deepseek-flash] #Notable differences and observations:
281da5db [deepseek-flash] #
281da5dc [deepseek-flash] #- `scrapeschema.gd` is better suited for command-line/headless export because it uses `SceneTree`
281da5dc [deepseek-flash] #and calls `quit()`.
281da5dc [deepseek-flash] #- `scrapegodot.txt` has the scaffolding for scraping method signatures, but it does not actually
281da5dc [deepseek-flash] #write them into the schema.
281da5dd [deepseek-flash] #- The JSON’s `variant` list is generated from `type_string` and starts at type ID `1`, so it omits
281da5dd [deepseek-flash] #`Nil` and includes `Variant` manually. That matches the output.
281da5dd [deepseek-flash] #- The category lists intentionally overlap: a `Node` is also an `Object`, a `Resource` is also an
281da5dd [deepseek-flash] #`Object`, and editor classes can appear in both `node`/`resource` and `editor`.
281da5de [deepseek-flash] #- Both scripts use `ClassDB.is_parent_class(current_name, "Node")` etc. That is the correct Godot 4
281da5de [deepseek-flash] #pattern for checking inheritance from the perspective of the current class.
281da5de [deepseek-flash] #- Minor style issues: `scrapegodot.txt` has some inconsistent indentation, and `scrapeschema.gd`
281da5de [deepseek-flash] #contains a stray string literal near the top that has no effect.
281da5df [deepseek-flash] #
281da5df [deepseek-flash] #If the goal is a richer API schema, the next step would be to merge the signature helpers from
281da5df [deepseek-flash] #`scrapegodot.txt` into `build_schema_dictionary()` and add per-class method/property data to the
281da5df [deepseek-flash] #JSON. As it stands, the JSON is a class-category schema, not a full API signature dump.
281da5e0 [deepseek-flash] #
