28229b89 [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.005 75.82KB 16.44s
28229b89 [deepseek-flash] #That constant evaluates to 8,388,608 bytes = 8 MiB exactly (512 KB × 16). If your intent
28229b89 [deepseek-flash] #was 16 MB, you're off by 2× — `16  1024  1024` is 16,777,216. The expression reads as "512
28229b89 [deepseek-flash] #KB times 16", which is an easy way to land on 8 MB while thinking 16.
28229b89 [deepseek-flash] #
28229b8a [deepseek-flash] #That alone wouldn't "fail silently" though — it would just enforce half the ceiling. Silent failure
28229b8a [deepseek-flash] #usually means something upstream rejects the payload before your constant is ever
28229b8a [deepseek-flash] #consulted, and the rejection never reaches the client.
28229b8a [deepseek-flash] #
28229b8b [deepseek-flash] #Likely culprits, in order
28229b8b [deepseek-flash] #
28229b8b [deepseek-flash] #1. A reverse proxy or body parser caps first.
28229b8b [deepseek-flash] #   - nginx `client_max_body_size` defaults to 1 MB → returns 413, your app code never runs.
28229b8c [deepseek-flash] #   - Caddy `request_body max_size`, Cloudflare (100 MB free tier), or an origin cap.
28229b8c [deepseek-flash] #   - Express `express.json({ limit })` defaults to 100 KB.
28229b8c [deepseek-flash] #   If the client doesn't read the HTTP status or WS close code, the drop is invisible.
28229b8c [deepseek-flash] #
28229b8c [deepseek-flash] #2. WebSocket `maxPayload`. The `ws` library defaults to 100 MiB, but if it's set lower the
28229b8d [deepseek-flash] #server closes with code 1009 (message too big) and your size check is never reached.
28229b8d [deepseek-flash] #
28229b8d [deepseek-flash] #3. Wrong unit or type at the check. Comparing a byte limit against a JS string's `.length`
28229b8d [deepseek-flash] #(UTF-16 units) or against base64 text inflates ~1.33×. Use `Buffer.byteLength(...)`.
28229b8d [deepseek-flash] #
28229b8e [deepseek-flash] #4. The error is swallowed. An empty `catch {}`, `.catch(() => {})`, or try/finally that
28229b8e [deepseek-flash] #doesn't rethrow will trigger the limit and tell nobody.
28229b8e [deepseek-flash] #
28229b8e [deepseek-flash] #5. Two sources of truth. If the relay reads its limit from config or an env var, editing
28229b8e [deepseek-flash] #this constant changes nothing — the config wins silently.
28229b8f [deepseek-flash] #
28229b8f [deepseek-flash] #6. Truncation instead of rejection. Some buffers/protocols silently truncate rather than
28229b8f [deepseek-flash] #error.
28229b8f [deepseek-flash] #
28229b90 [deepseek-flash] #Quick diagnostics
28229b90 [deepseek-flash] #
28229b90 [deepseek-flash] #- Log at the check site and at the HTTP/WS layer, then upload a file just under and just
28229b90 [deepseek-flash] #over 8 MiB.
28229b90 [deepseek-flash] #- Watch for 413 on the HTTP path and close code 1009 on the WS path — those tell
28229b91 [deepseek-flash] #you which layer is cutting it.
28229b91 [deepseek-flash] #- Confirm `Buffer.byteLength(payload)` vs `payload.length` if the body is text or base64.
28229b91 [deepseek-flash] #- Check for a second hardcoded limit or a config/env override.
28229b91 [deepseek-flash] #
28229b91 [deepseek-flash] #To narrow it down: what's the relay stack — Node + `ws`, Nostr, or something else — and is it
28229b92 [deepseek-flash] #behind nginx, Caddy, or Cloudflare? That determines which of the above is actually biting.
28229b92 [deepseek-flash] #
