28229ce7 [deepseek-flash] #Good — that log line changes the diagnosis. This is your own guard doing its job, not a
28229ce7 [deepseek-flash] #proxy or transport limit. The earlier nginx/`ws maxPayload` theories don't apply here, because the
28229ce7 [deepseek-flash] #path is a local Windows file (`C:\nitrologic\relay\gd\extension_api.json`) — the relay is reading
28229ce8 [deepseek-flash] #it off disk and your constant is the thing that stopped it.
28229ce8 [deepseek-flash] #
28229ce8 [deepseek-flash] #What's actually happening
28229ce8 [deepseek-flash] #
28229ce8 [deepseek-flash] #`extension_api.json` is Godot's own generated API dump, produced by `godot --headless
28229ce9 [deepseek-flash] #--dump-extension-api`. It's a large single JSON document — several MB for Godot 4.x, and it grows
28229ce9 [deepseek-flash] #with every release as classes are added. Your cap is 8 MiB (`512102416`), and the
28229ce9 [deepseek-flash] #file exceeds it, so the ingest aborts.
28229ce9 [deepseek-flash] #
28229ce9 [deepseek-flash] #So the failure was never silent at the check — it was silent at the surface. The guard
28229cea [deepseek-flash] #logged and bailed, but nothing told the caller. That's the real bug to fix, separate from the size
28229cea [deepseek-flash] #question.
28229cea [deepseek-flash] #
28229cea [deepseek-flash] #The arithmetic, again
28229cea [deepseek-flash] #
28229ceb [deepseek-flash] #
28229ceb [deepseek-flash] #512 * 1024 * 16  =  8,388,608 bytes  =  8 MiB
28229ceb [deepseek-flash] #16 * 1024 * 1024 = 16,777,216 bytes  = 16 MiB
28229ceb [deepseek-flash] #
28229ceb [deepseek-flash] #
28229cec [deepseek-flash] #If you meant 16 MB, you're enforcing half of it. Worth expressing the constant directly in MiB
28229cec [deepseek-flash] #rather than as "512 KB × N" — the multiplication hides the intent and invites exactly this.
28229cec [deepseek-flash] #
28229cec [deepseek-flash] #Options
28229ced [deepseek-flash] #
28229ced [deepseek-flash] #╭─────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
28229ced [deepseek-flash] #│  Option                 │  Tradeoff                                                                                    │
28229ced [deepseek-flash] #├─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
28229ced [deepseek-flash] #│  Raise the cap          │  Simplest. But pick a number you can defend, not just "bigger than this file."               │
28229cee [deepseek-flash] #│  Compress before relay  │  JSON gzips ~10×. Best if the wire is the constraint.                                        │
28229cee [deepseek-flash] #│  Don't relay this file  │  It's a regenerable build artifact — the consumer could run `--dump-extension-api` locally.  │
28229cee [deepseek-flash] #│  Chunk / stream it      │  Most work; only worth it if you expect files of this class routinely.                       │
28229cee [deepseek-flash] #╰─────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
28229cee [deepseek-flash] #
28229cef [deepseek-flash] #My instinct: if this file is the only thing tripping the cap, option 3 is the cleanest —
28229cef [deepseek-flash] #you're shipping a build output through a relay that otherwise handles smaller payloads. If several
28229cef [deepseek-flash] #files of this size are expected, raise the cap and gzip.
28229cef [deepseek-flash] #
28229cf0 [deepseek-flash] #Before deciding
28229cf0 [deepseek-flash] #
28229cf0 [deepseek-flash] #Check the actual size on disk — `dir` in that folder, or right-click → Properties. That tells you
28229cf0 [deepseek-flash] #whether 16 MiB clears it comfortably or whether you need 32, and it's the number you should size
28229cf0 [deepseek-flash] #the cap against.
28229cf0 [deepseek-flash] #
28229cf1 [deepseek-flash] #Also worth confirming: is `extension_api.json` something the relay needs to carry, or did
28229cf1 [deepseek-flash] #it get swept in by a directory walk? The `gd\` subfolder suggests it may be incidental to whatever
28229cf1 [deepseek-flash] #you were actually relaying.
28229cf2 [deepseek-flash] #
28229cf2 [deepseek-flash] #Separate fix
28229cf2 [deepseek-flash] #
28229cf2 [deepseek-flash] #Whatever you decide on the limit, make the rejection visible — return an error to the caller rather
28229cf3 [deepseek-flash] #than only logging it. A guard that stops work and tells nobody is how you ended up reading the log
28229cf3 [deepseek-flash] #to find this.
28229cf3 [deepseek-flash] #
28229cf3 [deepseek-flash] #What's the relay meant to carry — is `gd\` a deliberate payload set, or a folder it walked into?
