28769613 [nitro@ryzen5] what other standards would have peers inferring adjacent paths?
28769972 [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.014 89.43KB 53.04s
28769972 [deepseek-flash] #Three separate problems are usually bundled into that question, and different standards solve
28769972 [deepseek-flash] #different ones.
28769972 [deepseek-flash] #
28769973 [deepseek-flash] #• 1. Resolving a bare name to an adjacent resource (the mechanism)*
28769973 [deepseek-flash] #
28769973 [deepseek-flash] #- RFC 3986 §5 — relative-reference resolution against a base URI. Everything below is a
28769973 [deepseek-flash] #specialisation of this.
28769974 [deepseek-flash] #- JSON Schema `id` / `ref` — a subschema's `id` establishes the base for nested `ref`s, so
28769974 [deepseek-flash] #sibling files resolve by position. `dynamicAnchor`/`dynamicRef` (draft 2020‑12) is the one
28769974 [deepseek-flash] #mechanism designed specifically for "resolve to whichever sibling overrides this."
28769974 [deepseek-flash] #- JSON-LD `@base` / `@id` — relative IRIs in `@id` expand against the document base.
28769975 [deepseek-flash] #RO‑Crate is the worked example: entities are `./biography/aaronswartz.json`, resolved
28769975 [deepseek-flash] #against `ro-crate-metadata.json`.
28769975 [deepseek-flash] #- Bazel labels — `//books/biography:timbernerslee`, with `:name` meaning "in this package."
28769975 [deepseek-flash] #Package-relative naming is the whole point of the syntax. Buck2 and Pants share it.
28769976 [deepseek-flash] #- PSR‑4 (PHP), Java packages, C# namespaces, PEP 328 relative imports, Rust `super::` —
28769976 [deepseek-flash] #namespace/name determines path by convention. PSR‑4 is the cleanest statement of the rule.
28769976 [deepseek-flash] #- C `#include "x.h"` vs `<x.h>` — the crisp framing of the design choice: quote-relative
28769976 [deepseek-flash] #(adjacent) versus search-path (authority-resolved).
28769977 [deepseek-flash] #- MediaWiki wikilinks — title → adjacent page, with namespace and redirect resolution. The
28769977 [deepseek-flash] #oldest widely-deployed version of this.
28769977 [deepseek-flash] #- WebFinger (RFC 7033) and `.well-known` (RFC 8615) — discovery when there is no
28769977 [deepseek-flash] #path at all. AT Protocol's handle → DID resolution goes through `/.well-known/atproto-did` for
28769978 [deepseek-flash] #exactly this reason.
28769978 [deepseek-flash] #
28769978 [deepseek-flash] #• 2. Typing the relation (what kind of peer)*
28769978 [deepseek-flash] #
28769979 [deepseek-flash] #- RFC 8288 Web Linking — the IANA registry includes `related`, `prev`, `next`, `alternate`,
28769979 [deepseek-flash] #`via`, `author`, `item`. Atom (RFC 4287) uses the same registry.
28769979 [deepseek-flash] #- FOAF `foaf:knows` — the original "peers" property, and the FOAF convention is that the
28769979 [deepseek-flash] #document lives at a dereferenceable adjacent URL.
2876997a [deepseek-flash] #- schema.org `knows`, `follows`, `memberOf`, `sameAs` — note there is no `colleague`
2876997a [deepseek-flash] #property; that one only exists in vCard.
2876997a [deepseek-flash] #- vCard 4 (RFC 6350) `RELATED;TYPE=colleague` — a formal peer-type vocabulary.
2876997a [deepseek-flash] #- Dublin Core `dcterms:relation` / `references` / `isReferencedBy` / `replaces` — the
2876997b [deepseek-flash] #generic library answer.
2876997b [deepseek-flash] #- OWL `sameAs`, RDFS `seeAlso` / `isDefinedBy` — `isDefinedBy` is literally "the adjacent
2876997b [deepseek-flash] #document that defines this."
2876997b [deepseek-flash] #
2876997c [deepseek-flash] #• 3. The bibliographic precedents (closest to your case)*
2876997c [deepseek-flash] #
2876997c [deepseek-flash] #These are the ones worth reading before choosing, because they've already fought the name-collision
2876997c [deepseek-flash] #problem:
2876997d [deepseek-flash] #
2876997d [deepseek-flash] #- BibLaTeX `related` / `relatedtype` — a key references another key in the same `.bib`,
2876997d [deepseek-flash] #with a typed relation (`reviewof`, `bytranslator`, `multivolume`). This is the nearest structural
2876997d [deepseek-flash] #analogue to `peers`.
2876997e [deepseek-flash] #- BibTeX `crossref` and BibLaTeX `@xdata` — inheritance between adjacent entries.
2876997e [deepseek-flash] #- Crossref relation types — `is-related-to`, `is-preprint-of`, `is-supplement-to`,
2876997e [deepseek-flash] #`is-reply-to`, `is-version-of`.
2876997e [deepseek-flash] #- DataCite `relationType` — `IsRelatedTo`, `IsCitedBy`, `IsPartOf`, `Continues`,
2876997f [deepseek-flash] #`IsDerivedFrom`.
2876997f [deepseek-flash] #- MARC 787 (nonspecific relationship) plus 780/785 (preceding/succeeding), with `$w` for
2876997f [deepseek-flash] #the link. MARC 7XX is the "added entry" family — peers, in library terms.
2876997f [deepseek-flash] #- MADS `<related>` between authority records; BIBFRAME relation properties
28769980 [deepseek-flash] #(`precededBy`, `succeededBy`, `references`/`referencedBy`).
28769980 [deepseek-flash] #- RDA Appendix K — relationship designators between persons: collaborator, associate,
28769980 [deepseek-flash] #friend, spouse.
28769980 [deepseek-flash] #- TEI `<listRelation>` / `<relation name="peer" mutual="true">` — peer relations as
28769981 [deepseek-flash] #first-class, bidirectional.
28769981 [deepseek-flash] #- METS `xlink:href` and OAI‑ORE resource maps — relative links between files in a
28769981 [deepseek-flash] #package.
28769981 [deepseek-flash] #- ISAAR(CPF) 5.3 and ISAD(G) 3.5.3 — the archival equivalents.
28769982 [deepseek-flash] #
28769982 [deepseek-flash] #• What this means for your two options*
28769982 [deepseek-flash] #
28769982 [deepseek-flash] #If `peers` stays a plain string, you get nothing for free — every consumer invents its own slugging
28769982 [deepseek-flash] #rule, and "Tim Berners-Lee" → `timbernerslee` vs `tim-berners-lee` vs `bern-ers-lee` is a silent
28769982 [deepseek-flash] #failure. Name-slugging is also locale-hostile: "Türkiye", "Côte d'Ivoire", "São Paulo" all break
28769982 [deepseek-flash] #naive rules.
28769982 [deepseek-flash] #
28769982 [deepseek-flash] #If you want adjacency, the closest fit to what you already have is JSON-LD with `@base` set to
28769983 [deepseek-flash] #`books/biography/`, so `@id: "timbernerslee.json"` resolves by position, with the relation typed
28769983 [deepseek-flash] #via RFC 8288's `related`. That is RO‑Crate's model almost exactly.
28769983 [deepseek-flash] #
28769983 [deepseek-flash] #But note you already carry the better key. Your `identifier` block in `biblispec.json` has VIAF,
28769983 [deepseek-flash] #ISNI, Wikidata, ORCID. Those are stable, unique, and locale-independent — the reason authority
28769984 [deepseek-flash] #files exist. A `peers` entry that is a Wikidata Q‑number sidesteps slugging entirely and lets the
28769984 [deepseek-flash] #path be derived rather than guessed. The cost is that it stops being human-readable, which may be
28769984 [deepseek-flash] #why you wrote plain names in the first place.
28769985 [deepseek-flash] #
28769985 [deepseek-flash] #The honest tradeoff: readable names are lossy keys, stable IDs are opaque ones. Standards that try
28769985 [deepseek-flash] #to have both — DID Core's `alsoKnownAs`, ActivityPub's `alsoKnownAs`/`movedTo`, IndieWeb `rel=me` —
28769985 [deepseek-flash] #all end up carrying both a human handle and a machine identifier, and accepting that they can drift
28769985 [deepseek-flash] #apart.
28769986 [deepseek-flash] #
