2876a5de [nitro@ryzen5]    This document specifies an Internet standards track protocol for the   Internet community, and requests discussion and suggestions for   improvements.  Please refer to the current edition of the "Internet   Official Protocol Standards" (STD 1) for the standardization state   and status of this protocol.  Distribution of this memo is unlimited.
2876a68e [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.003 95.26KB 10.50s
2876a68e [deepseek-flash] #That's the pre‑2010 IETF boilerplate — the "Status of This Memo" block used on Standards Track
2876a68e [deepseek-flash] #documents before RFC 5741 replaced it. RFC 5741 (2010) and RFC 7841 (2016) moved to explicit
2876a68e [deepseek-flash] #`Stream:` and `Status:` lines, so the version you've pasted is itself a dating signal: if a
2876a68e [deepseek-flash] #document carries this wording, it predates the reform.
2876a68f [deepseek-flash] #
2876a68f [deepseek-flash] #A few things worth noting for an index like yours:
2876a68f [deepseek-flash] #
2876a68f [deepseek-flash] #• The boilerplate is already structured metadata.* It declares four things: the track
2876a690 [deepseek-flash] #(Standards Track), the STD 1 relationship, the discussion expectation, and the distribution terms.
2876a690 [deepseek-flash] #All four are extractable, and all four vary by document era.
2876a690 [deepseek-flash] #
2876a690 [deepseek-flash] #• Two axes, not one. RFC 5741 separates Stream (IETF, IRTF, IAB, Independent,
2876a690 [deepseek-flash] #Editorial) from Status* (Standards Track, BCP, Informational, Experimental, Historic). Older
2876a691 [deepseek-flash] #RFCs collapse these into a single phrase. If your schema has one `status` field, it will need to
2876a691 [deepseek-flash] #hold the old collapsed form and the new split form — or the two need separate fields with a version
2876a691 [deepseek-flash] #gate.
2876a692 [deepseek-flash] #
2876a692 [deepseek-flash] #• STD 1 is a many-to-one relation.* A single STD number can cover several RFCs, and an RFC
2876a692 [deepseek-flash] #can be in no STD at all. That's structurally the same shape as the `peers` problem from the
2876a692 [deepseek-flash] #biography entry — a document pointing at adjacent documents through an indirection table.
2876a693 [deepseek-flash] #
2876a693 [deepseek-flash] #• The last line is a license grant. "Distribution of this memo is unlimited" is the
2876a693 [deepseek-flash] #operative permission for older RFCs. This is the direct answer to the ambiguity I flagged in the
2876a693 [deepseek-flash] #Aaron Swartz entry: the RFC series states its terms inline, in the document*. The biography
2876a694 [deepseek-flash] #file declared MIT in a field and left the reader to guess the scope. Where you have the
2876a694 [deepseek-flash] #boilerplate, you don't have that problem.
2876a694 [deepseek-flash] #
2876a694 [deepseek-flash] #• ECMA is a different model in three ways:*
2876a695 [deepseek-flash] #
2876a695 [deepseek-flash] #- Editions, not numbers. ECMA‑262 is revised as a numbered edition; RFCs are immutable once
2876a695 [deepseek-flash] #published, with errata kept separately. If your schema has one `version` field, it will mean
2876a695 [deepseek-flash] #"revision" in one case and "erratum" in the other.
2876a696 [deepseek-flash] #- Its own notice. Ecma International copyright plus a royalty‑free patent policy statement,
2876a696 [deepseek-flash] #not the IETF Trust provisions. RFC 5378 (2008) is the cutoff for which IETF terms apply — before
2876a696 [deepseek-flash] #it, the old copyright; after it, the Trust legal provisions.
2876a696 [deepseek-flash] #- ECMA‑262 specifically has a long notice covering both the copyright and the patent
2876a697 [deepseek-flash] #policy, and it names the contributors. It reads more like a standards‑body contract than a
2876a697 [deepseek-flash] #distribution statement.
2876a697 [deepseek-flash] #
2876a697 [deepseek-flash] #Do you want me to sketch how these would fit alongside the existing `license` and `identifier`
2876a698 [deepseek-flash] #blocks, or are you still deciding on the top‑level shape?
2876a698 [deepseek-flash] #
