2876c90d [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.029 553.8KB 74.66s
2876c90d [deepseek-flash] #`scratch/` is doing four jobs at once: display engine, landing page, reference dumps, and runners
2876c90e [deepseek-flash] #into the sibling `maze` repo. Only the first two change together; the dumps don't.
2876c90e [deepseek-flash] #
2876c90e [deepseek-flash] #• The idea worth keeping* is the pairing in `archaic.md` — RFC 2119 keywords beside the
2876c90e [deepseek-flash] #Early Modern English verb table. "SHALL" in a standards document is the same modal as "shall" in a
2876c90f [deepseek-flash] #legal instrument, and the file says so by juxtaposition rather than by explaining it. The
2876c90f [deepseek-flash] #`codepoint_card` then extends it: `SHALL_TRUNCATE`, `shall_render`. That's RFC 2119 language used
2876c90f [deepseek-flash] #as a UI assertion vocabulary, and it's coherent.
2876c90f [deepseek-flash] #
2876c910 [deepseek-flash] #Two fixes there:
2876c910 [deepseek-flash] #- The first JSON block is missing a comma after the closing `]` of `RFC2119keywords`.
2876c910 [deepseek-flash] #- Casing is split — `shall_render` / `shall_clip` / `shall_center` (lowercase keys) against
2876c910 [deepseek-flash] #`SHALL_TRUNCATE` / `SHALL_HIGHLIGHT` (uppercase values). If the rule is "lowercase boolean flag,
2876c911 [deepseek-flash] #uppercase enumerated assertion", state it once; otherwise it reads as drift.
2876c911 [deepseek-flash] #- RFC 2119 spells them with spaces, not underscores (`MUST NOT`, `SHALL NOT`, `NOT RECOMMENDED`).
2876c911 [deepseek-flash] #Underscores are fine for a schema but won't match spec text if anything is ever compared.
2876c912 [deepseek-flash] #
2876c912 [deepseek-flash] #• `bibli.js` — one real bug, several latent:*
2876c912 [deepseek-flash] #
2876c912 [deepseek-flash] #- `if((appWidth!=cols)||(appHeight|=rows))` — `|=` is bitwise-OR-assign; it should be `!=`. As
2876c913 [deepseek-flash] #written, `appHeight` is corrupted on every resize and the branch fires almost always.
2876c913 [deepseek-flash] #- `link`, `close`, `url`, `alphaStyles` are implicit globals. Fine in a classic `<script>`, fatal
2876c913 [deepseek-flash] #the moment you add `type="module"` — which the file's own TODO anticipates.
2876c913 [deepseek-flash] #- The `for(const alpha of alphabet)` loop computes `alphaStyles` and discards it. That's leftover
2876c914 [deepseek-flash] #from the CSS-property exploration visible in `notes.txt`.
2876c914 [deepseek-flash] #- `emit` writes via `innerHTML` and builds anchors by string concatenation, so a `"` or `<` inside
2876c914 [deepseek-flash] #a JSON string value gets parsed as markup.
2876c914 [deepseek-flash] #- `onSize` reads `terminalPre`, which only exists after `onLoad` assigns it — a resize before load
2876c915 [deepseek-flash] #throws.
2876c915 [deepseek-flash] #- `new Error(response)` stringifies to `[object Response]`; `response.statusText` would say
2876c915 [deepseek-flash] #something.
2876c915 [deepseek-flash] #
2876c916 [deepseek-flash] #• `biblispec.json` — the Egyptian hieroglyph entries:*
2876c916 [deepseek-flash] #
2876c916 [deepseek-flash] #- Fish 1, Birds 1, Animals 1, Women, and Eyes carry `range` values that don't correspond to their
2876c916 [deepseek-flash] #own `codePoints`. Fish 1 is `U+1319B–U+1319F`, which is 78235–78239, not 7963–7967. Tens, Hundreds,
2876c917 [deepseek-flash] #Thousands, Ten-Thousands and Eye Parts are self-consistent, so it looks like the category entries
2876c917 [deepseek-flash] #were populated from a different source or an older offset.
2876c917 [deepseek-flash] #- Hundreds (`U+13406–U+1340E`) and Ten-Thousands (`U+13404–U+1340C`) overlap by seven code points.
2876c917 [deepseek-flash] #Both can't be right.
2876c918 [deepseek-flash] #
2876c918 [deepseek-flash] #• `index.html`:*
2876c918 [deepseek-flash] #
2876c918 [deepseek-flash] #- `og:title` says 1.5.5; the payload is 1.5.8.
2876c919 [deepseek-flash] #- The paths can't all resolve from one base. It links `bibli.css` and `bibli.js` (which live in
2876c919 [deepseek-flash] #`scratch/`), `books/ambulate.json` (one level up), and `bibli.js` fetches `./biblispec.json`.
2876c919 [deepseek-flash] #Whichever directory it's served from, something 404s.
2876c919 [deepseek-flash] #
2876c91a [deepseek-flash] #• `notes.txt` has three generations of the same data.* The submarine cables appear as both
2876c91a [deepseek-flash] #`backbone` and `pipes` with different years and capacities. The AWS/Alibaba zones appear three
2876c91a [deepseek-flash] #times, and the third block is corrupted — `Manila@ap-southeast-6ượng`, `HongKong@cn-hongkongc`,
2876c91a [deepseek-flash] #`Beijing@cn-beijingj`, `London@eu-west-1c` (London is `eu-west-2`). That's the drift that
2876c91b [deepseek-flash] #propagates into `biblispec.json` if anything gets copied across, so it's the first thing I'd
2876c91b [deepseek-flash] #resolve.
2876c91b [deepseek-flash] #
2876c91b [deepseek-flash] #Minor: `test.bat` opens with `echo off` rather than `@echo off`, so the command itself prints on
2876c91c [deepseek-flash] #the first line. `relay.md` is a 19-byte stub — fine if it's a placeholder, worth deleting if not.
2876c91c [deepseek-flash] #
2876c91c [deepseek-flash] #If you want an order of attack: the `bibli.js` `|=` and the hieroglyph `range` mismatches are the
2876c91c [deepseek-flash] #two that are silently wrong rather than merely untidy.
2876c91d [deepseek-flash] #
