2876e21c [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.009 564B 40.02s
2876e21c [deepseek-flash] #Assuming you mean SSH/TCP sessions to an Arch host, the short version is: Arch didn’t break
2876e21c [deepseek-flash] #connection lifetime; it removed the distro-specific defaults that were hiding the real timeout.
2876e21c [deepseek-flash] #A connection stays alive only as long as every layer in the path agrees it’s alive. The effective
2876e21d [deepseek-flash] #lifetime is the minimum of the application keepalive, TCP keepalive, firewall/conntrack
2876e21d [deepseek-flash] #timeout, NAT timeout, Wi‑Fi power‑save behaviour, and power management. Change the host OS, and you
2876e21d [deepseek-flash] #change the defaults in that chain.
2876e21e [deepseek-flash] #
2876e21e [deepseek-flash] #Arch is minimal, rolling, and close to upstream. It generally does not ship the tuning
2876e21e [deepseek-flash] #files that Debian/Ubuntu/Fedora may add. So you get upstream kernel and package defaults. If your
2876e21e [deepseek-flash] #previous host had a config that sent keepalives, disabled Wi‑Fi powersave, or set a firewall
2876e21f [deepseek-flash] #timeout, Arch won’t have it unless you add it.
2876e21f [deepseek-flash] #
2876e21f [deepseek-flash] #The usual Arch‑specific suspects:
2876e21f [deepseek-flash] #
2876e220 [deepseek-flash] #1. sshd keepalives
2876e220 [deepseek-flash] #   Arch’s `sshd_config` is upstream: `ClientAliveInterval 0`, `TCPKeepAlive yes`. TCP keepalive on
2876e220 [deepseek-flash] #Linux defaults to `net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time = 7200` (2 hours). Many NATs drop idle TCP after
2876e220 [deepseek-flash] #5–60 minutes. So the connection is already dead from the middlebox’s point of view, but the host
2876e220 [deepseek-flash] #won’t notice for up to two hours. Other distros or cloud images often set `ClientAliveInterval`;
2876e221 [deepseek-flash] #Arch leaves it commented out.
2876e221 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e221 [deepseek-flash] #2. Wi‑Fi power save
2876e221 [deepseek-flash] #   NetworkManager’s default is `wifi.powersave=3` (enable). Arch doesn’t override it. Some distros
2876e222 [deepseek-flash] #ship a `/etc/NetworkManager/conf.d/` file to disable it. This is a very common cause of idle drops,
2876e222 [deepseek-flash] #especially on Intel Wi‑Fi.
2876e222 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e222 [deepseek-flash] #3. Firewall / conntrack
2876e223 [deepseek-flash] #   Arch has no firewall by default, but if you install one, its conntrack timeouts may be shorter
2876e223 [deepseek-flash] #than the kernel’s 5‑day TCP established timeout. Also, Arch’s `iptables` is nft‑based, which can
2876e223 [deepseek-flash] #behave differently from legacy iptables on older distros.
2876e223 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e223 [deepseek-flash] #4. systemd-logind
2876e224 [deepseek-flash] #   `IdleAction`, `KillUserProcesses`, and session timeouts can kill sessions. Arch’s systemd is
2876e224 [deepseek-flash] #newer, and defaults are usually safe, but if you enabled something it can terminate idle sessions.
2876e224 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e224 [deepseek-flash] #5. Power management
2876e225 [deepseek-flash] #   TLP, laptop‑mode, suspend on idle or lid close. Arch users often install TLP; other distros may
2876e225 [deepseek-flash] #not have it. Suspending the host kills connections.
2876e225 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e225 [deepseek-flash] #6. Newer kernel and drivers
2876e226 [deepseek-flash] #   Rolling release means new TCP stack, NIC firmware, and Wi‑Fi drivers. A regression or changed
2876e226 [deepseek-flash] #default (IPv6, MTU probing, etc.) can affect long‑lived connections. If it started after `pacman
2876e226 [deepseek-flash] #-Syu`, check `dmesg`.
2876e226 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e227 [deepseek-flash] #7. NetworkManager / dhcpcd / systemd-networkd
2876e227 [deepseek-flash] #   DHCP lease renewal or IPv6 RA lifetimes can cause address changes and drop connections.
2876e227 [deepseek-flash] #Different distros use different network stacks and defaults.
2876e227 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e227 [deepseek-flash] #Why other distros seem fine: they often ship `/usr/lib/sysctl.d/` or `/etc/NetworkManager/conf.d/`
2876e228 [deepseek-flash] #files that lower keepalive time, disable Wi‑Fi powersave, or set SSH keepalives. Arch leaves these
2876e228 [deepseek-flash] #to you. So the “lifetime” you saw before was likely a distro‑specific default, not a property of
2876e228 [deepseek-flash] #Linux itself.
2876e228 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e229 [deepseek-flash] #If you want to confirm which layer is timing out, look at:
2876e229 [deepseek-flash] #- `sysctl net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_intvl net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_probes`
2876e229 [deepseek-flash] #- `ss -o state established` to see the keepalive timer
2876e22a [deepseek-flash] #- `iw dev <iface> get power_save` and `nmcli -f 802-11-wireless.powersave connection show <conn>`
2876e22a [deepseek-flash] #- `nft list ruleset` and `conntrack -L`
2876e22a [deepseek-flash] #- `journalctl -u sshd -u NetworkManager -u systemd-logind -b`
2876e22a [deepseek-flash] #- `dmesg | grep -i -E 'iwlwifi|firmware|link is not ready'`
2876e22b [deepseek-flash] #
2876e22b [deepseek-flash] #That will show whether the host is sending keepalives, whether powersave is on, or whether
2876e22b [deepseek-flash] #conntrack is dropping. The fix is usually to align the keepalive at the layer that times out first
2876e22b [deepseek-flash] #— but the why is that Arch exposes the upstream timeout that your previous distro was
2876e22c [deepseek-flash] #silently overriding.
2876e22c [deepseek-flash] #
2876e927 [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.028 265.0KB 100.86s
2876e927 [deepseek-flash] #Broad, ambitious systems layer — ALSA, DRM/GBM/EGL, udev, V4L2, libxml2, hiredis, plus hand-rolled
2876e928 [deepseek-flash] #JSON, base64, WAV and Intel HEX. A few things stand out as genuinely new or different from the
2876e928 [deepseek-flash] #usual versions of this kind of code, and a few are real bugs rather than style choices.
2876e928 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e928 [deepseek-flash] #• Behavioural bugs*
2876e929 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e929 [deepseek-flash] #- `crypto.h`: the macOS and Linux paths produce different hashes for the same input.
2876e929 [deepseek-flash] #`ccSHA512HashBytes` ignores the `salt` argument entirely; `evpSHA512HashBytes` uses
2876e929 [deepseek-flash] #`EVP_sha3_512()` (not SHA‑512, despite the name) and appends the salt. The EVP path also rewrites
2876e92a [deepseek-flash] #`/` to `-`; the CommonCrypto path does not. Since these feed Redis keys (`BlobSalt`, `HandleSalt`,
2876e92a [deepseek-flash] #`ConnectSalt`), a blob written on one platform won't be found on the other.
2876e92a [deepseek-flash] #- `base64.h`: `Encode64` has `,` at index 63 while `Decode64` has `/` at index 63. Any sextet equal
2876e92a [deepseek-flash] #to 63 encodes to a character the decoder can't map back. The round‑trip check in `base64()` only
2876e92b [deepseek-flash] #catches it when that value occurs.
2876e92b [deepseek-flash] #- `alsaaudio.h`: `PCMCapabilities::pcmFormats` has an operator‑precedence bug — `a?1:0|b?2:0`
2876e92b [deepseek-flash] #parses as `a?1:((0|b)?2:0)`, so the format mask is never 3.
2876e92c [deepseek-flash] #- `synth.h`: `AudioBuffer::area()` declares `double total;` uninitialised before `total += ...`.
2876e92c [deepseek-flash] #- `events.h`: `EventSink::eventCounter` has no initialiser but is incremented in `post()`.
2876e92c [deepseek-flash] #- `connections.h`: `listPorts` passes an uninitialised `udev *udev` to `udev_enumerate_new`.
2876e92c [deepseek-flash] #- `bitmap.h`: the call site passes `rgb888(r,b,g)` while the macro is `rgb888(r,g,b)`, so 32‑bit
2876e92d [deepseek-flash] #colours have green and blue swapped.
2876e92d [deepseek-flash] #- `video.h`: `testVideo()` calls `init_device()` before `open_device()`, so the first
2876e92d [deepseek-flash] #`VIDIOC_QUERYCAP` runs on `fd == -1`.
2876e92d [deepseek-flash] #- `displaytest.cpp`: `endGraphics()` calls `eglTerminate` and destroys the GBM surface, then
2876e92e [deepseek-flash] #`destroy()` tries to use both. The order is inverted.
2876e92e [deepseek-flash] #- `redis.h`: `run()`, `runBlob()`, `runStrings()` and `graph()` all shadow the outer `int error`
2876e92e [deepseek-flash] #with a local `utf8 error` in the failure branch. `error=context->err` assigns an int to a
2876e92e [deepseek-flash] #`std::string` (as a char), and the function returns the outer `error`, still zero. Every Redis
2876e92f [deepseek-flash] #failure reports success.
2876e92f [deepseek-flash] #- `fs.h`: `RPCError(num)` prints the literal string `"num"`, not the value.
2876e92f [deepseek-flash] #- `eglgraphics.h`: `createBuffer(GLenum, GLint buffer)` takes `buffer` by value, so `buffer=bo`
2876e92f [deepseek-flash] #writes to a local copy. It only appears to work because `glBindBuffer` has a side effect.
2876e930 [deepseek-flash] #- `xml.h`: `Element::findAll` never advances `result`, so it collects matching children of `this`
2876e930 [deepseek-flash] #for every path component instead of descending. Only `find` descends.
2876e930 [deepseek-flash] #- `heximage.h`: `int32()` shifts a nibble into bit 28 of a signed `int`; a high nibble ≥ 8 is
2876e930 [deepseek-flash] #signed overflow.
2876e931 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e931 [deepseek-flash] #• Parser and input handling*
2876e931 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e931 [deepseek-flash] #- `json.h`: `parseJSON` calls `context.top()` in the `,`/`]`/`}` branch without checking
2876e932 [deepseek-flash] #`context.empty()`, so a leading `,` or `}` is undefined behaviour rather than a parse error.
2876e932 [deepseek-flash] #- `nitrohost.h`: `splitString` stops at the first empty token, so `"a  b"` yields only `"a"`.
2876e932 [deepseek-flash] #`trimString` only trims trailing whitespace despite the name.
2876e932 [deepseek-flash] #- `http.h`: `onRequest` does `line.substr(0, line.length()-1)` on every header line, assuming CRLF.
2876e933 [deepseek-flash] #A client sending bare LF loses the last character of the value. Keep‑alive is also only honoured if
2876e933 [deepseek-flash] #the client sends `Connection: keep-alive`; HTTP/1.1's default persistence isn't handled and
2876e933 [deepseek-flash] #responses don't send `Connection: close`.
2876e933 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e934 [deepseek-flash] #• Lifetime and resources*
2876e934 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e934 [deepseek-flash] #- `opensocket.h`: `Device::OpenDevice` starts a `std::thread` stored as a raw pointer and never
2876e934 [deepseek-flash] #joins or detaches it — deleting a `Device` will call `std::terminate`. `SocketConnection::onRead`
2876e935 [deepseek-flash] #is empty and `writeBytes` returns 0, so socket data is discarded and writes are dropped silently.
2876e935 [deepseek-flash] #- `synth.h`: `SynthStream` `new`s `Voice`, `Oscillator` and `ADSR` and never frees them. `json.h`'s
2876e935 [deepseek-flash] #`JSObject`/`JSArray` likewise don't delete their `values`.
2876e935 [deepseek-flash] #- `headless.h`: `Process::makeargv` uses function‑static `args`/`argv`. A second `Process::init`
2876e936 [deepseek-flash] #frees the first process's argument block, and the `argv` stored as a member then dangles.
2876e936 [deepseek-flash] #`Process::start` uses `vfork()` and then touches `std::cout` in the child on `execvp` failure.
2876e936 [deepseek-flash] #- `drmdisplay.h`: every `drmModeConnector` leaks (never `drmModeFreeConnector`), and `deviceInfo`
2876e936 [deepseek-flash] #dereferences `drmModeGetEncoder`/`GetCrtc`/`GetFB` without null checks.
2876e937 [deepseek-flash] #- `eglgraphics.h` `loadGLSL` allocates `chars` and never frees it; `bluetooth.h` `query()` never
2876e937 [deepseek-flash] #frees `response_list`.
2876e937 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e938 [deepseek-flash] #• Timing*
2876e938 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e938 [deepseek-flash] #- `clocks.h`: `getPicos`/`getNanos` compute with double literals. `1e12 * tv_sec` exceeds 2^53 for
2876e938 [deepseek-flash] #any `CLOCK_REALTIME` value, so picosecond timestamps lose exactness, and `NitroSystem::TickStamp`
2876e939 [deepseek-flash] #uses `getNanos(CLOCK_REALTIME)`. Integer literals and `int64_t` would avoid it.
2876e939 [deepseek-flash] #- `duration()` pads only the hours field; minutes and seconds are unpadded and the fractional
2876e939 [deepseek-flash] #second is truncated before splitting.
2876e939 [deepseek-flash] #
2876e93a [deepseek-flash] #• Build*
2876e93a [deepseek-flash] #
2876e93a [deepseek-flash] #- `CMakeLists.txt` appends `-w` after `-Wall`, suppressing everything. `project(displaytest CXX)`
2876e93a [deepseek-flash] #is declared mid‑file after `project(fit3 C CXX)`, and `link_directories` points at an absolute
2876e93b [deepseek-flash] #`/home/ec2-user/...` path.
2876e93b [deepseek-flash] #
2876e93b [deepseek-flash] #• Smaller*
2876e93b [deepseek-flash] #
2876e93c [deepseek-flash] #- `recordPCM` sets `SND_PCM_FORMAT_S16_BE` under a `//LE` comment. The last argument to
2876e93c [deepseek-flash] #`snd_pcm_hw_params_set_rate` is a rounding hint (0 = exact), not a stream selector —
2876e93c [deepseek-flash] #`SND_PCM_STREAM_CAPTURE` happens to mean "round up".
2876e93c [deepseek-flash] #- `bitmap.h`: `sgn(x)` doesn't parenthesise `x`, and `rgb565`/`rgb888` end with a semicolon, which
2876e93d [deepseek-flash] #is hazardous in `if/else`.
2876e93d [deepseek-flash] #- `redis.h` hard-codes `AUTH valkey2026`.
2876e93d [deepseek-flash] #- `synth.h`: `Triangle::sample` returns `fabs(fmod(delta,2.0))-1`, which is a sawtooth, not a
2876e93d [deepseek-flash] #triangle.
2876e93e [deepseek-flash] #
2876e93e [deepseek-flash] #The cross-platform hash divergence and the Redis error-swallowing are probably the two worth fixing
2876e93e [deepseek-flash] #first, since they change observable behaviour rather than just correctness on edge cases.
2876e93e [deepseek-flash] #
28770ab8 [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.031 273.0KB 94.38s
28770ab8 [deepseek-flash] #The `SIGILL` is not being raised by the store at line 120. A `mov dword ptr [this+off], -1` cannot
28770ab8 [deepseek-flash] #generate `SIGILL` — the signal comes from either a `ud2` trap instruction emitted by the compiler,
28770ab8 [deepseek-flash] #or from the instruction pointer having been redirected into the middle of some instruction stream.
28770ab9 [deepseek-flash] #The `-g` line number is the nearest source line the compiler could map the address to, not
28770ab9 [deepseek-flash] #necessarily the instruction you think you're executing.
28770ab9 [deepseek-flash] #
28770ab9 [deepseek-flash] #Get the raw instruction first:
28770aba [deepseek-flash] #
28770aba [deepseek-flash] #
28770aba [deepseek-flash] #(gdb) disassemble /m HttpConnection::closeHandle
28770aba [deepseek-flash] #(gdb) x/8xb $pc
28770abb [deepseek-flash] #(gdb) info registers rip rsp rbp
28770abb [deepseek-flash] #(gdb) p $_siginfo
28770abb [deepseek-flash] #(gdb) bt full
28770abb [deepseek-flash] #(gdb) frame 1
28770abc [deepseek-flash] #(gdb) info locals
28770abc [deepseek-flash] #(gdb) p connection->fd
28770abc [deepseek-flash] #(gdb) x/64cb this->readBuffer
28770abc [deepseek-flash] #
28770abd [deepseek-flash] #
28770abd [deepseek-flash] #If `x/8xb $pc` shows `0f 0b`, it is `ud2` — a compiler-emitted trap. If it shows something like `c7
28770abd [deepseek-flash] #47 xx ff ff ff ff`, then the store is real and the debug info is fine, and the IP has been
28770abd [deepseek-flash] #corrupted (usually by stack overwrite) so that GDB is attributing a frame from a bad return
28770abd [deepseek-flash] #address.
28770abd [deepseek-flash] #
28770abe [deepseek-flash] #Most likely cause on Arch: `_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS`. Arch's `makepkg.conf` sets
28770abe [deepseek-flash] #`-Wp,-D_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS` in `CXXFLAGS`. CMake picks that up from the environment. That turns
28770abe [deepseek-flash] #every `std::string::operator[]`, `std::vector::operator[]`, `std::string_view::operator[]` and
28770abe [deepseek-flash] #similar into a bounds-checked access; on failure libstdc++ calls `std::__glibcxx_assert_fail`,
28770abe [deepseek-flash] #which in recent GCC is `__builtin_trap()` → `ud2` → `SIGILL`. The trap instruction lives inside
28770abe [deepseek-flash] #inlined libstdc++ code, and `-g` maps it back to the closest user line, which is why it lands on
28770abe [deepseek-flash] #`fd=-1` even though nothing on that line is bounds-checked.
28770abe [deepseek-flash] #
28770abe [deepseek-flash] #Concrete bugs in the code that could be the underlying cause:
28770abf [deepseek-flash] #
28770abf [deepseek-flash] #• `onRequest` Content-Length overflow.* `contentLength` is read from the header with no
28770abf [deepseek-flash] #upper bound and used directly as the write length into `readBuffer`:
28770abf [deepseek-flash] #
28770abf [deepseek-flash] #
28770ac0 [deepseek-flash] #size_t contentLength=longValue(header["Content-Length"]);
28770ac0 [deepseek-flash] #...
28770ac0 [deepseek-flash] #while(n<contentLength){
28770ac1 [deepseek-flash] #    ssize_t nn=recv(fd,readBuffer+start+n,contentLength-n,0);
28770ac1 [deepseek-flash] #    ...
28770ac1 [deepseek-flash] #}
28770ac1 [deepseek-flash] #content=utf8(readBuffer+start,contentLength);
28770ac2 [deepseek-flash] #
28770ac2 [deepseek-flash] #
28770ac2 [deepseek-flash] #`start + contentLength` can exceed `MaxRequest` (240000). `readBuffer` is the last member of
28770ac2 [deepseek-flash] #`HttpConnection`, so this writes past the end of the heap block and into whatever is adjacent. A
28770ac3 [deepseek-flash] #request with `Content-Length: 1000000` is enough. Guard with `if (start + contentLength >
28770ac3 [deepseek-flash] #MaxRequest) { ... reject ... }` before the loop.
28770ac3 [deepseek-flash] #
28770ac3 [deepseek-flash] #• `filepath.substr(r)` with `r == npos`.* In the GET handler:
28770ac3 [deepseek-flash] #
28770ac3 [deepseek-flash] #
28770ac3 [deepseek-flash] #size_t r=filepath.rfind('.');
28770ac3 [deepseek-flash] #utf8 suffix=filepath.substr(r);
28770ac4 [deepseek-flash] #
28770ac4 [deepseek-flash] #
28770ac4 [deepseek-flash] #If the path has no `.`, `substr(npos)` throws `std::out_of_range`. Uncaught, that is
28770ac4 [deepseek-flash] #`std::terminate` → `abort` (SIGABRT), not SIGILL, but it is still a crash waiting for the first
28770ac5 [deepseek-flash] #extensionless path.
28770ac5 [deepseek-flash] #
28770ac5 [deepseek-flash] #• `closeHandle` has no return statement.* `int closeHandle()` with no `return` is UB. `-w`
28770ac5 [deepseek-flash] #is hiding the warning. At `-O0` the caller reads garbage from `eax`; harmless here because the
28770ac5 [deepseek-flash] #caller discards it, but it is the kind of thing you want the compiler to tell you about.
28770ac5 [deepseek-flash] #
28770ac5 [deepseek-flash] #• `-Wall -w` in `CMakeLists.txt`. Those cancel each other out. Drop `-w`; you are
28770ac5 [deepseek-flash] #currently building with every diagnostic disabled, including the ones that would point at
28770ac6 [deepseek-flash] #uninitialised members (`EventSink::eventCounter`, `AudioBuffer::total`, `udev udev` in
28770ac6 [deepseek-flash] #`listPorts`) and at the missing return.
28770ac6 [deepseek-flash] #
28770ac6 [deepseek-flash] #• Thread join.* `hostIPv6` calls `pthread_create` with default attributes and never joins.
28770ac6 [deepseek-flash] #Each accepted connection leaks a thread stack in addition to the 240KB `HttpConnection`. Not the
28770ac6 [deepseek-flash] #SIGILL, but it will accumulate.
28770ac7 [deepseek-flash] #
28770ac7 [deepseek-flash] #Fastest path to the real bug: recompile with `-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer`
28770ac7 [deepseek-flash] #and run the same request. ASAN will point at the exact line of the overflow (probably the `recv`
28770ac7 [deepseek-flash] #into `readBuffer`), and UBSAN will catch the uninitialised reads. That is usually quicker than
28770ac8 [deepseek-flash] #decoding a misattributed `ud2` from a `-O0` build.
28770ac8 [deepseek-flash] #
2877742a [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.012 295.1KB 34.70s
2877742a [deepseek-flash] #Three things changed relative to the version you showed earlier, and one of them is in the right
2877742a [deepseek-flash] #direction for the crash.
2877742b [deepseek-flash] #
2877742b [deepseek-flash] #• The Content-Length guard.* Moving the length check up to `start + contentLength <
2877742b [deepseek-flash] #MaxRequest` closes the unbounded write into `readBuffer` — that was the most plausible
2877742c [deepseek-flash] #heap-corruption vector feeding the `ud2` trap. Good change.
2877742c [deepseek-flash] #
2877742c [deepseek-flash] #But the guard has a hole because `start` is now hoisted out of the `if (contentLength)` block.
2877742c [deepseek-flash] #`ss.tellg()` returns `pos_type(-1)` if the stream has failed, and the implicit conversion to
2877742d [deepseek-flash] #`size_t` turns that into `SIZE_MAX`. The header loop reaches that state whenever the request never
2877742d [deepseek-flash] #supplies a blank line: `getline` hits EOF, sets `failbit`, `line` is empty, the loop breaks on
2877742d [deepseek-flash] #`length()==0`, and then `tellg` returns -1. With `start == SIZE_MAX`, `start + contentLength` wraps
2877742d [deepseek-flash] #around in unsigned arithmetic — a `Content-Length` of 100 gives 99, which passes the `< MaxRequest`
2877742e [deepseek-flash] #test. What follows is `n -= start` wrapping upward and `utf8(readBuffer+start, contentLength)`
2877742e [deepseek-flash] #reading one byte before the buffer. Smaller than the original overflow, still undefined.
2877742e [deepseek-flash] #
2877742e [deepseek-flash] #The fix is to validate the stream before trusting the offset — check the stream state after the
2877742f [deepseek-flash] #header loop, or capture the position into a signed type and reject negative. `if (!ss) return
2877742f [deepseek-flash] #false;` immediately after the loop would do it.
2877742f [deepseek-flash] #
2877742f [deepseek-flash] #• One of the two `substr(npos)` sites was fixed.* The 404 branch now guards `r ==
28777430 [deepseek-flash] #std::string::npos`. The second one, in the `loadString` success path, is unchanged: `size_t
28777430 [deepseek-flash] #r=filepath.rfind('.'); utf8 suffix=filepath.substr(r);`. If a request resolves to an existing file
28777430 [deepseek-flash] #with no dot in the name, that throws `std::out_of_range`, which is uncaught and terminates. Same
28777430 [deepseek-flash] #one-line guard applies.
28777431 [deepseek-flash] #
28777431 [deepseek-flash] #• `closeHandle()` still has no `return`. `int closeHandle()` falls off the end. At
28777431 [deepseek-flash] #`-O0` the caller reads whatever is in `eax`; harmless here only because the return value is
28777431 [deepseek-flash] #discarded. `-w` in `CMakeLists.txt` is suppressing the warning that would flag it, along with the
28777432 [deepseek-flash] #uninitialised members I mentioned before (`EventSink::eventCounter`, `AudioBuffer::total`, the
28777432 [deepseek-flash] #`udev udev` in `listPorts`).
28777432 [deepseek-flash] #
28777432 [deepseek-flash] #• The `httpTask` reorder* — moving `closeHandle()` inside the mutex block — doesn't change
28777433 [deepseek-flash] #correctness either way. It just holds the lock across a `close(2)`. The shutdown path in
28777433 [deepseek-flash] #`closeHttpTasks` still works because closing a blocked thread's fd makes its `recv` return and it
28777433 [deepseek-flash] #then queues on the same mutex.
28777433 [deepseek-flash] #
28777434 [deepseek-flash] #Also worth noting: when the new guard rejects an oversize body, control falls through to
28777434 [deepseek-flash] #`if(content.length()!=contentLength) return false;`, which logs "content length does not match" and
28777434 [deepseek-flash] #drops the connection without a response. A 413 would be more accurate, and would stop the client
28777434 [deepseek-flash] #hanging until the receive timeout.
28777435 [deepseek-flash] #
28777435 [deepseek-flash] #If the SIGILL survives this, the next step is still the raw bytes at `pc` — `x/8xb pc` in the
28777435 [deepseek-flash] #frame. `0f 0b` confirms a compiler or libstdc++ trap; anything else means the instruction pointer
28777435 [deepseek-flash] #is misattributed and the real fault is a corrupted return address on the stack, which is a
28777436 [deepseek-flash] #different investigation.
28777436 [deepseek-flash] #
