29326345 [deepseek-flash] #Worth checking one thing while it runs: with no trailing slash on the source, rsync treats
29326345 [deepseek-flash] #`/mnt/fast/master` as a directory to place inside the destination.
29326345 [deepseek-flash] #
29326346 [deepseek-flash] #- If `/mnt/grid26/master` does not exist → it creates it and the contents land directly in
29326346 [deepseek-flash] #`/mnt/grid26/master/...`. Fine.
29326346 [deepseek-flash] #- If `/mnt/grid26/master` already exists → you get `/mnt/grid26/master/master/...` (a
29326346 [deepseek-flash] #nested copy).
29326347 [deepseek-flash] #
29326347 [deepseek-flash] #You can confirm which happened with:
29326347 [deepseek-flash] #
29326347 [deepseek-flash] #
29326348 [deepseek-flash] #ls -la /mnt/grid26/master/
29326348 [deepseek-flash] #
29326348 [deepseek-flash] #
29326348 [deepseek-flash] #If you see a `master` directory inside it, that's the nesting case. To get the contents copied
29326349 [deepseek-flash] #directly instead, the source needs a trailing slash:
29326349 [deepseek-flash] #
29326349 [deepseek-flash] #
29326349 [deepseek-flash] #rsync -rua /mnt/fast/master/ /mnt/grid26/master/
2932634a [deepseek-flash] #
2932634a [deepseek-flash] #
2932634a [deepseek-flash] #Also note `-u` skips files that are newer on the destination, which is what you asked for, but it
2932634a [deepseek-flash] #won't delete anything and won't overwrite a newer destination file. If the two trees are meant to
2932634b [deepseek-flash] #stay identical, `-a` plus `--delete` (and dropping `-u`) is the usual choice — but that's
2932634b [deepseek-flash] #destructive, so only if that's actually your intent.
2932634b [deepseek-flash] #
